\par\par \b \uc2 Credit Markets: BOJ Props Up Domestic Bonds -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Suryatapa Bhattacharya \par \uc2 946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2017\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (July 8, 2017).\par \par \uc2 TOKYO -- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to tame a rise in government-bond yields on Friday, signaling its determination to stick to its current policy mix, even as the recent selloff in global bond markets intensifies.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets came after the yield on the country's benchmark 10-year government bond rose earlier in the day to 0.105%, its highest since Feb. 3. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target is 0%. Bond yields rise as their prices fall.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year government bond later slipped back to 0.085%, after the BOJ said it would buy an unlimited amount of government bonds maturing in five to 10 years at a yield equivalent to 0.110% and raised the amount the amount of bonds it said it would buy at a regularly scheduled auction.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's display of determination to prop up domestic bond prices came after the yield on benchmark \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds, known as bunds, rose overnight to its highest level in 18 months, 0.57%, and \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields continued their recent climb. The bond selloff spilled into Asia on Friday, with yields on benchmark 10-year government bonds rising in Korea, Australia and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 The trigger for government-bond market declines has been talk among leading central bankers suggesting that years of easy monetary policy designed to stimulate struggling economies may be coming to an end. While the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is considering further interest-rate increases this year, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been signaling that it is getting ready to wind down its stimulus efforts after years of huge bond buying.\par \par \uc2 The situation in Japan is still somewhat different. While the U.S. has been recording solid growth and European officials appear ever more confident that economic recovery there is taking root, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained cautious. That is despite some encouraging signs: Japanese base wages grew at their fastest pace in more than 17 years in May, rising by 0.9% on-year.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policy makers have been yearning for evidence of better pay growth, which they hope will stimulate higher consumer demand. But the recent uptick likely isn't enough to push Japanese inflation up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.0% target. Consumer-price inflation rose at a 0.4% annual rate in May.\par \par \uc2 If the BOJ keeps to its inflation target, the market "cannot expect it to exit its stimulus program anytime soon," said Shuichi Ohsaki, rates strategist at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said little about giving up on the program of huge bond buying it introduced four years ago. The BOJ's official target is to buy 80 trillion yen ($703 billion) in government bonds each year. It already owns 40% of the country's outstanding government bonds.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not thinking of deciding in advance how we would change the pace of bond purchases," BOJ \uc2 Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  said last month.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its bond buying in recent months, leading some analysts to say it is "technically tapering." At the current rate of purchases, it may only buy around 55 trillion yen's worth this year, according to \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  estimates. The reduction had led some observers to suggest the BOJ might soon raise its target yield for 10-year government bonds to as high as 0.2%, meaning that it would be able to reduce its bond purchases further.\par \par \uc2 Yet the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's decision on Friday to buy unlimited amounts of government bonds to keep yields from rising too far suggests analysts may have been getting ahead of themselves.\par \par \uc2 "They sent a clear message that they are still going to stick to this range target of 0.1% and they will do whatever they can do to keep it," said Naka Matsuzawa, Japan rates strategist with \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If anything, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's determination to keep a lid on bond yields appears to have hardened. In February, it took similar steps to increase its bond buying but only when the yield on the 10-year tenor had risen as high as 0.150%.\par \par \uc2 At or near the top of the list of complicating factors for the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is the yen. Japanese policy makers have long been eager to prevent the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from becoming too strong against the dollar and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing the harm that might do to Japan's exporting giants.\par \par \uc2 After the BOJ's actions Friday, the greenback gained 0.5% against the yen, meaning one dollar now buys 113.80 yen.\par \par \uc2 Still, policy makers may be reluctant to let the yen slide indefinitely. A much weaker yen could weigh on importers, eroding consumer spending power for products such as gasoline and clothing.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say that by keeping yields around zero, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks driving bond prices higher than they otherwise would be. In turn, that could lead to losses for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it looks to exit its stimulus program.\par \par \uc2 "This makes them vulnerable to criticism about, 'Why are you doing this?' and 'It is costing the nation,'" Nomura's Mr. Matsuzawa said.\par \par \uc2 Saumya Vaishampayan and Yoko Kubota contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 08, 2017 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170708ed780007f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
