\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE--Japan reappoints  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  tsar Asakawa, seeks consistency dealing with U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2017\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Cuts extraneous words from headline)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 4 (Reuters) - Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat has been appointed for another one-year term in a regular finance ministry reshuffle, underscoring a desire to maintain policy consistency as Tokyo faces potentially tough economic talks with the United States.\par \par \uc2 Masatsugu Asakawa, a 59-year-old policy veteran with policymaker contacts inside and outside of Japan, played a key role in setting up Japan's bilateral economic dialogue with the United States, which kicked off in April.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that Asakawa will serve another year as vice finance minister for international affairs, a position he has held since July 2015.\par \par \uc2 The role oversees Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and international diplomacy and includes representing Tokyo in \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  (G7) and \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  (G20) meetings.\par \par \uc2 Asakawa's reappointment will help Tokyo negotiate with consistency in the second round of the bilateral economic dialogue with the United States, which is expected to be held later this year, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  campaigned for office on an "America First" platform, saying he would boost manufacturing jobs and shrink the country's trade deficit with the likes of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Not many officials have held the post for more than two years. Those that have done so include \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , the governor of the Bank of Japan since 2013.\par \par \uc2 Asakawa is close to Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2 , having served as his executive secretary when Aso was prime minister in 2008-2009, and as his executive assistant when he became finance minister in 2012.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market under Asakawa's stewardship, though he has frequently jawboned markets to stem yen spikes that risk hurting the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts see Asakawa as a dark-horse candidate to replace Kuroda at the BOJ when the incumbent's five-year term ends in April next year. (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Takaya Yamaguchi; Editing by Neil Fullick) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-7-4T09:01:06.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170704ed7400anh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
