\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Japan should push back on any US attack on yen policy-Abe adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2017\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Cars, agri could be among topics in U.S.-Japan econ dialogue\par \par \uc2 * BOJ should raise yield target when economy gets over-stimulated\par \par \uc2 * Current yen rate vs dollar not extremely strong (Adds more quotes, context on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and economy)\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko and Sumio Ito\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 7 (Reuters) - Japan should push back against any U.S. suggestion that it is suppressing the yen's value for trade advantage, an adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said, in a bid to preempt criticism of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Koichi Hamada, Cabinet adviser and emeritus professor of economics at Yale University, told Reuters in an interview that Tokyo should stress that Japan has a different \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from China.\par \par \uc2 With President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  criticising the trade policies of Japan, China and other major economies, Tokyo fears that trade friction could return for the first time in years, harming Japan's interests and its deep relations with Washington.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. official told Reuters that the administration is shifting its attention from countries that "manipulate" their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are "misaligned," even if the imbalance is unintentional.\par \par \uc2 "What Japan should argue is that Japan and China have totally different stances towards \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation," Hamada said on Thursday. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market under Abenomics, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy is targeted strictly at domestic economic targets."\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has not entered the market to sell yen for dollars since November 2011. However, the weak yen has been one of the most prominent outcomes of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's "Abenomics" stimulus policies, helping to boost exporters' profits and lift Japanese stocks sharply higher.\par \par \uc2 Inflation, however, is still anaemic and is well behind the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's 2 percent target.\par \par \uc2 "The current level of yen is not extremely strong against the dollar for Japanese businesses. The BOJ does not need to ease further," Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading around 110.50 yen when Hamada spoke. It stood around 110.60 yen in volatile market on Friday in Asia after news of U.S. missile strikes on Syria.\par \par \uc2 Since Trump's election victory in November, the dollar has rallied to hit above 118.60 yen on expectations he will deliver stimulus-enhancing policies, but has since retreated as the new U.S. administration faced early hurdles in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Hamada said sensitive trade issues such as exchange rates, cars and agriculture, will likely come up in high-level bilateral economic talks set to start this month. But he portrayed this as a way for the two governments to seek mutually beneficial agreements after the failure of an Asia-Pacific trade deal.\par \par \uc2 "It is natural to talk about these issues in the dialogue to seek criteria for win-win situations for both countries bilaterally," after Trump pulled America out of an ambitious Pacific trade agreement, he said.\par \par \uc2 BOJ\par \par \uc2 Hamada echoed BOJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  who recently said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't raise its long-term bond yield targets simply because overseas long-interest rates rise.\par \par \uc2 He said that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should manage monetary policy solely to influence its domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ does not need to raise its 10-year Japanese Government Bond yield target just because yields in the U.S. increase," he said.\par \par \uc2 "But if the BOJ keeps the long-term bond yield low regardless of the high U.S. yield, then it will naturally accelerate the yen's falls, and cause mild inflationary pressure in Japan."\par \par \uc2 The BOJ pledges to keep the 10-year JGB yield around zero percent but analysts predict the BOJ's next move would be to start scaling back its ultra-easy policy and they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise the yield target.\par \par \uc2 "If the Japanese economy gets over-stimulated, the BOJ will have to raise its 10-year JGB yield target to calm down the economy," Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 He also reiterated the BOJ does not have to stick to its 2 percent inflation target in an environment where the job market is tight and the economy is on a recovery trend.\par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, as long as employment is full and production is in favourable condition, the price inflation is hardly any longer the fundamental target. The price target is, in my opinion, a merely secondary target." (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko, Sumio Ito, Additional reporting by Chris Gallagher; Editing by William Mallard & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-4-7T10:01:30.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170407ed4700ckx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
