\par\par \b \uc2 *Japan Finance Minister: Told G-20 That Excessive  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Volatility Bad for Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 23:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 17 Mar 2017 18:04 ET *Japan Finance Minister: Told G-20 Important to Reaffirm Previous \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Views\par \par \uc2 17 Mar 2017 18:06 ET *Senior Japan MOF Official Thinks G-20 Supports Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Views\par \par \uc2 17 Mar 2017 18:08 ET *Japan Finance Minister: Explained to G-20 Tokyo's Stance to Continue Bold Efforts to End Deflation\par \par \uc2 17 Mar 2017 18:12 ET *Japan Finance Minister: Trade Must Be Conducted According to Free, Fair Rules\par \par \uc2 17 Mar 2017 18:12 ET *Senior Japan MOF Official: No Nation Accused Any Nation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manipulation During G-20 Talks\par \par \uc2 17 Mar 2017 19:25 ET  Japan Presses G-20 to Disapprove of \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Volatility Once Again\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BADEN-BADEN, Germany--Japan on Friday pressed the world's leading economies to disapprove of exchange-rate volatility, seeking to leave itself a leeway to act in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if the yen reverses course and rises sharply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Speaking from the standpoint that we ought to absolutely ensure stability in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, I said it was important to reaffirm the previous commitment on monetary and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy" during the first day of a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  gathering of finance officials, Finance Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2  said at a news conference.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Aso said the commitment to which he referred to includes the group's regular denouncement of excess volatility and disorderly movement in the exchange rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The "atmosphere" in the meeting room during Mr. Aso's speech suggested that other member countries were sympathetic with his comments, although there was no follow-up discussion on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issue, a senior \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japanese finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  official said. The official added that no member country accused any other of manipulating the exchange rate for trade advantage during the debate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G-20 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors will wrap up their meeting Saturday by issuing a communique to explain their agreement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials came to the spa resort of Baden-Baden hoping to rally the G-20 into reiterating its message against sharp \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements in the joint statement. Tokyo thinks that such a message could help discourage speculators from trying to reverse the yen's downtrend that started late last year, which has benefited Japan's exporters. It would also offer legitimacy to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's view that authorities should be allowed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in extreme circumstances, according to Japanese officials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to a G-20 official, statement writers have decided to preserve language denouncing excess volatility and disorderly movement in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The decision followed a direct push by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Germany to keep the passage in the communique, according to people familiar with negotiations. Germany---this year's G-20 chair---initially sought to remove it to simplify the statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Aso added that he called during the G-20 meeting for "free and fair" rules on international trade, aligning himself with Washington in pushing to soften a language on protectionism in the G-20 statement. Mr. Aso also reiterated Tokyo's plan to continue with "bold efforts" to end deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 17, 2017 19:25 ET (23:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170317ed3h00410\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
