\par\par \b \uc2 As Trump and Abe Meet, Investors Prepare  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Bets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2017\par 12:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--While pundits assess the state of U.S.-Japan ties when President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  meet later this week, investors will be watching for clues over another key relationship--that between the dollar and the yen.\par \par \uc2 After Mr. Trump's election win in November, the greenback surged to a 10-month high against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as bets on higher U.S. growth and inflation mounted. This year, though, the yen has risen 4.1% against the dollar as the post-U. S. election market euphoria fades.\par \par \uc2 Guessing what happens next isn't simple. Big questions loom over Mr. Trump's precise economic plans and the sustainability of Japan's latest move into unorthodox central banking.\par \par \uc2 That's against the backdrop of tension that has seen the leaders of the two countries recently exchange barbs over whether Japan has been weakening the yen to gain a trade advantage. Mr. Trump last week alleged the U.S. had sat back "like a bunch of dummies" as both Japan and China devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The jitters are clear in the pattern of market bets on the yen. Hedge funds and other leveraged funds started betting heavily on a yen decline in aggregate after the U.S. election, a reversal from much of 2016, according to \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Since the number of net yen-bearish futures and options contracts peaked on Jan. 3, leveraged funds have slashed those bets by nearly half.\par \par \uc2 "In the near term, political risk is the biggest thing" for the exchange rate, said Maoko Ishikawa, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The Trump-Abe meeting comes as concern grows that major countries may be drawn into competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations. On Thursday, New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Graeme Wheeler\uc2  said a decline in his country's exchange rate was needed to keep its growth balanced.\par \par \uc2 The current climate resembles a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "cold war," Joachim Fels, global economic adviser at fund manager Pimco said in a note this week, arguing that actions taken by the European, Japanese and Chinese central banks in the last half of 2016 contributed to the depreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For Japan and the U.S., the situation is complicated by some contradictions between their words and actions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe and other Japanese officials say their priority is stoking domestic inflation higher. But analysts say it is getting hard to argue that their policies don't look like \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key policies is to tether the yield on the 10-year Japanese government bond near zero. When the yield goes too high, that means it must step in to buy government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Keeping the 10-year JGB yield low is becoming more difficult with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to raise interest rates multiple times this year. As U.S. rates rise, investors seeking better returns may sell Japanese bonds and buy Treasurys, driving yields on JGBs higher and forcing Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy bonds to maintain its yield target.\par \par \uc2 That, in turn, means the difference in yields between Treasurys and JGBs could widen, putting downward pressure on the yen.\par \par \uc2 That scenario played out on Feb. 3, when the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to boost its bond purchases by more than $6 billion after the 10-year yield spiked to a year-high of 0.150%. Its move weakened the yen, albeit temporarily.\par \par \uc2 "From the outside, it looks like they're manipulating the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 's Ms. Ishikawa.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now watching for signs of further U.S. pressure on Japan over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "What the market is questioning is not the ability of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to contain long-term rates, but its willingness or commitment to keep long-term rates close to zero if this becomes a political issue," said Hiroshi Shiraishi, senior economist at BNP Paribas in Japan.\par \par \uc2 There are conflicting forces for the U.S. too. Recent comments from Mr. Trump and other members of his administration suggest they don't want the dollar to get too strong.\par \par \uc2 Yet economists say Mr. Trump's plans for corporate tax cuts and higher fiscal spending could drive U.S. inflation higher, forcing the Fed to raise interest rates more quickly. In turn, that would put upward pressure on the dollar against all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's attitude to trade is another key factor. Talk of a new border tax on imports is dollar-positive, for example, as it would likely crimp imports by U.S. companies, thus curbing their demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adding to the confusion is a lack of clarity over the timing and extent of any trade measures by the Trump administration--and how other countries will respond.\par \par \uc2 Pimco's Mr. Fels says the rational response for Japan and other major countries now is to try to allow further appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the hope of persuading the U.S. not to resort to protectionism.\par \par \uc2 "Whether this is enough to prevent the administration in Washington from pushing the nuclear tariff button remains to be seen," he said. "Time and tweets will tell."\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 09, 2017 06:17 ET (11:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170209ed29001ff\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
