\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Trump's view shouldn't be hindrance to BOJ easing - ex-IMF exec\b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2017\par 09:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * BOJ should be allowed to ease if needed - Shinohara\par \par \uc2 * Former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not work much\par \par \uc2 * Says BOJ easing is to end deflation, not devalue \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 1 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  should expand monetary stimulus if necessary and should not consider U.S. President Donald Trump's accusation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation as an obstacle to doing so, a former \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  executive said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not have much ammunition left to fight any abrupt rise in the yen, after more than three years of huge money printing, said Naoyuki Shinohara, an IMF deputy chief from 2010 to 2015.\par \par \uc2 But Trump's criticism over Japan's monetary policy should not act as a hindrance if the BOJ sees the need to expand stimulus to support the economy, he told Reuters on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "When necessary, the BOJ should take action," said Shinohara, formerly Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat.\par \par \uc2 The weak yen is among the few successful channels in which the BOJ's stimulus helped the economy, though consumption and investment still lack momentum, said Shinohara, currently a University of Tokyo professor.\par \par \uc2 "That's why the BOJ's policy may be interpreted as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation. But the BOJ's policy is clearly aimed at beating deflation and the yen's weakening is just a side-effect," he said. "It would therefore be puzzling for anyone to say the BOJ should stop easing."\par \par \uc2 Asked whether yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become an option if the yen keeps rising in the wake of Trump's remark, Shinohara said: "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't be too effective."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective in addressing short-term, abrupt moves in exchange rates, which is not what is happening now, he added.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was on the defensive after Trump and trade adviser Peter Navarro criticised China, Germany and Japan, saying they were devaluing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the disadvantage of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers hit back at Trump's accusation, stressing that Tokyo was abiding by a \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  agreement to refrain from competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo considers a strong yen as negative for Japan's economy as it hurts exports, and worries that a reversal of the recent weak-yen trend could weigh on a fragile recovery.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since November 2011. However, the weak yen has been considered as one of the few successes of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's "Abenomics" stimulus policies aimed at pulling the economy out of deflation. (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-2-4T08:28:49.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170201ed2100eqn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
