\par\par \b \uc2 Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Export Volume Rises at Fastest Pace in Nearly Two Years\b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2016\par 01:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO\u8212\'20\'14Japan\u8217\'20\'19s export volume rose at the fastest pace in nearly two years in November, boosted by demand from China, signaling that the world\u8217\'20\'19s third-largest economy is on a steady growth track.\par \par \uc2 After suffering two years of choppy expansion that undercut Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ambitions to revive the economy, figures this year suggest Japan is on a more solid footing. The economy expanded modestly in each of the first three quarters of 2016, and economists expect something similar in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 Exports, which traditionally drive Japan\u8217\'20\'19s industrial growth, have turned from a drag to a tailwind recently, thanks to factors such as growing demand for Japanese parts from Chinese smartphone makers.\par \par \uc2 Japan\u8217\'20\'19s exports to China increased 4.4% from a year earlier to \u165\'00\'A51.1 trillion ($9.3 billion) during November, the first rise in value terms since February. Semiconductor maker \uc2 Toshiba Corp\uc2 .\u8217\'20\'19s stock price has jumped 38% since the beginning of October.\par \par \uc2 Overall, export volume rose 7.4% in November from a year earlier, driven by demand for semiconductors, ships and car parts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The figures add to the likelihood that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will raise its assessment of Japan\u8217\'20\'19s economy, including export demand, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  concludes its regular two-day policy meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 SMBC Nikko Securities\uc2  economist Junichi Makino said there was even a small chance that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  would tighten monetary policy by raising its target for the interest rate on 10-year government bonds. The target currently stands at zero.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Makino said he still expected the BOJ to stand pat, and other economists dismissed the chance of a rate increase, the discussion shows how the debate has shifted. Until recently, each BOJ meeting was preceded by speculation of possible new easing steps to jolt the lackluster economy.\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the U.S. presidential election, the yen has fallen sharply and stock prices have moved to a high for the year. A weaker yen makes Japanese exporters more competitive and makes the country a more attractive destination for tourists, which bodes well for the economy in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Still, economists said Japan was vulnerable to any unexpected shifts in Mr. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s policies. He has criticized China for what he described as that nation\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and might take a dim view of the yen\u8217\'20\'19s recent fall, said another SMBC Nikko Securities economist, Yoshimasa Maruyama.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA further appreciation in the dollar wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t sit well with President-elect Trump\u8217\'20\'19s intentions,\u8221\'20\'1D Mr. Maruyama said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s possible that he will try to correct the dollar\u8217\'20\'19s strength through verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said during the campaign that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taking advantage of the U.S. by exporting many cars. According to Monday\u8217\'20\'19s data, Japan exported 180,919 vehicles to the U.S. in November, up 26% from the year-earlier figure. Meanwhile, Japan\u8217\'20\'19s imports fell nearly 9% in November, the 23rd consecutive month of contraction.\par \par \uc2 Another risk is a sudden downturn in China, where government efforts to restrain credit growth have triggered market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 18, 2016 19:45 ET (00:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161219eccj0006a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
