\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Japan should not hesitate to act if yen spikes rapidly: Ex-MOF Watanabe\b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2016\par 06:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the yen spikes rapidly, even if U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  opposes such a move, the country's former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe made the remark a day after the Republican nominee's surprise win in the presidential election roiled global markets.\par \par \uc2 The yen surged more than 3 percent on Wednesday as Trump swept to victory over heavily favoured rival \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , prompting verbal warnings from Japanese policymakers who are concerned that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would derail the fragile export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Markets were calmer on Thursday, with the dollar reversing earlier selling against the safe-haven yen to touch a high of 105.87 yen, its highest since July 27.\par \par \uc2 "If the yen spikes by 3 yen or more, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 " regardless of U.S. concerns about such action, Watanabe told Reuters in an interview, adding that he told this recently to Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  and current top financial diplomat Masatsugu Asakawa.\par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hesitated to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of deference (to Trump), it would not be able to act for the coming four years," said Watanabe, who is now president of the Institute for International Monetary Affairs, a private think tank.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since Nov. 2011.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe served as vice finance minister for international affairs for three years to 2007, and has a close network with policymakers in and outside of Japan.\par \par \uc2 He said Japan-U.S. economic ties won't change much under Trump, shrugging off some concerns about trade friction.\par \par \uc2 Japan does not export as much to the United States as it used to, and Japanese carmakers are boosting direct investment and creating jobs there, he noted.\par \par \uc2 For now, Watanabe said the yen is likely to weaken slowly as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to go ahead with an interest rate hike in December or January, supporting the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , Watanabe said as long as the economy grows steadily, he saw no need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease already massive monetary stimulus further barring big shocks to financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think further easing would help much to spur flagging consumer prices," Watanabe said, adding that the BOJ should not be fixated on hitting its elusive 2 percent inflation target.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-13T05:30:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161110ecba00ac9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
