\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar, Mexican peso, stocks topple as Trump heads for White House\b0\par\par\uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2016\par 08:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar dives as investors face risk of shock Trump win\par \par \uc2 * Asia shares slump, US S&P 500 futures skid in Brexit re-run\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso hits lifetime low as safe-haven yen and euro gain\par \par \uc2 * Markets sharply lengthen odds on Fed hike in Dec, bonds rally\par \par \uc2 * Safe-haven yen and gold surge while oil slips\par \par \uc2 By Wayne Cole and Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/LONDON, Nov 9 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar sank and stocks plummeted in market mayhem on Wednesday as investors faced up to a shock win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the U.S. presidential election that could upend the global political order.\par \par \uc2 European shares looked set to follow with losses of more than 4 percent as every new TV network projection in the U.S. election showed the race to be far closer than anyone had thought, sending investors stampeding to safe-haven assets.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign bonds, the Japanese yen and gold surged while the Mexican peso went into near free-fall in chaotic trading as once again polls and betting markets proved woefully wrong.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are reacting as though the four horsemen of the apocalypse just rode out of Trump Tower," said Sean Callow, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 "Or at least 3 of them - it might be 4 when the prospect of a clean sweep of Congress sinks in."\par \par \uc2 As of 0742 GMT, news networks were calling the election for Republican candidate Trump and \uc2 CNN\uc2  reported that his Democrat rival Clinton had conceded.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures dived 5 percent at one point, worse than the carnage caused by the British vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in June that wiped trillions of dollars off world markets.\par \par \uc2 Investors fear a Trump victory could cause global economic and trade turmoil and years of policy unpredictability, which among other things will discourage the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  from raising interest rates in December as long expected.\par \par \uc2 Fed fund futures were even starting to toy with the idea of a cut in rates next year <0#FF:> and it was possible the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be forced to ease policy yet further.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets reeling, South Korean authorities were thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steady their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and dealers wondered if central banks globally would step in to calm nerves.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat signalled Tokyo's readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary as the surging yen threatened to snuff out its fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The scale of the scare was clear in the Mexican peso, which plunged more than 13 percent against the dollar at one point in the biggest daily move in two decades.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of Trump's negative geopolitical rhetoric was concentrated around Mexico and trade with Mexico and tearing up the \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  agreement, so the peso just become this natural barometer of the election," said \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Gautam Kalani.\par \par \uc2 The risk of a global trade war likewise hammered \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Asia, with the Australian dollar leading the rout.\par \par \uc2 The story was very different against the safe-haven yen, with the U.S. dollar shedding as much as 3.3 percent to 101.85 yen. The euro jumped 2.3 percent to $1.1278 as well though both had started to nudge off their highs as Europe opened.\par \par \uc2 Graphic of live election results: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2fxyZV0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2fxyZV0}}}\par \par \uc2 Graphic of live market reaction: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2fXfo0L"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2fXfo0L}}}\par \par \uc2 Live Coverage: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://live.reuters.com/event/election_2016"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://live.reuters.com/event/election_2016}}}\par \par \uc2 MAXIMUM UNCERTAINTY\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks skidded, with MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific stocks outside Japan down 2.5 percent and the Nikkei off a savage 5.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 With voting completed in more than two-thirds of the 50 U.S. states, the race was still too close to call in Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania and New Hampshire, states that could be vital to deciding who wins the presidency.\par \par \uc2 News channels projected Trump's Republicans had retained control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Markets had favoured Clinton as a status quo candidate who would be considered a safe pair of hands at home on the world stage. Analysts had no such certainty about Trump.\par \par \uc2 "With Brexit we had one bad day but this is different. This is what's scary about putting the most powerful position in the world in the hands of a man who many believe is temperamentally unstable," said Donald Selkin, chief market strategist at National Securities in New York.\par \par \uc2 "His tax cuts could open up a huge increase in the budget deficit and his trade sanctions could interrupt world trade. This could put us in a recession."\par \par \uc2 Sovereign bonds flew ahead, pushing yields on 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  notes down as much as 12 basis points to 1.75 percent, again the largest drop since Brexit, though they too pared back slightly to stand at 1.80 percent by 0715 GMT.\par \par \uc2 In commodity markets, safe-haven gold climbed 3.5 percent to $1,320 an ounce as the dollar slid.\par \par \uc2 Oil turned tail on concerns over the global economic outlook. U.S. crude shed $1.30 to $43.68 a barrel at its lowest, while Brent fell $1.15 to $44.89 before it steadied and clawed back to just above $45 barrel.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kim Coghill & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-9T08:46:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161109ecb900abc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
