\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  May Warn, But  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2 , BOJ Rate Cuts Unlikely: \uc2 Resona Bank\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 166 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2016\par 08:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japan government officials may signal their resolve to act against sharp market moves, but it's difficult to imagine their taking any concrete steps such as yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts, says Takashi Hiratsuka, a trading group leader at the asset-management division of \uc2 Resona Bank\uc2 . "It's difficult to anticipate an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar is above 100 yen," he says. "There have been negative effects from [the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 ] pushing down the yield curve, and so there is also a limit on monetary policy. Only fiscal policy is a likely option," he adds. Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Masatsugu Asakawa says the ministry will take appropriate action against speculative, excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. BOJ's \uc2 Masayoshi Amamiya\uc2  says the bank will closely monitor markets in cooperation with the government.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: PJK\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 09-11-16 0720GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020161109ecb9000cb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
