\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Govt Standing by to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in Volatile Market -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2016\par 03:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] As the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets have become volatile as results roll in for the U.S. presidential race, Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Yoshihide Suga\uc2  promises to take action in the markets if necessary. "We will carefully watch if there are any excessive movements in the markets and respond if necessary," he says. The dollar is currently trading around 102.40 yen, while the Nikkei Stock Average has dropped 2.2% to 16788.90 at the midday close. He adds the election result will have a huge impact on the global economy but refrained from making any comments on potential impact of TPP discussions. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The room for further increases in consumer-price inflation is likely to be limited as credit growth has begun to slow, Julian Evans-Pritchard says at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . Home prices are starting to cool and farmers have responded to high pork prices by boosting pig supply, he adds. "The upshot is that inflation is unlikely to be a major concern for policymakers over the coming year," he says. "Looking ahead, we think producer-price inflation will recover further in coming quarters but will top out at a little over 4% year-over-year before dropping back again." (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: AAP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0256 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Spot gold prices are rising in Asia, trading up 2.1% at $1,302.39/oz, as investors across financial markets flee to safety after polls suggest Republican \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  is edging ahead in battleground states. "We are seeing real risk-off sentiment," says Woon Tian Yong, an investment analyst at Phillip Futures. "We're seeing massive outflows from risky assets into safer assets like gold and even the yen." (ese.erheriene@wsj.com; Twitter: @Ese_Journo) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JVW \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY gyrates down, touching 102.04, its lowest since Oct. 3, amid early election results that are reminiscent of the Brexit shock, says a Japanese bank dealer, recalling the plunge to 99.00 on June 24. "Everyone is fretting over each and every election result," says the dealer. A potential Trump victory will likely cause knee-jerk buying into the safety of the JPY, but "it's hard to foresee how far the dollar could fall," now that the USD/JPY has briefly fallen below a recent low of 102.55 set on Nov. 3, the dealer adds.(hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0250 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Ray Attrill, the global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at National Australia, based in Sydney, stepped away from screens for 20 minutes to get lunch to return to see the U.S. electoral map glowing red and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the Mexican peso in sharp decline. "Everything has got a major risk-off and this has got a Brexit smell about it," he said. "Brexit, Trump coming to a country near you. Please name the next country in this sequence," he says. Sterling dropped by well over 10% on the day of Brexit and so far the peso has done close to a third of that. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "could do a hell of a lot more," he adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0246 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Investors are buying Asia-Pacific government bonds as latest US polls move in favor of Republican \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 , reversing an earlier sell-off. A Trump win is considered a more risky outcome and should boost the demand for safe-haven assets such as government bonds. Yields on the 10-year debt of Australia have dropped 6 basis points, bidding at 2.301%, after rise to a more than six-month high earlier, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Yields fall as bond prices rise. Yields on the 10-year debt of Australia, New Zealand, Singapore, Indonesia all followed a similar pattern: yields rose in early Asian trade but have since turned lower. Meanwhile, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will hold monetary policy meeting today. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  to hold rates, given its comments on the need to preserve policy ammunition. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Ray Attrill, the global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Australia Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , based in Sydney, stepped away from screens for 20 minutes to get lunch to return to see the U.S. electoral map glowing red and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the Mexican peso in sharp decline. "Everything has got a major risk-off and this has got a Brexit smell about it," he says. "Brexit, Trump coming to a country near you. Please name the next country in this sequence," he says. Sterling dropped by well over 10% on the day of Brexit and so far the peso has done close to a third of that. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "could do a hell of a lot more," he adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0242 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korean stocks turned lower in midday trading as the U.S. election results suggested that the race would be much tighter than some investors had expected. South Korea's benchmark Kospi index gave up a modest morning advance to fall 1.7% late Wednesday morning. Among the decliners was \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which gave up a morning gains of 0.8% to fall 2.4% late Wednesday morning. (jonathan.cheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @jchengwsj) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0241 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Japanese yen surges and Mexican peso drops Wednesday in Asian trade as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  consolidates a lead over \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hillary Clinton\f28 \uc2 \f2  among the states that have concluded voting. "It's a bit of a surprise, for sure," said Prashant Singh, senior portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Neuberger Berman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore, as many polls had forecasted a win for Clinton in recent days. Still, he added: "This is what you expect in the eventuality of a Trump win, with risk assets under pressure." The yen gained 2.7% against the dollar in recent action, with one dollar buying 102.41 yen compared with 105.14 late in New York trade on Tuesday. The Mexican peso slumped more than 7% against the dollar recently. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; Twitter: @saumvaish) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0237 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] As the yen strengthened to 102.5 against the dollar, investors in Japan are reminded of market turbulence earlier this year when the U.K. voted to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  and investors' flight to safety moving to the yen, seen as a safe haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "We remember Brexit in June, the situation is quite similar to that at this moment," says Masayuki Kubota, chief strategist at online brokerage \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rakuten Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  gains over \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hillary Clinton\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the race to the U.S. presidential bid. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @SuryatapaB) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Markets will likely stage a correction in the case of a surprise Trump win, which would lead to a knee-jerk reaction in sectors such as textiles, Indian IT services and Asian exporters, according to Catherine Yeung, investment director for equities at Fidelity. A Trump presidency would lead to a risk-off environment. There are major concerns and uncertainty surrounding his trade policies, which could include huge tariff hikes and blocking certain imported goods. Given the immense rise in passive investing, large-cap names would likely be sold off, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fidelity\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "This is event-driven risk. Investors need to look more at the structural story: balance sheet strength, brand equity, franchise strength, the rise of the middle class, and the competitive advantage of companies," advises Yeung. In the longer term, it's worth keeping in mind that policies to stifle trade dramatically will be difficult to pass through a potentially split American government. "It would be very hard to sell an Apple iPhone into the U.S. market without Taiwanese components, just given their sheer expertise and assembly lines," says Yeung. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0232 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Malaysian ringgit falls against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday morning as Republican nominee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  takes the lead halfway through the U.S election vote counting. The ringgit opens at 4.1800 against one U.S dollar and slips to 4.2000. The FTSE \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index declines 0.3% to 1,659.36 points. The 30-stock benchmark stock index remains relatively flat earlier Wednesday. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 08, 2016 21:58 ET (02:58 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161109ecb9000af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
