\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Japan PM Abe adviser says govt should consider more fiscal stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2016\par 13:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Adviser shifts emphasis to fiscal from monetary stimulus\par \par \uc2 * BOJ expected to stand pat after policy overhaul\par \par \uc2 * Hamada: No need for deeper negative rates, more bond buys (Adds details, quotes, context)\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko and Sumio Ito\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 26 (Reuters) - Japan should consider more government spending to maximise the impact of monetary policy, an adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said on Wednesday, indicating a shift in emphasis to fiscal from monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Koichi Hamada, long known as a "reflationist" keen to have the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  flood the economy with cash to kindle price rises, singled out the role of fiscal stimulus after a recent policy shift by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taken to mean the BOJ is in no hurry to boost its massive easing.\par \par \uc2 "Under the current monetary policy of keeping long-term interest rates around zero percent, if interest rates should rise due to more fiscal spending, monetary policy can rein such moves, and crowding out of private investment won't happen," Hamada, an emeritus professor of economics at Yale University and adviser to the cabinet, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "This means monetary policy will work even better."\par \par \uc2 The BOJ last month switched its policy target to controlling market interest rates from expanding the monetary base, after its massive asset purchases failed to generate sustained inflation.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to hold off on expanding stimulus next week, despite an expected downgrade in its price forecast that may show Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  won't see inflation hit his 2 percent target before his tenure ends in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Speculation is rising that the government will compile another extra budget for this fiscal year, even though parliament this month enacted a $32-billion second extra budget to help fund Abe's economic stimulus package.\par \par \uc2 "Fiscal policy needs to be considered to reinforce the effect from monetary policy, because the improvement in the labour market could stall and it is important that the current (improvement in) the labour market continues," Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 Hamada said the BOJ should not further cut the minus 0.1 percent rate it imposes on some excess reserves that commercial banks park with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as, he said, this hurts businesses. He also said the BOJ should not ramp up its asset purchases at the moment.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ last month committed to getting consumer prices above 2 percent, but Hamada said it would be enough to get "core-core" inflation, excluding food and energy prices, to rise about 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 If that is achieved, the government could raise the national sales tax by 1 percentage point a year, he said.\par \par \uc2 The government plans to raise the sales tax hike to 10 percent from 8 percent in October 2019, in a bid to curb the nation's ballooning debt, after having postponed the painful increase twice. The economy slid into recession after a hike to 8 percent from 5 percent in April 2014.\par \par \uc2 Hamada repeated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if yen spikes were caused by speculative moves. And he reiterated buying foreign bonds would be an option for the BOJ if yen sharply appreciates.\par \par \uc2 "When excessive moves are seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Finance Ministry needs to punish speculators," he said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar stood around 104.00 yen on Wednesday, after having hit an almost 3-month high of 104.87 yen in overseas trading on expectations of a U.S. rate hike in December. (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko and Sumio Ito; Editing by William Mallard and Clarence Fernandez)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-10-29T11:22:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161026ecaq00jd9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
