\par\par \b \uc2 Yellen Closer to Embracing Fed Stock Purchases -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2016\par 23:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 17:11 ET - Yellen moves a little closer to embracing stock purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Oopening up the stock market to Fed purchases could give policy makers an extra tool to fight an economic downturn, she says. Using equity purchases as a monetary-policy instrument has become a hot topic among economists, particularly in Europe where the \uc2 ECB\uc2  faces restraints on the government bonds it can purchase through its quantitative-easing program. Central bankers in Switzerland and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. Yellen's comments suggest the US might join that club if a crisis hits. "There could be benefits to the ability to buy either equities or corporate bonds," she says. "There would also be costs as well that would have to be carefully considered." Allowing the Fed to buy stocks would require an act of Congress. (david.harrison@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:56 ET - Eight years after the global financial crisis prompted a sharp leg down in interest rates, Fitch is concerned life insurers will begin "over-reaching for yield by increasing exposure to higher risk assets." Life insurers earn a substantial portion of profits by investing customers' premiums until needed to pay claims. Insurers have increased allocations to alternative investments, private placements and commercial mortgages, given the low yields of the high-quality corporate bonds they typically favor, and for now, strategies are "reasonable," the firm says. Fitch sees a rate hike in December as likely but says it won't be enough to avert continued declines in portfolio yields over the next 12 to 18 months. (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:51 ET - The Mexican peso rebounded briefly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates for the third time this year--to 4.75% from 4.25%--but ended weaker on the day at 19.4810 versus 19.3730 Wednesday. "We believe that, while the [\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] move might not be sufficient to dramatically change the current direction of the peso in the short term, it will help to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says. The IPC stock index gave up early gains and closed down 0.8%, in line with US equity-market losses. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:44 ET - New York-based law firm \uc2 Cadwalader, Wickersham & Taft\uc2  is closing two offices in Asia, citing a change in strategy. In an internal firm memo, Cadwalader leadership says offices in Hong Kong and Beijing will close as part of a renewed focus to primarily serve its core client groups of financial institutions, large corporates and funds. Four equity partners and 21 other lawyers will lose their jobs. Other US law firms have pulled out of Asia in recent years, including Fried, Frank, Harris, Shriver & Jacobson. Western firms operating in Asia face restrictions on practicing local law and intense competition. (sara.randazzo@wsj.com; @sara_randazzo)\par \par \uc2 16:40 ET - Industrial production fell 5.7% on the year in August, says Argentina's statistics agency. Industrial output has now fallen on the year for seven consecutive months. The bad news comes after the government said earlier this week that the economy shrank 5.9% in July from a year ago. Officials note, though, that cement shipments were up in August, a sign that the construction industry is beginning to recover just as the government ramps up spending on infrastructure projects. Economists expect the economy to shrink about 1.5% this year before expanding 3%-4% next year. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 16:34 ET - The Agriculture Department says it will, starting Oct. 5, add cattle sales that take place on a new online exchange to the regular reports the agency publishes on prices meatpackers pay for cattle in the negotiated markets. The \uc2 USDA\uc2  service mandates participation by large livestock buyers, to add transparency to the marketplace. The Fed Cattle Exchange, run by Superior Livestock Auction, this year launched a platform where feedlot sellers and meatpacker buyers could post online bids, asking prices and eventual deals for slaughter-ready cattle. US cattlemen pushed for the exchange in response to concerns about a lack of transparency in negotiated and futures prices. (kelsey.gee@wsj.com; @kelseykgee)\par \par \uc2 16:20 ET - The \uc2 Conservation Law Foundation\uc2  announces it plans to sue \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  (XOM), the first US civil lawsuit focused on environmental damages tied to the company's record of climate science and advocacy, according to the group. Citing journalism from last year that XOM has disputed, the group alleged in a press release that XOM misled regulators about whether its assets in Everett can withstand rising seas. The lawsuit "aims to hold \uc2 Exxon\uc2  accountable for decades of dishonesty," according to the foundation. (bradley.olson@wsj.com; @bradnews).\par \par \uc2 16:08 ET - Yesterday's glow from rallying oil prices wears off as US stocks fall on weakness in health care, financials and utilities. Concerns that hedge funds are being more cautious in dealing with \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  counterparty risk weighs on the broader financial sector. Health care shares fall with some disappointing drug trial results hurting sentiment in pharmaceuticals and volatile biotechnology shares. DJIA falls 195 points to 18143, the S&P 500 loses 20 to 2151 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  slips 49 to 5269. Treasury prices end higher on safe haven flows after a day of choppy trading, with the 10-year yield slipping to 1.56%. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:00 ET - \uc2 Sally Beauty\uc2  (SBH) shares have yet to get back to the $30 range where they traded shortly before last month's F3Q sales miss at its namesake outlets, and today isn't helping. The beauty retailer is off 7.5% at $25.96 following the departure of its CFO to "pursue other interests." While Cowen says management changes don't affect its long-term optimism, the departure comes at a time when SBH has yet to gauge the full extent of "tactical marketing changes," made over the summer. SBH hired a new email marketer, sent fewer coupons and made changes to its loyalty database, which Cowen believes contributed to 3Q sales pressure. Still rating SBH at outperform, Cowen says "SBH still has a lot to prove." (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:55 ET - African Americans and Hispanics accounted for a slightly larger share of mortgages that lenders extended to home buyers in 2015. Black borrowers accounted for 5.2% of purchase home mortgages in 2015, up from 4.9% a year prior, according to data from the \uc2 Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council\uc2 . Hispanic borrowers accounted for 7.9% of home-purchase mortgages, up from 7.5%. But Asian Americans fell to 5.5% of purchase mortgages extended last year, down from 5.7% in 2014. Their share increased in the refi market, from 4.5% to 5.1%. Despite the pickup for blacks and Hispanics, they experienced higher denial rates for home-purchase mortgages that lenders sold off to \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  or \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  than white applicants, according to the data. The data encompasses 6,913 US banks and other financial institutions covered under the Home Mortgage Disclosure Act. (annamaria.andriotis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:54 ET - \uc2 UBS\uc2 , after experimenting with blockchain technology for about two years, plans to build a trade finance system that uses distributed ledgers to streamline import-export transactions globally. Working with \uc2 IBM\uc2 , the Swiss bank says so-called smart contracts on a blockchain ledger--the technology that supports bitcoin digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --could shrink trade finance transaction time from a typical seven days to one hour. \uc2 IBM\uc2  predicts a production system will be ready in 12 to 18 months, while UBS Senior Innovation Manager Alex Batlin declined to cite a timeframe. "There are too many moving parts to give a timelines," he says. (kim.nash@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:53 ET - Mortgages that are insured by the \uc2 Federal Housing Administration\uc2  accounted for a greater share of home loans lenders extended last year for home purchases and refinances, according to new federal data. \uc2 FHA\uc2  mortgages accounted for 25% of mortgages extended to home buyers in 2015, up from 21% a year prior. They accounted for 14% of refinances, up from 9%. The increase could be due to the \uc2 FHA\uc2 's reduction in the annual mortgage insurance premium in early 2015, according to the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 . Lower costs may have spurred demand among more borrowers. (annamaria.andriotis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2016 17:11 ET (21:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160929ec9t003im\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
