\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Down Slightly, Led by Auto Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2016\par 02:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japanese stocks are down slightly, led by auto stocks, after \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's stance of gradual tightening weakened the dollar against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen. Japanese exporters, including auto makers, are vulnerable to the yen's strength. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) is down 3.1% at 2,984.5 yen. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) is 3.1% lower at Y5,955. Some profit-taking is also kicking in after \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  introduced the new yield curve control that is seen friendly to financial stocks and boosted them on Wednesday. \uc2 Dai-ichi Life Insurance\uc2  (8750.TO) is down 3.3% at Y1,495.0. \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2  (8306.TO) is off 2.0% at Y542.6. "We are seeing some pullback, but BOJ's communication is getting better," says Hideyuki Ishiguro, senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Securities\uc2 . "Now that the target is on the interest rate, there is greater latitude for easing." Nikkei is down 0.1% at 16789.58. Japanese markets were closed Thursday for a public holiday.\par \par \uc2 (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0042 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Oil prices edge lower in Asia trade Friday morning on profit-taking as excitement and hopes for action by \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  to either cut or freeze production has started to fizzle. Traders remain pessimistic that \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Russia can agree on an oil production cap due to the lack of chatter or a significant breakthrough, says Stuart Ive, a client manager at OM Financial. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 36 cents at $45.96 a barrel, Brent is down 27 cents at $47.38 a barrel. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0028 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/SGD is creeping up, building on yesterday's bounce off the Ichimoku Cloud support zone, which was triggered by a significant rebound of USD/JPY. USD/SGD may consolidate within a range of 1.3503-1.3643, propped up by the Cloud and capped by the entrance of the Bollinger uptrend channel. The greenback is unlikely to launch into a sustained rally, as the outlook for U.S. rates remains subdued. However, the post-FOMC dollar-selling wave in Asia has tentatively ended, after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warned that it could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to arrest the excessive strengthening of the yen. The yen had appreciated 1.4% after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  flattened its long-term interest rate projections. USD/SGD is now 1.3572 from its Thursday closing of 1.3560. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0022 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW is propped up before the 1,103 entrance of the Bollinger downtrend channel, and could inch higher following the bounce of USD/JPY. The latter turned around from weakness after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warned that it could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after an excessive strengthening of the yen due to speculation in the wake of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's FOMC meeting. The buoyancy of USD/JPY is also pushing other USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs higher. If USD/KRW ends the day above the 1,103 mark, it may consolidate in a range of 1,103-1,113 in the near term, capped by the Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone. But if it slips into the Bollinger downtrend channel, it could head for the 1,080 round-figure barrier. USD/KRW is now 1,104.8 from its Thursday closing of 1,103.3. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0012 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are drifting around Friday after a bounce in USD/JPY sapped the momentum of yesterday's dollar-selling wave, which was triggered by a dovish rate outlook from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 . On Thursday, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  warned of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yen was fluctuating excessively due to speculation. That kept the yen from testing the 100.00 round-figure trading barrier, which if breached might have caused a spike. The fundamental factors are still negative for USD/Asia, however, as the market embraces a more sanguine outlook for U.S. monetary policy, which promotes risk-taking, especially in high-yield emerging market assets. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2346 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The Japanese yen is likely to prove sensitive to a weakening dollar and continued rise in oil prices. The greenback fell broadly against all major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged Wednesday. Oil prices have been pushed higher by a surprise drop in U.S. inventories and a weakening dollar. This helped make oil, priced in dollars, more affordable to buyers who use foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The yen is up 0.07% at 100.83 against the dollar. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @SuryatapaB)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2334 GMT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues to have concerns over \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia & New Zealand Banking\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (ANZ.AU) execution of its restructuring efforts. The brokerage believes there's an attractive three-year story on costs and restructuring at ANZ, but over a one-year view it says there are worries about funding sizable additional provisions, revenue loss from rebalancing the institutional and Asia portfolio and the prospect of additional bad-debt charges as the bank exits Asian corporate exposures. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  adds ANZ is booking a surprisingly large provision to settle a legal dispute over the sale of a fertilizer plant, prompting a 1% reduction in fiscal 2016 earnings estimates. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RobbMStewart)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2232 GMT - RBA Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philip Lowe\f28 \uc2 \f2  appeared before the House of Representatives Standing Committee on Economics this week, and he was relatively upbeat on the economy. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lowe's\f28 \uc2 \f2  testimony, along with recent communication from the RBA, suggest the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is comfortably in a wait-and-see mode around interest rates. That said, an easing bias remains in place given its outlook for inflation. ANZ says it expects to see interest rates on hold at 1.5% for now. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2212 GMT - The New Zealand dollar is down 0.6% following the RBNZ's signal of further easing Thursday. "Our current projections and assumptions indicate that further policy easing will be required to ensure that future inflation settles near the middle of the target range" and that "A decline in the exchange rate is needed" to boost tradeables inflation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says. NAB says the RBNZ statement is consistent with cutting interest rates in November. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2202 GMT - The Australian dollar-New Zealand dollar cross has been a strong performer in the last 24 hours, rising to NZ$1.0453 from around NZ$1.036 prior. CBA says dovish comments by the RBNZ 24 hours ago containing the words "further easing is required" and "a lower exchange rate is needed" have had a modest dampening effect on the kiwi dollar, but it is likely to find support above US$0.7300. CBA anticipates the kiwi will remain well-supported. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2153 GMT - The Australian dollar lifted to touch a fresh two-week high of US$0.7675 in overnight trade. A softer US dollar post the September-FOMC meeting, and the virtual certainty of no interest rate rise by the Fed until mid-December, has weakened the US dollar, and helped generate a rally in industrial metal prices and global equity markets. CBA says it anticipates the US dollar will probably spend some time consolidating into the rest of the week, with a bias to move slightly lower. The pair now trades at US$0.7642. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2116 GMT - Something unusual happened on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets overnight--at least if the past few weeks' trading is anything to go by. "Despite a generally weaker USD session overnight, the NZD underperformed (which is not something we have said very often lately!)," ANZ says. The NZD/USD was last at 0.7311, down from 0.7329 in US trading hours. With New Zealand's economic outlook well appreciated, ANZ thinks global drivers will hold sway from here. "While the New Zealand dollar may have some further downside prodding over the coming weeks, we still expect dips to be shallow," ANZ says. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 22, 2016 20:46 ET (00:46 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160923ec9n00065\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
