\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Markets on Hold, Already Gearing for Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 3,018 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2016\par 05:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Snapshot: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks seen flat; dollar steady; bonds seen rangebound; Brent crude $46.34; gold $1314.68\f28 \par \par \uc2 -\uc2 ECB\uc2 's Weidmann Warns on Extended Use of Easy-Money Policies\par \par \uc2 -Japan Finance Minister Says BOJ Must Call Shots on Monetary Policy\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Is Asked to Pay $14 Billion to Resolve U.S. Probe\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Unilever\uc2  Is in Talks to Acquire Jessica \uc2 Alba's Honest Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -Fiat Chrysler Recalls 1.9 Million Vehicles Globally\par \par \uc2 Watch For: \uc2 EU\uc2  summit in Slovakia; no major data or earnings scheduled \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Headline News/Outlook: \par ===================== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bundesbank President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jens Weidmann\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a fresh warning Thursday over the risks of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's easy-money policies, which he said could curb productivity and undermine banks' ability to lend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Mr. Weidmann, who sits on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 25-member governing council, also gave a measured defense of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's current stimulus, which he said could help create jobs and boost tax revenues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments, from a prominent critic of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's government bond purchases, come amid a fresh wave of criticism in Germany of \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  policies. ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  will travel to Berlin later this month to address German lawmakers, some of whom have expressed strong concerns over the effect of ultra low interest rates on German savers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Low interest rates aren't just negative," Mr. Weidmann said. "Citizens as a rule aren't just savers, but also workers, shareholders, taxpayers or property owners."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Mr. Weidmann added, has been about as successful as the Bundesbank at keeping prices stable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the Bundesbank President warned that easy-money policies could allow unviable firms to stay in business and harm productivity, by slowing structural changes in the corporate sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also criticized analysts whom he said were calling for ever deeper \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in financial markets by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday that monetary policy is up to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to decide, staying out of debate over whether the economy needs deeper negative rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My fundamental stance is that monetary policy should be left for the BOJ," Mr. Aso said at a news conference.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  leaders -- minus the U.K.'s \f28 \uc2 \f2 Theresa May\f28 \uc2 \f2  -- gather for a brainstorming exercise in Bratislava Friday on what to do next in and with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  once Britain leaves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials have sought to lower expectations and cast it as a meeting that is "part of the process" of coming to terms with Brexit and what the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  will do next. No written statement -- customary for \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  summits -- is being prepared for the meeting. No major overhaul of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  institutions or their powers are expected, as leaders are still fundamentally at odds over whether repatriating more powers from Brussels or doubling down on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  institutions' responsibilities is the answer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beginning Friday, the top uniformed military officers from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2  will begin a two-day fall meeting in Split, Croatia. On top of the agenda is working on plans to implement the new Baltic military force approved by the alliance in July. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Stocks: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock futures were largely unchanged Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were higher, helped by gains in Apple's suppliers, while some uncertainty remained over Japan and U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy outcome next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was up 0.5%, and Australia's \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  200 was up 1.1%. Markets in China, Hong Kong, and South Korea were closed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology stocks led major U.S. indexes higher, the fourth big move in five sessions but volatility has returned to stocks after being largely absent for most of the summer. Thursday's 1% gain for the S&P 500 marked the fourth session out of five in which the index swung 1% or more. Before that, the index went 43 trading days without a 1% move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All 30 components of the Dow industrials rose. The blue-chip index rallied 1%, along with the S&P 500, and the Nasdaq Composite rose 1.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brad McMillan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , chief investment officer for Commonwealth Financial Network, said that many investors remain focused on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You're getting this common narrative from central banks that maybe they've done all they can do, and I think that's worrying markets, but, at the same time, stimulus continues," he said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Corporate News: \par ============== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Justice Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  proposed that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  pay $14 billion to settle a set of high-profile mortgage-securities probes stemming from the financial crisis, according to people familiar with the matter, a number that would rank among the largest of what other banks have paid to resolve similar claims and is well above what investors have been expecting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The figure is described by people close to the negotiations between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the government as preliminary, and they said it came up in discussions between the bank and government lawyers in recent days. It hasn't been previously disclosed. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expected to push back strongly against it, the people said, and it is far from clear what the final outcome will be.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  is in talks to acquire \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honest Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., the consumer-products retailer co-founded by actress Jessica Alba, according to people familiar with the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  is discussing a deal valued at over $1 billion but significantly less than the $1.7 billion valuation that was placed on Honest in a fundraising round last year, the people said. The talks are at an early stage, and Honest hasn't ruled out going for an initial public offering instead, one of the people said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiat Chrysler\f28 \uc2 \f2  recalled 1.9 million vehicles world-wide for possible air-bag and seal-belt failures linked to at least three deaths and five injuries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The auto maker said Thursday that vehicles equipped with a certain occupant restraint control module and front-impact sensor wiring with a specific design can fail to activate air bags and seat-belt restraints in some crashes. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was steady in Asia having slid Thursday, weighed down by a round of weak U.S. economic data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The soft data has some investors questioning whether the economy is strong enough to bear a rate increase from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  in coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed-funds futures, used to bet on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, on Thursday showed investors and traders assigned a 46% likelihood of a rate increase in December, down from above 53% a day ago, data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME\f28 \uc2 \f2  showed. The chances for an increase at the Fed's September 20-21 meeting stood at just 12%, down from more 15% on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts believe markets may be underpricing the chances of a rate increase in coming months. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may attempt to keep a December rate increase in play in the statement accompanying next week's decision, analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If markets are skeptical, however, "the dollar would likely challenge the lower ends of its recent ranges (against) the euro and yen," analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Bonds: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bonds are seen rangebound at the open, with prices of long-dated \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt falling again on Thursday after a brief respite, underscoring investors' uneasiness amid lofty bond valuations and worries over less monetary stimulus support from major central banks overseas.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest trigger: a round of tepid releases including retail sales and industrial production cemented expectation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unlikely to raise interest rates next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That view drove investors to sell long-term debt and buy short-term notes as the latter's yields are more sensitive to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy outlook. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 premium investors\f28 \uc2 \f2  demanded to hold the benchmark 10-year Treasury note relative to the two-year note rose to a four-month high as appetites for long-term debt diminish.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late afternoon trading, the yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note settled at 1.703%, higher than 1.689% on Wednesday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Energy: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices fell in early Asian trade in the wake of speculation about a recovery in exports from Libya and Nigeria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Libya's state oil company lifted curbs on sales from three ports on Wednesday. The market is also struggling with the prospect of a potential recovery in output from some \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  members.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0227 GMT, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  was down 26 cents at $43.65/bbl, while Brent was 25 cents lower at $46.34/bbl. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Metals: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices retreated to a week's low, despite weak U.S. retail sales data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are trading cautiously amid uncertainty about Fed's direction of a possible increase in interest rates. And there have been speculative outflows from gold-focused exchange traded funds, which has weakened the metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0220 GMT, gold was at $1,314.68/ounce, down from $1,315.29/ounce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to paul.larkins@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 Sep 2016 23:55 ET \par European Morning Briefing: DJ Exclusive Headlines; Other Top Stories\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DJ Exclusive Headlines:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  Is in Talks to Acquire Jessica \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alba's Honest Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/unilever-is-in-talks-to-acquire-jessica-albas-honest-co-1473981465"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/unilever-is-in-talks-to-acquire-jessica-albas-honest-co-1473981465}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Regulator Tells Betterment to Revise Policies After June Trading Halt\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/regulator-tells-betterment-to-revise-policies-after-june-trading-halt-1473962235"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/regulator-tells-betterment-to-revise-policies-after-june-trading-halt-1473962235}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 SEC's Former High-Frequency Trading Expert Joins Citadel\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/secs-former-high-frequency-trading-expert-joins-citadel-1473978359?tpl=financialregulation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/secs-former-high-frequency-trading-expert-joins-citadel-1473978359?tpl=financialregulation}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michel Temer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Encourages Foreign Investment\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/brazil-president-michel-temer-encourages-foreign-investment-1473960926"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/brazil-president-michel-temer-encourages-foreign-investment-1473960926}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Top Stories:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Weidmann Warns on Extended Use of Easy-Money Policies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bundesbank President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jens Weidmann\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a fresh warning Thursday over the risks of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's easy-money policies, which he said could curb productivity and undermine banks' ability to lend.\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/ecbs-weidmann-warns-on-extended-use-of-easy-money-policies-1473962354?tpl=centralbanking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/ecbs-weidmann-warns-on-extended-use-of-easy-money-policies-1473962354?tpl=centralbanking}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan Finance Minister Says BOJ Must Call Shots on Monetary Policy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday that monetary policy is up to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to decide, staying out of debate over whether the economy needs deeper negative rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Is Asked to Pay $14 Billion to Resolve U.S. Probe\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Justice Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  proposed that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  pay $14 billion to settle a set of high-profile mortgage-securities probes stemming from the financial crisis, according to people familiar with the matter, a number that would rank among the largest of what other banks have paid to resolve similar claims and is well above what investors have been expecting.\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/deutsche-bank-is-asked-to-pay-14-billion-to-resolve-u-s-probe-into-mortgage-securities-1473975404?tpl=financialregulation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/deutsche-bank-is-asked-to-pay-14-billion-to-resolve-u-s-probe-into-mortgage-securities-1473975404?tpl=financialregulation}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiat Chrysler Recalls 1.9 Million Vehicles Globally\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles NV\f28 \uc2 \f2  recalled 1.9 million vehicles world-wide for possible air-bag and seat-belt failures linked to at least three deaths and five injuries.\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/fiat-chrysler-recalls-1-9-million-vehicles-globally-1473969448