\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan PM's adviser says: BOJ buying foreign bonds is an option to weaken yen\b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2016\par 12:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * BOJ' foreign bonds buying is moderate form of \b \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * It could dispel views BOJ has reached limit\par \par \uc2 * Rolling back BOJ's policy in Sept would be "worst decision" (adds more quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko and Sumio Ito\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 30 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could consider buying foreign bonds as an option to weaken the yen, if government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is deemed by the United States to be exchange rate manipulation, an adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market not to change the yen's general course but to discourage speculators - who bet too much on the rise of the yen - whenever it rises excessively," Koichi Hamada, an emeritus professor of economics at Yale University, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considered manipulation of exchange rates, BOJ buying of foreign bonds is an option for the same objective in a moderate form," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  industrial powers have committed to avoiding competitive devaluations while warning against wild exchange-rate moves.\par \par \uc2 At a G7 meeting in Japan in May, U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew told Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  that it was important to refrain from competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations.\par \par \uc2 "I think the government had a fairly stern warning from U.S. authorities not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 He said BOJ purchases of foreign bonds is sensitive but it would help to dispel views that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has reached the limit" of its policy options.\par \par \uc2 As exchange rate policy is the government's preserve rather than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has avoided buying foreign bonds in the past.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  was Japan's top financial diplomat in his career at the Finance Ministry and repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen against the dollar, typically by selling large amounts of yen for dollars and using the proceeds to buy U.S. Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 Kuroda has ruled out the BOJ buying foreign bonds to ease monetary policy because it can be considered as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November 2011. The yen is far weaker than when Kuroda became \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief in early 2013, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rebounded some 20 percent this year against the dollar, raising worries in government that it could hurt Japan's exports.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Yoshihide Suga\uc2  told Reuters on Tuesday the government is ready to respond "appropriately", when asked whether Tokyo could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem excessive yen rises.\par \par \uc2 Hamada expressed hope that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would raise U.S. interest rates, after top Fed officials made hawkish statements in recent days.\par \par \uc2 "If the U.S. really delivers a rate hike, then it will be blessing for the Japanese economy and I don't need to persuade the finance ministry to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as it naturally will lower the yen," he said.\par \par \uc2 Turning to monetary policy, Hamada said it is difficult to say whether \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will ease next month, before the next Fed rate decision on Sept. 21.\par \par \uc2 "Some speculate that the BOJ may roll back its monetary policy. That would be the worst decision," he said. (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko and Sumio Ito, additional reorting by Chang-Ran Kim, Editing by William Mallard and Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-9-2T10:44:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160830ec8u00htq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
