\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Shares Rebound, with Banks Leading Gains -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2016\par 07:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] After two days of declines, Indian shares pick up steam Thursday as investors are more comfortable that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  won't raise interest rates in a hurry. The S&P BSE Sensex climbs 0.7%, led partly by banks like \uc2 ICICI Bank\uc2 , which is up 1.6%, as well as the \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2 , which is up nearly 1.2%, and \uc2 Axis Bank\uc2 , up 1%. Investors expect the \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates later this year, which would help boost profits for banks. (shefali.anand@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0516 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/KRW has nearly returned to flat against Wednesday's closing, in a bounce triggered by the rally of USD/JPY back above the 100.00 round-figure trading barrier. The latest round of verbal threats at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed the yen's earlier rally to 99.62 versus the dollar. USD/JPY currently at 100.16 is almost unchanged from yesterday's closing. The MOF also warned of action to arrest excessive appreciation of the yen on Wednesday, but its effect was short-lived. USD/KRW is likely to dribble lower if USD/JPY slips under 100.00 again. The USD/KRW Bollinger downtrend channel, which could encourage bets on the South Korea won, will be reinstated if the dollar ends the day weaker than 1,101.4. USD/KRW is now at 1,108.0 from its Wednesday closing of 1,108.3. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0515 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY erases earlier losses to hit as high as 100.35 (from around 100.10), after The Wall Street Journal cited Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  advisor Etsuro Honda that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will take bold action next month. "The USD/JPY was rising on this article," says a senior bank dealer. There has been market speculation that the BOJ may pare back its stimulus during a planned review of its monetary easing steps in September. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0450 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 , calls the yen's recent strength "clearly excessive," underlying growing frustrations among Tokyo officials with the soaring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, Honda urges Tokyo authorities to take "bold" action on the yen "no matter what U.S. officials say." The remark reflects his worries that yen buyers are becoming more aggressive in their speculation that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -policy hands are tied by U.S. opposition to any effort to weaken the yen. Honda notes that the yen's strength means that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's monetary easing is insufficient. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0449 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 , wants the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to expand its quantitative easing program by 25% to Y100 trillion a year, mainly by increasing Japanese government bond purchases. He spurns a wide-spread view among economists that the Japanese government bond purchase program has reached its limit. Honda emphasizes that the BOJ has only one third of JGBs in the market, so there are many more bonds it can still buy. What Japan needs now is to have the BOJ indirectly finance increased government spending, what would be "de facto" helicopter money, Honda says. "We must fire a bold monetary bazooka once again and increase fiscal spending in order to change expectations for the future," Honda adds. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0449 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's coming "comprehensive assessment" will likely reach the conclusion that monetary easing is inadequate, leaving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  no choice than to step up its stimulus measures, says Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The planned policy review has so far generated a range of speculation in the markets--some see it as a step toward further easing, while others suspect it may open the door to eventual policy withdrawals. Honda is in the first group, tipping a "more than 50%" possibility of bold monetary easing coming September 21. His belief in the need for monetary easing is so obvious that the assessment is unnecessary. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0449 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] More Japanese government bond purchases by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  may cause the yield curve to further flatten, making the life of commercial banks more difficult. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should still proceed with the purchases, says Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 . That's because the impact a flattened curve could have on banks' profitability is ultimately smaller and less protracted than the impact that continued deflation could have on it, Honda says. "We need to overcome deflation as soon as possible, or financial institutions would become unprofitable" eventually, he says. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0448 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  ought to step up its quantitative easing next month to rejuvenate inflation expectations, but it doesn't need to further lower its negative deposit rate from the current minus 0.1%, says Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Further cuts could add to concerns about the health of commercial banks, whose profits have taken a hit from the negative rate policy, he says. Honda's stance toward negative rates, while unchanged from the previous months, underlines how unpopular the measure remains among the public, banks and government policy-makers. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0448 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A view is growing among circles of Japanese economists that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy has reached its limits. However, Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 , isn't buying it. "I don't think he feels that way," Honda says of whether Abe agrees with those economists. Honda does acknowledge that the effects of the BOJ's quantitative easing have been diminishing, but he says that means that the bank ought to do more, not that it should give up. That's the "spirit of Abenomics," Honda says. The remarks underline re-intensifying debate in Japan over how much monetary policy can do in fighting deflation---debate that has continued since the late 1990s. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0446 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] For bondholders, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Natixis\f28 \uc2 \f2  believes Chinese state-owned enterprises that are controlled directly by the central government, and Chinese corporates with relevant exposure to offshore markets, are safer bets and the chance for bondholders to recover at least part of their investment is higher in cases of default. For example, central government-owned Sinosteel Group, which defaulted onshore bonds last year, could now undergo a debt-to-equity swap. In the offshore market, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guosen Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  injected capital to prevent its Hong Kong unit from defaulting on its dim sum bond in April. This demonstrates that the Chinese government is finding ways to rescue these troubled companies. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Natixis\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks any event in the offshore market would be highly detrimental to the cost of funding for Chinese corporates. This could also deter foreign investors' interest in China and would certainly be a blow to China's plans to attract inflows. Hence, Chinese authorities will likely find ways to prevent any negative impact or spillover. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0445 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY (now at 100.08) may come to stay below Y100, if the pair gains downside momentum to fall below 99.50, says Marito Ueda, director at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Prime by GMO. That may weaken upside momentum of the USD/JPY to "reach Y101 at most if any recovery," Mr. Ueda says. "There are no crucial support points" of the pair until around 94.60 (a 61.8 Fibonacci retracement line using the USD/JPY high and low since 2011), Mr. Ueda says. The falloff below 100 looks inevitable in light of a no-devaluation pledge among industrialized nations, but short-term negative impact of a higher JPY is hard to ignore, ranging from a cutback in exporters' profitably to weaker spending by tourists venturing to Japan. That leaves the possibility of a worsening real economy in Japan, he says. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0415 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Foreign investments into Malaysian government bonds are strong and are expected to remain so at a time where many developed markets are giving negative yield. "Malaysia government bonds are attractive," David Ng, chief investment officer of Affin Hwang Asset Management says Thursday, adding that investors get a yield of 3.5% from Malaysia versus negative yield in 40% of developed markets. Foreign investors upped their holdings of Malaysia debt by 5.7 billion ringgit (US$1.4 billion) in July, mainly driven by inflows into government bonds, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Affin Hwang Asset's Mr. Ng says the current foreign holdings of Malaysian bonds also marks a record high. Kuala Lumpur-based Affin Hwang Management has some MYR34.8 billion in assets under administration as of the end of July. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 18, 2016 01:19 ET (05:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160818ec8i000a1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
