\par\par \b \uc2 Thailand's Baht Retreats From 13-Month High Vs USD -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2016\par 04:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand's baht is down Wednesday, trading at 34.67 to the dollar, sharply reversing from yesterday's 13-month high of 34.49. The retreat of the baht, as well as other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was sparked by a weakening of the yen on the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Verbal threats by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier, warning of action if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates too rapidly, drove USD/JPY slightly higher. The dollar was on its knees before that, after an overnight selloff that sent the benchmark ICE U.S. dollar index to a two-month low. The release of July's U.S. FOMC minutes later today could restrain the dollar's bounce. If the minutes reveal that voting members of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  remain dovish, the greenback may subside. USD/THB is now 34.67 from its Tuesday closing of 34.61. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 16, 2016 22:57 ET (02:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160817ec8h00080\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
