\par\par \b \uc2 Japan manufacturers may cut profit targets on post-Brexit yen surge -Reuters poll\b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2016\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * 38 pct of manufacturers may cut targets due to Brexit impact               \par \par \uc2 * Electronics, auto firms particularly worried about Brexit impact               \par \par \uc2 * 63 pct of firms think govt should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen               \par \par \uc2 By Chris Gallagher               \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 22 (Reuters) - Nearly four in 10 Japanese manufacturers say they may cut their annual earnings targets in the wake of Britain's decision to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , fretting about the impact of a surge in the safe-haven yen, a Reuters poll showed.               \par \par \uc2 Worries were particularly acute at major exporting industries with 61 percent of electronics firms and 57 percent of auto companies flagging the possibility of downward revisions.               \par \par \uc2 Lower corporate profits would complicate Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's efforts to regalvanise a sputtering economy, as firms would have less scope for wage increases and capital investment - seen as key to ensuring a sustained recovery.               \par \par \uc2 "More than Britain's exit from the EU, it's the accompanying strengthening of the yen that is tough on earnings," wrote a manager at an electrical machinery firm.               \par \par \uc2 Almost all firms flagging possible downward revisions said they expected any cuts to be small rather than large, according to the Reuters Corporate Survey which was conducted July 1-15.               \par \par \uc2 Even so, 63 percent of firms said they thought the government should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the yen, underscoring angst about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.               \par \par \uc2 Market expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are, however, low as the yen's current strength has not been driven by factors specific to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any action taken by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone would likely be limited in effectiveness. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2011.               \par \par \uc2 The survey, conducted monthly for Reuters by Nikkei Research, polled 534 big and medium-sized firms with managers responding on condition of anonymity. Around 260 companies answered questions on the impact of the Brexit decision.               \par \par \uc2 Corporate Japan was also downbeat on the outlook for the domestic economy, with 62 percent of firms forecasting almost no growth while another 24 percent think there could be periods of economic contraction in upcoming quarters.                              \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  PAINS               \par \par \uc2 Large Japanese manufacturers have on average forecast assumed a rate of 111 yen for the financial year to next March and are expecting a 12 percent decline in recurring profits, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.               \par \par \uc2 But those assumptions now look quite rosy.               \par \par \uc2 The yen - seen as a safe haven during periods of risk aversion - strengthened in the run up to the June 23 Brexit vote - and then jumped to as high as 99 to the dollar in the  immediate aftermath.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later pulled back, trading between 102 and 106 during the survey period. It was trading at around 106 to the dollar on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 In the survey, 64 percent of all firms predicted the yen would on average trade at least 5 percent stronger than their assumed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates for the rest of the financial year.               \par \par \uc2 "Overall concern regarding slower global growth could cause JPY appreciation, with a potentially negative impact on earnings indirectly if the yen appreciation sustains itself for a long period of time," said Naoki Kamiyama, chief strategist at \uc2 Nikko Asset Management\uc2 , who reviewed the survey results.               \par \par \uc2 He added that those concerns could prompt firms to cut capital spending in the short-term.               \par \par \uc2 The results of the survey come ahead of a slew of Japanese quarterly earnings next week including results from \uc2 Canon Inc\uc2  , \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2  and \uc2 Nissan Motor Co\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Most firms are, however, reporting first-quarter earnings when revisions to profit outlooks are rare. Instead companies are expected to hold off until later in year before embarking on any downward cuts.               \par \par \uc2 In contrast to hopes for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 65 percent of respondents thought no further monetary easing was warranted - a sign of fading confidence in the effectiveness of policy action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has undertaken unprecedented easing since Abe took office three-and-a-half years ago but its adoption of negative interest rates this year failed to weaken the yen and has been widely panned.                                             \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-22T00:00:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160721ec7l01crl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
