\par\par \b \uc2 No need for G20 to tame  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  markets after Brexit -IMF economist\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2016\par 21:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, July 19 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are not "disorderly" in the wake of Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and there is no need for the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  major economies to broadly agree to tame them, the International Monetary Fund's chief economist said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen sharply against the dollar since the June 23 \uc2 EU\uc2  referendum in Britain, including the pound, the euro and China's yuan, while the yen has also seen more volatility as well.\par \par \uc2 Maury Obstfeld\uc2 , the IMF's chief economist, said flexible exchange rates have acted as a buffer to shocks such as the Brexit vote, and most often provide important price signals that guide economic activity, allowing countries to pursue monetary and fiscal policies suited to their economic needs.\par \par \uc2 "So I sort of reject the premise that volatility needs to be tamed," Obstfeld said in a news conference, adding that a G20 repeat of the 1985 Plaza Accord deal to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates was "simply not something that's going to happen."\par \par \uc2 He also said another \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem excessive yen strength - Japanese officials have described \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as "nervous" since the Brexit vote - would not be "necessary or useful" in boosting Japan's moribund economy. .\par \par \uc2 While there may be a case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when markets become," Obstfeld said he has not seen such movements, including in Japan.\par \par \uc2 "We have seen some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility in recent weeks, but we would not characterize conditions in the yen market as being disorderly conditions," he said, adding that Japan was better off pursuing policies to increase wages, add fiscal stimulus and implement structural economic reforms.\par \par \uc2 "We don't view \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as being a necessary or useful part of that package," Obstfeld said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  earlier on Tuesday cut its global growth forecasts for 2016 and 2017 because of the uncertainty over trade, investment and market confidence created by the Brexit vote.\par \par \uc2 Brexit and its aftermath will be a key topic on the agenda when the G20 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meet this weekend in Chengdu, China, amid calls for measures to boost growth and ensure financial market stability.\par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  will be among the participants in the meeting. (Reporting by David Lawder; Editing by Paul Simao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-19T20:41:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160719ec7j0116z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
