\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Surge on Strong Jobs Data\b0\par\par\uc2 2,657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2016\par 23:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET Friday) \par  \par                          LAST    CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                 18146.74    250.86    1.40% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2                 4956.76     79.95    1.64% \par S&P 500               2129.90     32.00    1.53% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    15106.98   -169.26   -1.11% \par Hang Seng            20564.17   -142.75   -0.69% \par Shanghai Composite    2988.09    -28.75   -0.95% \par S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex       27126.90    -74.59   -0.27% \par Australia: S&P/ASX    5230.50      2.60    0.05% \par UK: FTSE 100          6590.64     56.85    0.87% \par  \par                  PRICE CHG  YIELD \par U.S. 2-year         -2/32   0.613 \par U.S. 5-year          1/32   0.954 \par U.S. 10-year         6/32   1.366 \par Australia 10 Year   -5/32   1.893 \par China 10 Year        0/32   2.825 \par India 10 Year       -2/32   7.387 \par Japan 10 Year        4/32  -0.287 \par German 10 Year       6/32  -0.186 \par  \par                        LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)    0.7569   0.0090 \par Yen (USD/JPY)            100.55  -0.20 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW) 1149.80  -8.45 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)   6.6881   0.0067 \par Euro (EUR/USD)           1.1052  -0.0011 \par WSJ Dollar Index          86.76  -0.26 \par  \par                LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     45.12   -0.02   -0.04% \par Brent Crude   46.55    0.15    0.32% \par Gold        1367.4     5.3     0.39% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks soared Friday as a stronger-than-expected June jobs report reassured investors that U.S. economy is growing solidly after a weak report in May. Despite the encouraging data, Treasurys finished mixed, with the 10-year yield hitting a new record low, and the dollar fell. Oil prices ended higher on bargain buying and dollar weakness, while gold prices edged lower. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's rise against the dollar has prompted speculation that Japanese authorities could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem strength in the safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are "unlikely to have a sustainable effect" because the yen is supported by global safe-haven flows. They add that the yen's rise isn't "disorderly" and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would therefore be difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to justify to G7 nations. "The worst the BOJ could do in a situation such as this would be to tempt the market into further speculation with a leaning against the wind approach," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts say. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A boost in hiring in June catapulted the S&P 500 right near its record closing high and lifted all major U.S. stock indexes back to where they were before the U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 rose 1.5% to 2129.90.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average climbed 1.4% to 18146.74, above its pre-Brexit level of 18011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nasdaq Composite gained 1.6% 4956.76.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology and consumer discretionary companies, which typically do well when investors expect economic expansion, helped lead stocks higher Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. employers added far more jobs than expected in June, providing reassurance that the U.S. economy is growing solidly after a weak report in May. The strong gains could bolster the case for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to raise interest rates later this year, but many investors brushed off that possibility and instead focused on the economic implications.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nonfarm payrolls rose by 287,000 in June, the Labor Department said Friday, the strongest month of hiring since October. Economists had estimated that nonfarm employers added 165,000 jobs last month. The May report had showed just 38,000 jobs were created, the worst reading since 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It helps alleviate fears that this economy was actually doing a U-turn and weakening," said Quincy Krosby, market strategist at Prudential Financial.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unemployment edged up to 4.9% in June from 4.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With a very strong report, it is possible the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  may raise rates later this year, according to some investors, though instability in the global economy and financial markets could still make it very difficult, they say. This general consensus that a short-term rate increase by the Fed in 2016 is off the table is also helping drive the market higher, some analysts say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economy is chugging along, creating some jobs, but the Fed is still unlikely to do anything for a while," said Erik Davidson, chief investment officer at Wells Fargo Private Bank, adding that this should encourage investors to put money into U.S. stocks and, in particular, the consumer discretionary sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is a golden age to be a consumer in the U.S.," he said, pointing to signs of a strong economy, solid jobs growth, a relatively strong dollar and the ability to borrow money cheaply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What stocks need now, analysts and investors say, is a sign that U.S. corporate earnings are improving.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Next week, second-quarter earnings season begins in earnest, with companies including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  set to report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earnings of companies in the S&P 500 are expected to contract for the fifth consecutive quarter, according to FactSet. As of June 30, analysts expected corporate profits to fall 5.3% in the second quarter from the prior year, FactSet data show. For the year, on Friday analysts expected earnings growth of about 0.5%, down from 0.6% a week ago, according to FactSet. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell Friday as investors remained cautious on the prospect of U.S. interest-rate increases despite a strong U.S. jobs report for June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar erased gains against the yen and fell against riskier emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Labor Department's June report showed that nonfarm payrolls rose by 287,000 jobs, easily topping the 165,000 gain economists had expected. Average hourly earnings, a closely watched measure, were up 2.6% compared with a year earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, unemployment rose to 4.9% in June and May's payrolls figure was revised down to a meager gain of 11,000, the weakest month of hiring since the U.S. stopped shedding jobs in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shahab Jalinoos, an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said the jobs numbers were "mediocre" when averaged out over recent months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Are today's numbers a reason to buy the dollar?" said Mr. Jalinoos. "No, they're not."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the strong data are unlikely to alter the Fed's cautious plans for raising interest rates, clouding the outlook for the dollar. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note set a record low Friday despite an encouraging employment report, underscoring the growing influence of global factors in setting benchmark U.S. interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year yield settled at 1.366%, narrowly beating out the previous record closing low of 1.367% set Tuesday. It closed at 1.387% Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields fall as bond prices rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield reached as high as 1.432% in the initial minutes after the Labor Department reported that the U.S. economy added 287,000 new jobs last month, handily beating the consensus forecast. But it quickly fell again as buyers rushed back into the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The whipsaw action at the long end of the Treasury curve provided further evidence that "there's just so much demand for bonds that it's hard to see a selloff sustained," said Jeff Greenberg, director and macro strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS Securities LLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the jobs report was widely hailed as good news by economists, it still came one month after the economy was reported to have added the fewest jobs since 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That, as well as fears of possible negative economic headlines over the coming weekend, likely also contributed to the muted reaction from the bond market, said Anthony Karydakis, chief economic strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Miller Tabak\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adding to the mixed signals sent by the financial markets, the yield on the two-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note, which is highly sensitive to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy outlook, settled at 0.613%, up from 0.589% Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed-funds futures, which are used to place bets on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, indicated a 24% likelihood of an interest-rate increase by the Fed's December meeting, compared with 19% before the jobs report, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil markets ended higher Friday due to bargain-buyers and dollar-watchers, but gains were far too small to reverse a bearish turn in the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil fell for the week, its third losing week in the last four, and prices held at a near two-month low set Thursday, weighed down by heavy supplies. Tankers and stockpiles full of gasoline and diesel are building up along the East and Gulf coasts, swamping the market and damping any pressure to buy, brokers and traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude for August delivery settled up 27 cents, or 0.6%, at $45.41 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent, the global benchmark, gained 36 cents, or 0.8%, to $46.76 a barrel on \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futures Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders who move based on momentum saw oil as a relatively cheap buy after big falls earlier this week, said Ric Navy, senior vice president for energy futures at brokerage \f28 \uc2 \f2 R.J. O'Brien & Associates\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Oil lost 4.8% Thursday and fell to its lowest settlement in nearly two months on concerns about a growing glut of gasoline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rally Friday was still largely neutralized by bloated stockpiles, traders and brokers said. It would have had to go past $46 a barrel to keep feeding itself, Mr. Navy said. But it failed at that point, and retreated to trade around unchanged for the rest of the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're on that treadmill," Mr. Navy said. "They've stopped the bleeding."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it may not have stopped for long. The weakness of the rally along with the oversupply points to a strong possibility oil will resume its fall next week, brokers and traders said. The strongest oil rally since the financial crisis has petered out in the last month, with oil steadily falling since it cracked the $50 barrier and settled at a nearly one-year high of $51.23 on June 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude finished the week down $3.58 a barrel, or 7.3%, the biggest weekly loss in more than five months. Brent lost $3.59 a barrel, or 7.1% for the week, its worst week since mid-January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Jul 2016 17:06 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Surge on -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In other commodity markets, gold prices edged lower Friday after economic data showed stronger-than-expected growth in the U.S. workforce. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par  \par AUSTRALIAN LEADER CLAIMS ELECTION VICTORY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malcolm Turnbull\f28 \uc2 \f2  declared victory Sunday, a week after cliffhanger elections were held, leaving his conservatives with a fragile grip on power amid voter angst as Australia's long resources-boom draws to an end. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ELECTION STRENGTHENS \f28 \uc2 \f2 SHINZO ABE\f28 \uc2 \f2 'S HAND IN BID TO REVISE CONSTITUTION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 's ruling coalition put in a strong showing in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan's upper house\f28 \uc2 \f2  election Sunday, strengthening Mr. Abe's hand in achieving his long-held goal of revising the constitution. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CITI'S CONSUMER BUSINESS IN CHINA: NINE YEARS OF RED INK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . began accepting deposits from Chinese households in April 2007, the bank celebrated with gongs and television crews positioned at its teller windows. Now, it's a milestone marked each year in red ink. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA CONSUMER INFLATION SOFTENS IN JUNE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer inflation in China decelerated in June as food prices increased at a slower pace, giving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more leeway to ease monetary policy in the world's second-largest economy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANGELA MERKEL\f28 \uc2 \f2  DISMISSES SUGGESTIONS BREXIT WON'T HAPPEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Chancellor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Angela Merkel\f28 \uc2 \f2  dismissed suggestions that the U.K. might not push ahead with an exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  despite last month's vote to leave. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EGYPT'S FOREIGN MINISTER TO VISIT ISRAEL FOR FIRST TIME IN ALMOST A DECADE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Egypt's foreign minister called on Israel on Sunday to take serious steps to resolve the decades-old conflict with the Palestinians, saying Cairo would support efforts to bring peace. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DIAGEO\f28 \uc2 \f2 'S INDIAN SPIRITS ARM CLAIMS 'IMPROPER TRANSACTIONS' INVOLVING FORMER CHAIRMAN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Diageo PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Indian spirits arm said it had discovered "improper transactions" involving the local company's former chairman, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vijay Mallya\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and that it would inform law-enforcement agencies and seek to recover any diverted funds. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AT LEAST 17 KILLED IN KASHMIR PROTESTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At least 17 people, including a police officer, were killed and hundreds more injured in weekend clashes between protesters and Indian police in the Muslim-majority region of Kashmir after security forces killed a separatist militant, a police official said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par G-20 SAYS INDUSTRIAL OVERCAPACITY HAS PUT DENT IN GLOBAL TRADE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Excess industrial-production capacity is a "global issue" that has depressed international commerce and harmed workers, trade ministers from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  wealthiest nations said in a statement Sunday after a weekend meeting in China. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par U.S. JOB-GROWTH REBOUND CALMS FEARS OF ECONOMIC SWOON \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A powerful rebound in hiring last month eased fears about an economic downturn as the U.S. expansion enters its eighth year, putting the nation on solid footing to absorb global shocks and market turbulence. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  COMPETITION REGULATORS PREPARING ADDITIONAL CHARGES AGAINST \f28 \uc2 \f2 GOOGLE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  competition regulators are said to be preparing to issue an additional layer of formal charges as soon as late July against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  over its behavior with its shopping service. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CONSUMER CREDIT INCREASED AT 6.18% PACE IN MAY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace of U.S. consumer borrowing picked up in May as Americans took out more student and auto loans, a sign households are growing increasingly comfortable with more debt. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TATA STEEL\f28 \uc2 \f2  IN TALKS FOR POSSIBLE EUROPEAN JOINT VENTURE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tata Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  is in talks with Germany's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thyssenkrupp\f28 \uc2 \f2  and other steel industry firms as part of the company's plan to find a better alternative for its European businesses. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THERANOS CEO BANNED FROM OPERATING LABS FOR TWO YEARS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. health regulators dealt a major blow to Theranos, banning founder \f28 \uc2 \f2 Elizabeth Holmes\f28 \uc2 \f2  from operating a blood-testing lab for at least two years and yanking approval for its California lab. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OMNI HOTELS WARNS OF DATA BREACH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Omni Hotels\f28 \uc2 \f2  warned customers that hackers stole payment-card information from the hotel chain by hacking into its point-of-sale systems and installing malicious software. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ZIKA VIRUS SUSPECTED IN UTAH DEATH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The first adult death in the continental U.S. potentially related to the Zika virus was reported in Salt Lake City. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par  \par Deutsche Boerse Considers Lowering Tender Threshold to Save Merger With \f28 \uc2 \f2 LSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Sources \par Essar Steel's Minnesota Unit Files for Chapter 11 Bankruptcy \par Bank of Mexico Chief: Recent Big Rate Moves Don't Set Precedent \par Food Giants Set Their Sights on Organic, Natural Companies \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Univision\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sues \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charter Communications\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Alleging Breach of Contract \par Nordstrom Buys Stake in Software Firm \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par 2245  NZ   Jun Electronic Card Transactions \par 2350  JPN  May Orders Received for Machinery \par 2350  JPN  Jun Money Stock, Broadly-defined Liquidity \par 0130  AUS  May Housing Finance \par 0600  JPN  Jun Preliminary Machine Tool Orders \par 0600  JPN  Jun Preliminary Machine Tool Orders \par 0800  ITA  May Industrial Production \par 0900  \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2    Q1 Quarterly sectoral accounts \par 1000  FRA  May OECD Composite Leading Indicators \par 1215  CAN  Jun Housing Starts \par 1400  US   Jun Employment Trends Index \par 2301  UK   Jun BRC-KPMG Retail Sales Monitor \par 2350  JPN  Jun Corporate Goods Price Index \par  N/A  PAK  Jun External Trade \par  N/A  FRA  OECD Environmental Performance Reviews: France report launch \par  N/A  FRA  OECD Better Policies for Sustainable Development report launch \par  N/A  EU   Eurogroup meeting of eurozone finance ministers \par  N/A  EU   U.S. / \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  trade negotiations 14th round commences \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc