\par\par \b \uc2 Strong Yen Pounds Japanese Stocks, Losses Lower in China -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 2,064 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2016\par 05:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japan led Asia stocks lower Wednesday as fears of a fragile global economy continued to unnerve investors. Nowhere was that more apparent than the safe-haven buying in the Japanese yen and the yield-hunting in Japanese and Australian government bonds. "The stronger yen is obviously hurting Japan, so Japan's [stocks are] crumbling," said Andrew Clarke, director of trading at Hong Kong-based brokerage firm Mirabaud Asia. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was down 3% in the second consecutive day of losses, stalling a brief rally over the last week and a half. Stephen Innes, a senior trader at Oanda Asia Pacific, said the yen may not strengthen past 100.50 as fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese government officials to counter the yen's rise sets in. Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 1.3%, Korea's Kospi was down 2%, and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index sank 2%. Only China remained somewhat shielded from the risk-off market sentiment, with the Shanghai Composite Index down just 0.2%.\par \par \uc2  (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0325 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Sentiment for Asian credit market is mixed Wednesday. The yield spread of Asian investment-grade bonds to comparable U.S. Treasurys have widened by 2-3 basis points, but in terms of absolute bond prices, they securities are rallying, according to a bond trader. "The [IG bonds] drop in yields [is] unable to catch up [to] the fall in U.S. Treasurys," he says. Ten-year U.S. Treasurys fell to fresh historic lows Tuesday and is now hovering at 1.345%. The trader says, however, that demand for Asian credits continues as the market is flooded with cash fueled by monetary easing from global major banks. Taking Huarong (2799.HK) as an example. Bond prices of its dollar bonds due 2026 which issued in late May, have risen for 5-6 points to 105.2 cents on the dollar in less than two months. However, Yunnan Metropolitan Construction's newly priced three-year dollar bond saw some selling pressure Wednesday, with the yield spread widening by 10 basis points due to the large issue size (US$500 million) and the ample supply of bonds from Chinese local-government financial vehicles, according to the trader. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0319 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong's purchasing managers' index fell to 45.4 in June from 47.2 in May, marking the 16th consecutive month of contraction (reading below 50) and indicates a solid deterioration in the city's business sector amid China's slowdown. Index compiler Markit says recent global developments, including the Brexit decision, add uncertainties to local businesses in the second half. Markit economist Annabel Fiddes says the latest Hong Kong PMI figures suggest that the current downturn in private sector intensified at the end of the second quarter. "Key variables such as output, new orders and purchasing activity all contracted at sharper rates amid reports of weak global economic conditions and muted spending among clients," she says. "Unless there is a marked improvement in customer demand, it seems unlikely that Hong Kong's private sector will be able to lift itself out of the current downturn." (chester.yung@wsj.com; Twitter:@chester_yung) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0316 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] More signs emerged this morning that Tokyo's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -policy hands are tied. Deputy Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroshige Seko opted to hold off on verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  despite the soaring yen, saying instead that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economic fundamentals are solid. Japan's senior finance ministry bureaucrats also kept tight-lipped about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this morning. Their silence is a curious development given that USD/JPY is now around Y100.50, well below where it was in April and May, when Japan sharply escalated verbal jawboning. Speculation could further strengthen that U.S. opposition to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , coupled with the yen's powerful, broad uptrend versus various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , may have made Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials feel there is little they could do to change the tide. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0307 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The government is preparing to guarantee 10 billion baht ($285 million) of loans from 5,000 small to mid-sized enterprises, with a preference to back loans for research and development. Kobsak Pootrakool, Vice Minister for the Office of the Prime Minister, says that business operators qualified for the support will receive backing of 1-20 million baht, depending on their nature of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "This will allow companies more access to funds while promoting [bank] lending...and inject around 40 billion baht ($1.13 billion) into the economic system," says Kobsak. "It is also expected to help boost gross domestic product growth rate by 0.05 percentage points per annum for the next five years." (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0303 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The euro will likely test $1.0939 (a 61.80% Fibonacci retracement line), if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tests its downside below $1.10, says IG Securities market analyst Junichi Ishikawa in a note. With renewed fears about the post-Brexit uncertainties inducing a euro selloff, "it's just a matter of time," to see the euro to fall below the $1.10-line, says Ishikawa. He notes the magnitude of decline in the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt yield stands out among government bonds, a sign that investors believe the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  must launch extra stimulus soon, given the Brexit shock. The euro is now changing hands at $1.1045, above the $1.1010-$1.1000, where bids are being placed. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says second-quarter consumer price index data in Australia, due July 27, "could prove critical for the near-term [\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] policy outlook." If core inflation prints as Deutsche expects (their view is that core inflation will be broadly in line with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's own forecasts, which had the core inflation rate at 1.5% over the year to the second quarter), this may be sufficient for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to lower the cash rate again by 25 basis points at the August meeting (as core inflation will, once again, be confirmed to remain materially outside the 2%-3% target band). The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday left interest rates unchanged at a record low 1.75%. (rachel.pannett@wsj.com; Twitter: @RachelPannett) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't likely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the Japanese yen's strength against the U.S. dollar, says Masashi Murata, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The bank will "take a moment and have a wait and watch stance," and will likely wait to coordinate efforts with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  before making any moves. Until then, expectations of an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are moderate unless the country's finance minister makes a statement, he says. The U.S. dollar fell below 101.00 against the yen to touch a low of Y100.80. "The strong yen is due to the weaker British pound and a market risk off sentiment," said Murata. Another trade helping the yen is that some traders had gone long on emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the yen and now have to buy the latter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market. This happened after emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose as investors are buying high-yield but risky bonds to get any yield at all as benchmark government bonds from developed countries offer a negative yield. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese shares retreat Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sets the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to its weakest level in nearly six years amid fragile sentiment, as Brexit concerns resurface in overseas markets. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index slips 0.6% to 2987.63 with nearly 800 stocks falling. Gold shares are the few gainers as investor risk appetite dwindles. China's onshore yuan trades 0.4% weaker against the U.S. dollar by far, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at its lowest level since November 2010. Zhongjin Gold (600489.SH) and Western Gold (601069.SH) rally 9.1% and 4.9%, respectively. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Vanke\f28 \uc2 \f2  (000002.SZ) ceases to trade limit down, falling a mere 1.5% after climbing 4.5% at the start of Wednesday's trade. The company said in a filing last night that its shareholder \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baoneng Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a private insurer, bought 75.3 million of Vanke's A-shares Tuesday, bring its total holding to nearly 24.97%. The Shenzhen Composite Index edges down 0.4% at 1997.71 and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2 -style ChiNext board slips 0.6% at 2233.45. (Yifan.xie@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0219 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Benchmark 10-year government debt yields from Japan and Australia in Asia Pacific fell to fresh historic lows on Wednesday morning, following the trends seen for U.S. treasurys and the debt of other western countries. This comes amid persistent uncertainty surrounding the global economy and the political fallout from Britain's decision vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which is boosting demand for haven assets. The 10-year debt of Japan is now hovering at minus 0.269% while the 10-year debt of Australia is at 1.861%, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Yields of Thai government debt also fall. Yields drop when bonds prices rise. Markets in several countries in Asia, including Singapore, Malaysia, Philippines and Indonesia, were closed due to public holidays. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the rally in global government bonds doesn't come as a surprise. "With policy effectiveness very much in doubt, bond yields can go lower still. What is quite different about this rally, in contrast to previous ones, is the lack of confidence in the ability of the authorities, political or financial, to come to the rescue. So that means the 'low for long' mantra is much more valid this time around," SocGen says. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Jul 2016 23:42 ET \par Strong Yen Pounds Japanese Stocks, Losses Lower -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0210 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/JPY Intraday: Downside prevails. The pair has crossed below the 101.00 level while trading around the lower Bollinger band as those bands were widening, suggesting a further acceleration to the downside. The intraday relative strength index is headed below the over-sold level of 30, indicating continued downward momentum for the pair. The present down-leg could bring the pair down to 100.40 and 99.90 (supports seen on the great down-day on June 24). Key resistance has been lowered to 101.75. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0209 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/USD Intraday: Testing 1.1045. Currently the pair continues on its down-leg started overnight and shows no upward momentum. Intraday technical indicators are negatively oriented, with the 20-period moving average staying far below the 50-period one, and the relative strength index submerging below the over-sold level of 30. The pair is testing the immediate downside support at 1.1045, a break of which could lead to a further drop toward 1.1025 (around the low of June 30). Key resistance is located at 1.1120. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 05, 2016 23:42 ET (03:42 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160706ec760002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
