\par\par \b \uc2 EXCLUSIVE-Banks saw unprecedented step up in market supervision around UK vote\b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2016\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story moved on Monday without change to text)\par \par \uc2 * Cbanks wanted detailed updates every six hours - sources\par \par \uc2 * Seen as sign of level of concern over impact of Brexit\par \par \uc2 * BoE, others, pledged more market supervision around vote\par \par \uc2 By Patrick Graham\par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 4 (Reuters) - Central banks raised oversight of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to an unprecedented degree around Britain's shock vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , demanding detailed updates from major trading desks every six hours throughout last week, industry sources said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 One senior banker with a major global bank said the calls, never before conducted as often or consistently, had been seen as a sign that officials were worried an "Out" vote could trigger the sort of financial sector problems not seen since the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in 2008.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  all declined to comment on the calls, their conduct, content and aims.\par \par \uc2 But some officials pointed to a general promise by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and others to increase supervision of markets around the vote and said the calls were chiefly evidence of greater - and so far successful - coordination of regulatory efforts by the world's big central banks.\par \par \uc2 The BoE said ahead of the vote it could ask banks for daily checks on their funding levels if need be. It has also become the norm for \uc2 the ECB\uc2  to check more regularly in times of stress with banks about short-term liquidity or the situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or derivatives markets.\par \par \uc2 "These calls have been going on. They are reflective, I think, of a more systematic and modern approach from the regulators," said one industry source.\par \par \uc2 "If they make these things regular and keep to a pro forma then it is easier to compare notes with other central banks on what is happening. You can infer also that there is an awful lot more conference calling going on between the central banks."\par \par \uc2 A second source, the senior banker, said he had been present on a handful of the calls to his bank, held consistently every six hours during major market business hours since the vote on June 23.\par \par \uc2 "There are all sorts of regulators present on these calls," he said. "Typically they want to know about liquidity volumes, they want to know of any clients in stress, any margin calls, certain points in the market that may be of stress. I assume they are holding them with other banks, too."\par \par \uc2 He said in his experience of the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, stretching back to the 1990s, regulators had never taken such action.\par \par \uc2 While central banks were deeply involved with the plumbing of the banking sector in the worst moments of the 2008 crisis, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other major over-the-counter markets have not demanded that level of supervision in the past decade.\par \par \uc2 "Classically, we would get this kind of call every so often from one individual in the markets section of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and he would go away and write up a report on the conversation and it would get passed up the line," the senior banker said.\par \par \uc2 "They want to know a granularity of what's happening that we have not seen before. It shows the sensitivity and the interest to not have a Lehman-type event."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 There was much speculation ahead of the vote that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  might have to step in, potentially with the support, direct or otherwise, of other central banks, to provide a bid in the market that would steady sterling as it fell.\par \par \uc2 In the event, only the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to oppose more gains for the franc, while the BoE has confirmed it did not, even as sterling dived almost 18 cents against the dollar on June 24.\par \par \uc2 Traders reported bids from Japanese banks on the night that consistently opposed gains for the yen past 102 per dollar, but data last week showed the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One member of \uc2 the ECB\uc2 's Governing Council, asking not to be named, said that they and other central banks had been prepared for a lot more volatility than materialised and that cooperation with the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  had been "exemplary".\par \par \uc2 He said they got regular updates and that BoE Governor \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  personally was in touch many times. (Additional reporting by Balazs Koranyi in FRANKFURT, David Milliken in LONDON, and Tim Ahmann in WASHINGTON; Editing by Alison Williams)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-8T05:00:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160705ec750086j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
