\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Japan's Abe pledges broad policy support to weather Brexit shock\b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2016\par 11:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds Abe adviser, ex-BOJ official)\par \par \uc2 * Abe urges BOJ to pump ample funds into markets\par \par \uc2 * Finmin Aso told to keep close watch on yen moves\par \par \uc2 * Retail sales fall more than expected in May\par \par \uc2 * Abe adviser warns speculators of solo \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Govt-BOJ meeting unlikely to lead to action-analysts\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto and Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 29 (Reuters) - Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  pledged on Wednesday to use all available policy tools to keep the wheels of the economy turning as financial markets were gripped by uncertainty in the wake of Britain's shock vote to exit the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen's spike following the referendum has kept Japanese policymakers on edge as a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens to put more pressure on the export-reliant economy, already reeling from weak demand at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 Retail sales fell more than expected in May, data showed earlier in the day, keeping policymakers under pressure to roll out more stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "Consumer spending has been stagnant and the trend is likely to continue for a while due to sluggish growth in wages," said Hidenobu Tokuda, senior economist at \uc2 Mizuho Research Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a meeting to discuss post-Brexit market developments, Abe urged \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (BOJ) Governor Haruhiko Kuroda to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides ample funds to the market to prevent any credit squeeze.\par \par \uc2 "A sense of uncertainty and worry about risks remain in the markets," Abe told the meeting, the second between the government and the BOJ since the June 23 referendum.\par \par \uc2 The premier also called on Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  to keep a close watch on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and respond flexibly to market developments in coordination with \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  economies.\par \par \uc2 Abe is expected to hold similar meetings regularly as Tokyo looks to put safeguards in place against potential instability in financial markets after Britain's messy \uc2 EU\uc2  divorce.\par \par \uc2 The rush of money to safe havens such as the yen has been stoked by fear Brexit would adversely affect Britain's economy and undermine already fragile growth in the \uc2 EU\uc2 , causing more disruption in global investment and trade.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 , BOJ STIMULUS\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen after the Brexit vote drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-year highs, but the risks of a costly failure may dissuade policymakers from matching their words with action.\par \par \uc2 Still, Masahiko Shibayama, an adviser to the premier, said unilateral yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be ruled out to counter excess speculation, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stand ready to expand its already massive monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "We won't hesitate to take action against excess speculation," he told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The dollar firmed to 102.63 yen on Wednesday, moving away from a 2-1/2-year low of 99.00 touched on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Former top BOJ economist \uc2 Hideo Hayakawa\uc2  told reporters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be forced into further easing at the July 28-29 policy meeting given prices are undershooting its forecasts. However, he shrugged off speculation about the BOJ holding an extra policy meeting before that.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is wary of rushing into expanding its monetary stimulus, preferring to wait and see if the market turmoil lasts long enough to threaten Japan's economic recovery, sources say.\par \par \uc2 Hence, analysts see Abe's meetings as more a symbolic move to show the public the government is doing what it can to contain damage ahead of a July 10 upper house election in Japan.\par \par \uc2 "There's not a lot of policy tools left for authorities to reboot the economy. Therefore, Abe has no choice but hold meetings one after another at least until the July election," said Yasuji Yajima, chief economist at NLI Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "If the yen spikes beyond 100 to the dollar, authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but I doubt whether it could have a lasting impact," he said.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy expanded at the fastest pace in a year in the first quarter but analysts say growth will not pick up much for the rest of this year as slow wage gains weigh on consumption. External headwinds, such as weak emerging market demand and the yen's gains, also cloud the outlook for exports.\par \par \uc2 Worried about the additional hit from Brexit, the government is willing to spend at least 10 trillion yen ($97.7 billion) on a stimulus package, sources have told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 102.3300 yen) (Additional reporting by Kaori Kaneko; Editing by Chang-Ran Kim & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-29T10:22:31.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160629ec6t0096n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
