\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Abe adviser: won't rule out solo yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2016\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government won't hesitate to respond to excess speculation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or conduct unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to safeguard the export-reliant economy, an adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Masahiko Shibayama said if markets became excessively volatile, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could hold an emergency meeting to ease policy further although the scope for such easing remains limited, given interest rates are already in negative territory.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Britain's shock vote to exit the \uc2 European Union\uc2  last week caused a spike in the yen, prompting Japanese policymakers to warn investors against pushing safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too high, which would deal a blow to exports and policy efforts to defeat deflation.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "We must be on our guard against speculative moves and won't hesitate to take action against excess speculation," Shibayama, Special Adviser to the Prime Minister, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "Our basic stance is to cooperate with other countries. But that doesn't mean that solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be ruled out."\par \par \par \par \uc2 While policymakers have threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants remain sceptical about Tokyo's appetite to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets given strong opposition from Washington.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Shibayama described the BOJ's negative rate policy as "powerful medicine", suggesting it might be hard for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates deeper into negative territory.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The negative rates policy drew criticism from banks for confusing rather than calming markets.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "It caused a bigger than expected impact. Taking that into account, I want the BOJ to carefully consider what it can do" when easing policy further, he said.   (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Sam Holmes)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-2T08:20:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160629ec6t00efh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
