\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rally as Brexit Worries Ease\b0\par\par\uc2 2,498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2016\par 23:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS AT A GLANCE\par \par \uc2 (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      LAST     CHANGE  % CHG \par DJIA                 17694.7  284.96  1.64% \par  \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2               4779.25  87.38   1.86% \par S&P 500              2070.77  34.68   1.70% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    15566.8  243.69  1.59% \par  \par  Hang Seng           20436.1  263.66  1.31% \par Shanghai Composite   2931.59  19.03   0.65% \par S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex       26740.4  215.84  0.81% \par Australia: S&P/ASX   5142.4   39.1    0.77% \par UK: FTSE 100         6360.06  219.67  3.58% \par  \par  \par                     PRICE CHG  YIELD% \par U.S. 2 Year          -1/32     0.637 \par U.S. 5 Year          -7/32     1.058 \par U.S. 10 Year         -14/32    1.515 \par Australia 10 Year    -5/32     2.012 \par China 10 Year        1/32      2.896 \par India 10 Year       0/32       7.444 \par Japan 10 Year        3/32      -0.236 \par German 10 Year       5/32      -0.124 \par  \par  \par                           LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)     0.7448     0.0062 \par Yen (USD/JPY)             102.8      0.05 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)   1154.32    -12.75 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)    6.6352     -0.0168 \par Euro (EUR/USD)            1.1124     0.0058 \par WSJ Dollar Index          86.66      -0.48 \par  \par  \par               LAST    CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     49.52   1.67    3.49% \par Brent Crude   50.89   1.63    3.31% \par Gold          1321.8  3.9     0.30% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNAPSHOT:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rallied, government bond yields edged higher and the dollar fell against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as worries about the U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  appeared to ease. Gold prices rose as continuing concerns over Brexit sent investors into the safe-haven metal. Oil prices settled higher, lifted by a broad-based market recovery, signs of shrinking stockpiles and the threat of a strike in Norway.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPENING CALL:\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government apparently finds it hard to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets because of lack of international support for it, leaving the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  no choice but to further ease monetary policy to counter the yen's strength, says BNP Paribas chief Japan economist Ryutaro Kono. "I previously thought that there was a 50% chance of additional easing steps at a planned policy meeting at the end of July, but I now think it is around 80%," he says. "If it doesn't move, there's a risk that the yen's appreciation will accelerate." Additional steps will likely center on cutting a deposit rate on yen reserves of commercial banks to minus 0.3% from the current minus 0.1%, or more sharply if the yen strengthens further, he says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EQUITIES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two of the best days for stocks this year helped undo two of the worst, turning major U.S. indexes positive for 2016.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rallied as worries about the U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  appeared to ease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders described a sense of equilibrium returning to the market. While gains in the previous session appeared to be driven predominantly by short-covering, Wednesday's rally appeared to have been lent some momentum by new buyers entering the fray, said Michael Antonelli, equity sales trader at Robert W. Baird.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global shares and sterling had initially plunged after Thursday's Brexit vote, as investors feared that the U.K.'s departure from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  would slow global economic growth and extend the period of low interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But markets are showing signs of stemming those losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial stocks in the S&P 500, which fell almost 10% in the two sessions after the U.K. referendum results were released, rose.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are gauging the risks from Brexit to be lower than during the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2  bankruptcy that unleashed the financial crisis in 2008 and during the height of the European debt crisis, said Andrew Sheets, chief cross-asset strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are starting to take stock that this is more country specific and is not affecting markets everywhere," he said. "So far, it doesn't appear to be a big risk to global growth."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors said they are betting that the impact of the U.K. vote will be limited, especially if economic data from the U.S. and China show growth. U.S. consumer spending picked up in May from a month earlier, the Commerce Department said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Of course, the volatility out of Europe makes us worried, but so far the real economic impact is difficult to gauge," said Mike Baele, senior portfolio manager at U.S. Bank Wealth Management, which oversees $133 billion in assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, shares rose across the board, with the Stoxx Europe 600 advancing 3.1% and France's CAC 40 rising 2.6%. London's FTSE 100, made up of companies that earn three-quarters of their revenues outside the U.K., gained 3.6%, erasing all its post-Brexit losses. The more U.K.-focused FTSE 250 also rallied, climbing 3.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares across Asia rallied Wednesday as worries about the U.K.'s decision to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  abated and investors began buying up badly beaten sectors, including financials.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as worries subsided about the immediate effects of a U.K. vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors sought refuge in the dollar and other safe haven assets in the days after June 23, when a majority of U.K. citizens voted in favor of a break with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a countrywide referendum.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While many investors remain uncertain about how the separation will effect global growth, the lack of any fresh news has given traders an opportunity to take profits on recent sharp \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves, said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in a note to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, "the overall level of uncertainty surrounding the Brexit and what it will mean for the U.K., the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  and broader global financial markets remains at historic levels," Mr. Esiner said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are also squaring away positions as the second quarter comes to a close, and ahead of a long weekend in the U.S., analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields edged higher, as investors continued to ease back into riskier assets following their initial flight to safety in the aftermath of the U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors sold riskier assets such as stocks and piled into haven government bonds on Friday and Monday following the Brexit vote, which took many analysts off guard and heightened anxieties about an already fragile global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk appetite returned to some degree. Still, selling in the bond market has been mild, a sign many investors remain cautious due to the uncertainties surrounding the political and economic fallout from Brexit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even before the Brexit vote, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields had fallen sharply this year, reflecting strong demand amid tepid global growth, low inflation, a cautious \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  and continued unconventional monetary stimulus in Japan and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Driven partly by the negative-interest-rate policies in Japan and Europe, a pool of negative-yielding bonds overseas has expanded dramatically, dragging down \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields, which look attractive to investors by comparison.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the consequences of the U.K. referendum are much lower expectations that the Fed will raise interest rates this year and even some speculation that it may need to cut rates if an economic slowdown abroad spills across U.S. borders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal-funds futures -- a popular tool for hedge funds and money managers to place bets on the Fed's future policy moves -- showed that there was only a 21% probability the Fed will raise interest rates by its December policy meeting and a 1% chance that it will cut rates by that time, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMODITIES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. oil prices made their largest gains in one session since early April as falling U.S. stockpiles and a broader market rebound sent traders back into crude and many other commodities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude stockpiles fell by 4.1 million barrels in the week ended Friday, double what analysts had forecast in a survey by The Wall Street Journal. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Energy Information Administration\f28 \uc2 \f2  also reported declines in U.S. production, in stockpiles at the key crude delivery hub of Cushing, Okla., and in distillate stockpiles, which include heating oil and diesel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market has been on its strongest rally since the financial crisis -- nearly doubling since a decade low in February -- as traders bet that supply outages and falling production will help resolve a longstanding glut. Analysts and brokers warned that resolution is still far off and that last week's drawdown still left stockpiles near records. But they also said it was clear traders weren't heading those warnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil's advance accelerated late Tuesday when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Petroleum Institute\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimated a 3.9 million barrel draw in crude stocks. The EIA confirmed that fall in crude and reported that total commercial oil stockpiles fell by 1 million barrels to 1.372 billion barrels, the sixth drawdown in the last eight weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But oil has also been helped by a broad-based market recovery from last week's Brexit vote, brokers and analysts said. The U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  had shocked investors into selling off riskier assets and buying traditional haven assets including the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices rose as continued concerns over Britain leaving the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  sent investors into the safe-haven metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S HEADLINES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed Clears 31 of 33 Big Banks for Capital Returns\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The largest U.S. banks got permission from regulators to return profits to investors, but the U.S. banking units of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Santander\f28 \uc2 \f2  were held back again as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  released the final results of its 2016 "stress tests."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GE Finance Arm Is No Longer Deemed 'Too Big to Fail'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. regulators rescinded stricter oversight of \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2 's finance arm, \f28 \uc2 \f2 GE Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 , after saying the company had made changes that significantly reduced its threat to U.S. financial stability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monsanto\f28 \uc2 \f2  Mum on Bayer Deal as Profit, Sales Slip\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 29 Jun 2016 17:32 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rally as -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monsanto's chief executive gave no formal update on a $62 billion takeover proposal from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bayer\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as the biotech-seed company said its earnings fell more than Wall Street expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Airbnb\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans Dual Stock Sales to Push Off IPO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Airbnb\f28 \uc2 \f2  lined up investors for a new funding round and an employee stock sale that values the room-rental website at up to $30 billion and helps defer an initial public offering, said people familiar with the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toyota Recalls 1.4 Million Cars for New Air-Bag Defect\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest recall by the world's largest auto maker isn't related to faulty air-bag inflaters made by Japan's Takata that have prompted widespread safety actions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sells $14 Billion in Bonds as Investment Grade Weathers Brexit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  sold $14 billion of bonds, underscoring the strength of the investment-grade corporate bond market in the aftermath of the U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GM\f28 \uc2 \f2 's U.S. Market Share Shrinks as Auto Sales Peak\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. market share for the nation's largest car maker has tumbled to a more than three decade low amid strong demand as its dealers say they are saddled with too many cars and too few pickups and SUVs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visa Files Counterclaim to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart\f28 \uc2 \f2  in PIN Debit Card Lawsuit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visa\f28 \uc2 \f2  fired back at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart Stores\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a legal dispute over letting customers authorize a debit-card transaction with a signature, saying that the retailer secretly configured its payment terminals so only personal identification numbers could be used.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Facebook Wins Belgian Court Appeal Over Data Collection\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2  won its appeal against Belgium's privacy watchdog, allowing the U.S. tech company to restore its ability to collect information about internet users in the country not registered with the social-media site.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apollo Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  Nears Deal to Buy Diamond Resorts\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Puerto Rico's Drastic Population Loss Deepens Its Economic Crisis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nielsen Unveils Streaming Ratings for 'Orange is the New Black,' 'Seinfeld'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ireland Ready if Companies Want to Relocate After Brexit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. Faces Pressure to Put New EU Trading Rules in Place After Brexit Vote\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart Deal With \f28 \uc2 \f2 JD.com\f28 \uc2 \f2  Includes Incentives for Retailer to Raise Stake\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S CALENDAR:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (All times GMT, followed by country and event)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2245 NZ May Building Consents Issued\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2300 SKA May Industrial Production Index\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2300 SKA May Service Industry Activity Index\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2350 JPN May Preliminary Industrial Production\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0100 NZ Jun ANZ Business Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0130 AUS May Financial Aggregates, incl 