\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Swings Hit Central Banks -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chelsey Dulaney and Corrie Driebusch   \par \uc2 1,294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2016\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  has set off a fresh round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressures in the world's largest economies, further complicating efforts by central banks to spur growth.\par \par \uc2 The pound hit a three-decade low on Monday, and both \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  cut their ratings on the U.K., saying that last week's vote raises risks to the country's economy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Japanese yen, Swiss franc and U.S. dollar posted further gains, as market turmoil resumed after the weekend and sent investors in search of havens. Government bonds also benefited from the flight from risk, with the yield on the 10-year British bond falling below 1% for the first time, as the rout in U.S. and European stocks deepened.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, in particular, pose risks for businesses and in turn for economies that have posted lackluster performance.\par \par \uc2 The resurgent yen and franc are putting renewed pressure on companies in Japan and Switzerland. Meanwhile, U.S. companies that had benefited from a weakening dollar this year face a bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related stress as the second-quarter earnings season looms.\par \par \uc2 Stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to make a country's exporters less competitive as the effective price of their goods goes up. They also tamp down inflation as import prices fall, frustrating outcomes for central banks in Japan, Europe and the U.S. that are trying to calibrate policies to boost growth and inflation. The moves could tempt central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or modify policies to limit the upward pressure.\par \par \uc2 "Policy makers are unlikely to sit idly by while their strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derails any economic progress that they've made," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at international-payments firm \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Central banks would be justified in stepping in."\par \par \uc2 The problem is \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't all weaken at once. The Swiss have been trying to push the franc down against the euro. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has nodded to the benefits of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it lowers interest rates into negative territory and expands its bond-buying programs. Japan has tried to weaken the yen against the dollar. And \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have cited the stronger dollar as an impediment to growth.\par \par \uc2 All are showing little success, and investors have raised concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tools for influencing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values are losing their effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 That situation is most evident in Japan, where the yen has appreciated sharply this year even as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  adopted negative interest rates. The yen had already strengthened by more than 10% against the U.S. dollar in 2016 before the U.K. voted Thursday to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2 , known as Brexit. Since late Thursday, the dollar weakened below Yen100 for the first time since late 2013. The yen is up 18% for the year. Late Monday in New York, the dollar bought Yen101.99, from Yen102.20 Friday.\par \par \uc2 "What the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to decide is whether they think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is helpful," said Daragh Maher, head of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 HSBC Holdings PLC\uc2 . "They want to be reasonably confident that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be more helpful than not. They haven't reached that point."\par \par \uc2 The yen's gains have put pressure on Japanese stocks, as investors worry that the country's export-dependent companies will become less competitive. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 7.9% Friday after the British result was announced. It rose 2.4% Monday, but is down 20% this year.\par \par \uc2 Technology exporters such as \uc2 Canon Inc\uc2 . have already warned that the yen's surge could eat into earnings. \uc2 Canon\uc2 , the maker of digital cameras and office printers, said each one-yen appreciation against the dollar reduces its annual operating profit by the equivalent of about $38 million.\par \par \uc2 The potential threat to already weak Japanese exports is adding pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to step in to control the yen's rise. Athanasios Vamvakidis, \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 's head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in Europe, expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to introduce new easing at its next policy meeting, which is scheduled for late July.\par \par \uc2 "Given the substantial risks that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will not be able to meet its inflation target, you want a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that's somewhat undervalued," said Mr. Vamvakidis. "Further strength in the yen would be a big problem."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is having similar trouble controlling the rise in the franc. It said Friday that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the most overt early reaction by the world's monetary authorities to the British vote. The franc weakened against both the euro and dollar over the past two sessions but remains up for the year.\par \par \uc2 "In terms of redirecting the market from migrating to the Swiss franc, I think it will have a marginal effect at best," said Peter Rosenstreich, chief market strategist at Swissquote Bank.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar's strength is posing a renewed threat to U.S. companies' profits as the second quarter draws to a close. The WSJ Dollar Index, which measures the greenback against 16 other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had fallen 5.6% this year before the U.K. decision. Over the past two days, the dollar index has rebounded 2.7%.\par \par \uc2 Analysts had expected U.S. corporations in the S&P 500 to post slight earnings growth in 2016 after a 6.6% contraction in the first quarter and an expected decline in the second quarter, which ends this week, according to FactSet. But the dollar's surge in the wake of the referendum is causing investors to re-evaluate those assumptions.\par \par \uc2 "If we were expecting earnings growth slightly positive for the year, Brexit is enough to move the dial down to flat or negative for earnings growth," said Nathan Thooft, head of asset allocation at John Hancock Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 The market turmoil and strength of the dollar are expected to keep the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's interest-raising efforts on hold. The Fed doesn't target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, but officials have repeatedly cited the dollar's strength as a drag on growth. Markets expect little chance of a rate increase this year and in fact are pricing in small odds of a rate cut. Higher rates would make the dollar more attractive to investors.\par \par \uc2 One group of potential beneficiaries of the recent moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are British exporters. With sterling slumping -- trading at $1.3225 late in New York on Monday -- British goods will become significantly cheaper to overseas consumers. That has helped support the shares of big U.K. companies despite the market tumult. The U.K.'s FTSE 100 Index, which includes many British exporters, has fallen 5.6% over the past two sessions, compared with the 11% decline for the broader Stoxx Europe 600 index.\par \par \uc2 Yet shares of U.K. real-estate companies, banks and airlines have been battered, as investors worry that a Brexit would dent investment and spending in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 "A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should by and large help British exporters. It should help raise inflation, which has consistently run below the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's target," said Mr. Esiner of \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "But it does cause investors to pause and rethink investments in the U.K."\par \par \uc2 Many analysts said the potential slowdown in the U.K. economy could prompt the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to ramp up stimulus in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 --Brian Blackstone and Juro Osawa contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chelsey Dulaney at Chelsey.Dulaney@wsj.com and Corrie Driebusch at corrie.driebusch@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 28, 2016 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160628ec6s000ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
