\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-BOJ can stand pat if Brexit turmoil proves temporary: ex-executive Momma\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2016\par 06:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has right to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Momma\par \par \uc2 * Adds BOJ can hold emergency meeting "any time"\par \par \uc2 * Boosting risky asset buying most likely next step\par \par \uc2 * Don't see immediate need to ease policy-Momma (Adds details, context)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Yoshifumi Takemoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 27 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  can hold off on expanding monetary stimulus if market turbulence caused by Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  proves temporary, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  executive said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stem sharp yen rises based on a shared understanding among G7 and G20 nations that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are undesirable, said Kazuo Momma, who was the BOJ's executive director overseeing international affairs until May.\par \par \uc2 "You can't conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  easily," Momma said, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to convince sceptics of yen-selling action such as the United States.\par \par \uc2 "But there's a shared understanding (among G7 and G20 nations) that if necessary, countries can step into the market," Momma, who has insight into G7 negotiations on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economic policies, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they see yen rises as excessive, though market players doubt whether Tokyo will step in given strong opposition from Washington.\par \par \uc2 The yen's spike also adds pressure on the BOJ to expand its massive stimulus at a rate review on July 28-29. Some investors speculate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ease if it holds an emergency meeting before the scheduled July gathering.\par \par \uc2 RISKY ASSET BUYING NEXT OPTION\par \par \uc2 Momma, who also served as the top BOJ bureaucrat on monetary policy, said the bank can hold an emergency meeting "any time," though whether to do so depends on how much the market rout damages Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2 "If it's a short-term shock, the BOJ doesn't need to act immediately. If it turns into something that damages Japan's long-term positive cycle, it's a different question," he said.\par \par \uc2 If the BOJ were to expand stimulus, increasing purchases of risky assets may be an easier option than topping up government bond buying or pushing interest rates deeper into negative territory, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Accelerating the pace (of bond purchases) isn't impossible but not easy," Momma said. "Deepening negative rates may be somewhat more likely. But there is strong resistance over negative rates even among some BOJ board members," he said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ stunned markets in January by adding negative rates to its massive asset-buying programme, under which it buys government bonds and risky assets to pump money into the economy at an annual pace of 80 trillion yen ($787 billion).\par \par \uc2 But three years of aggressive money printing has failed to accelerate inflation toward the BOJ's 2 percent target, with core consumer prices down 0.3 percent in the year to April.\par \par \uc2 Momma said the BOJ shouldn't fret over monthly fluctuations in prices and instead focus on whether economic conditions are falling in place for inflation to accelerate long-term.\par \par \uc2 "Japan's economy is gradually improving and job conditions are near full employment," Momma said. "I don't see a strong need for the BOJ to ease immediately."\par \par \uc2 The yen briefly soared above the key threshold of 100 to the dollar on Friday as investors hoarded the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Brexit vote, adding to headaches for policymakers worried about the effect a strong yen could have on exports. ($1 = 101.6300 yen) (Editing by Chris Gallagher and Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-30T04:54:46.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160627ec6r008hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
