\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  may struggle to match threats of  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  with action\b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2016\par 13:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * "Brexit" vote briefly drove yen past 100 per dollar\par \par \uc2 * Tokyo frets at damage strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could do to exports\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  wary of rushing to expand stimulus - sources\par \par \uc2 * Some policymakers doubt solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective\par \par \uc2 * Yen-selling could exacerbate global concerns of dollar shortage\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen after Britain's vote for "Brexit" drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-year highs, but the risks of a costly failure may dissuade policymakers from matching words with action.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (BOJ) is also wary of rushing into expanding its monetary stimulus, preferring to wait to see if the market turmoil triggered by Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  lasts long enough to threaten Japan's economic recovery, according to sources familiar with its thinking.\par \par \uc2 "When markets are moving on factors unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's doubtful whether any action - be it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or more easing - would be effective," one of the sources said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers, who have warned for months that they could act to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, watched helplessly on Friday as the unfolding result of Britain's referendum took the yen easily above the key threshold of 100 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 One official said he was too shocked to gather his thoughts as the result, which wrongfooted markets that were positioned for a "remain" vote, happened "all of a sudden".\par \par \uc2 Wary of the damage the yen's spike inflicts on exports, Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  on Monday instructed Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  to watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "ever more closely" and take steps if necessary.\par \par \uc2 The yen is sought as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of market stress due to Japan's huge net foreign assets and current account surplus - factors that encourage flows into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during bouts of risk aversion as Japanese firms repatriate cash.\par \par \uc2 Senior government and BOJ officials have met several times, including on Saturday, to discuss how to deal with the market turbulence - largely as a signal to markets that they are gearing up for action.\par \par \uc2 With the dollar having eased back to around 102 yen, however, Japanese authorities are hesitant to conduct solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes a sustained rise back above 100, say government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials with knowledge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  affairs.\par \par \uc2 "At this moment, the impact turned out to be somewhat smaller than we thought," said one official.\par \par \uc2 "We need to watch movements from now on," the official said, adding that Japanese authorities probably needed more clarity on where markets were heading before deciding whether to act.\par \par \uc2 CAUGHT IN A BIND\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials are caught in a bind. The effect of jawboning will wane unless Tokyo backs up the talk with deeds. But there are plenty of reasons to hold off from acting.\par \par \uc2 By stepping in to sell the yen, Tokyo risks infuriating Washington, which has warned against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could be seen as seeking to give Japanese companies a competitive advantage.\par \par \uc2 "It's very difficult to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government," said Seki Obata, a former finance ministry official who is now associate professor at Keio Business School.\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese government actually wants to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but all it can do is talk, so it's inflating its lip service."\par \par \uc2 There is also no guarantee solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could turn the market's tide in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's favour, particularly with the yen being driven up by factors beyond Tokyo's control.\par \par \uc2 The key worry among global policymakers is the possibility Brexit might trigger a shortage of dollars, which are in high demand from banks and investors in times of uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 If Japan were to sell yen to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it would be mopping up dollars from the market - a move that could exacerbate any shortage of dollars, one of the sources said.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  solo won't work," the source said. "It would be going against the global trend."\par \par \uc2 Some market players speculate that BOJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , a former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, may attempt to shock markets into selling the yen by further expanding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive money-printing programme.\par \par \uc2 With markets already predicting more monetary easing at its scheduled meeting in late July, they say Kuroda could spring a timing surprise by calling an emergency meeting in coming days.\par \par \uc2 But Kazuo Momma, a former top BOJ official who oversaw the bank's monetary policy drafting, said he did not expect the BOJ to hold an emergency meeting soon.\par \par \uc2 "If needed the BOJ can hold an emergency meeting any time," Momma said. "But Japan's economy is gradually improving and job conditions are near full employment. I don't see a strong need for the BOJ to ease immediately." (Additional reporting by Minami Funakoshi, Stanley White, Kaori Kaneko, Sumio Ito and Yoshifumi Takemoto; Editing by Alex Richardson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-27T12:42:20.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160627ec6r00loo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
