\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Japan could use British fallout as spur for action\b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2016\par 06:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Quentin Webb\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, June 27 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Japan could use the fallout from Britain's referendum as a spur to further action. While direct economic ties to the United Kingdom are limited, the resulting market ructions are once again undermining the country's stuttering economic revival. Responses could include bolder reform, more state spending, looser monetary policy, and even direct selling of the yen.\par \par \uc2 Japan's volatile stock market and the yen's appeal in turbulent times leave it exposed when global investors get jumpy. Britain's vote to quit the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is causing new shockwaves. The benchmark Topix stock index crashed more than 7 percent on June 24, and the yen surged, briefly breaching 100 per dollar. This is painful for an export-heavy corporate sector. A weak yen was central to the early success of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's "Abenomics".\par \par \uc2 The first response has been to talk tough. Abe has ordered his finance chief to take "various, aggressive responses" to keep markets stable. Jaw-boning may not lead to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though: moving unilaterally would be unpopular with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international partners, and less effective than a coordinated blitz alongside the Americans and others.\par \par \uc2 That said, a sense of calamity can aid reformers. It could help Abe secure a bigger victory in July 10's upper-house elections, strengthening his push for reforms such as overhauling Japan's outdated labour market. It also bolsters the case for increased fiscal stimulus. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economists reckon this year's "supplementary budget" might now total 6 to 8 trillion yen ($59 billion to $79 billion), rather than the previously mooted 5 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 What about the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 ? The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  already buys an epic 80 trillion yen's worth of government bonds each year, plus some stocks via exchange-traded funds. It also now charges banks for some deposits. It could expand some or all of these measures, for example, buying corporate debt, hoovering up more ETFs, or lending to banks at negative rates via its "loan support" scheme.\par \par \uc2 However, the BOJ's effectiveness and firepower are already in question, with negative rates in particular proving deeply controversial. If the \uc2 EU\uc2  splinters further, that would mean heightened market turmoil, and a bigger threat to global growth. Amid so much uncertainty, it would be wise for Japan to keep some of its powder dry.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/qtwebb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/qtwebb}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - Japan's government spokesman said on June 27 it was important to take all possible measures to ensure market stability with a close eye on short-term moves in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 - Deputy Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroshige Seko told a news conference that the government would closely monitor the impact of Brexit on Japan's real economy in the medium and long terms. He said the government and \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  would meet at appropriate times as needed.\par \par \uc2 - Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said he had instructed Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  to watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "ever more closely" and take steps if necessary, in the wake of Britain's historic vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - By 12.15 p.m. Tokyo time (0315 GMT) on June 27, the yen stood at 101.88 per dollar. The Topix stock index, which had dropped more than 7 percent in the previous trading session, recovered slightly to stand 0.9 percent higher at 1,215.58.\par \par \uc2 - Reuters: Japan PM Abe instructs finmin to take \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps as needed\par \par \uc2 - Reuters: Japan govt spokesman: Important to take steps to ensure market stability\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-30T04:39:17.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160627ec6r005mt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
