\par\par \b \uc2 EUR/GBP Should Continue It's Advance Toward 0.8320 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2016\par 02:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0041 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The EUR/GBP has started the week with a bullish gap above 0.8200 and is expected to continue its advance. The 20-period moving average stays above the 50-period one and the relative strength index is above 50. Further upside is therefore expected with 0.8320 and 0.8390 as the next targets. Only a break below the horizontal support at 0.7975 would allow for a retest of 0.7840 and 0.7700 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australia's benchmark 10-year government bond yields remain near record lows, after Britain's decision to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2  triggered a global flight into safe-haven assets. The 10-year government debt securities were yielding 2.04% during Asia morning trading, only slightly up from a record low of 1.9823% Friday. Fixed-income strategists at Goldman Sachs Asset Management say Australian government bond yields are likely to decline further. "We are positioned for the outperformance of European and Australian rates," they say. Bond yields fall, when bond prices rise. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; Twitter: @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD will likely ratchet down against the JPY, which is now at Y102.25, to hover around the 100-mark as investors eventually become less cautious about Tokyo's JPY-selling/USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Nomura Securities chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Yunosuke Ikeda in a morning note. "I would have no way but to say the possibility is low [for Tokyo] to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Ikeda, noting that a shift in Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2 's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric Friday suggests an inability to get a nod from his U.S. counterparts on JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ikeda tips the USD to trade in a Y96-Y105 range in next three months. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0033 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japan shares were up by a fraction Monday after getting slammed Friday by Britain's decision to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Nikkei Stock Average was up 1.3%, only a tiny reversal from an 8% loss last session. The S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  200 and South Korea's Kospi were still down, off 0.4% and 0.7% respectively. The fallout from last week's vote, results of which came early Friday Asia time, is expected to continue. Investors are watching global policymakers but also officials in Japan, who are under pressure to response to the jump in the Japanese yen. The local Nikkei media reported that Japan's government and the BOJ are discussing responses to the U.K. vote. Gold was up 1% while the yen traded around 102 to one U.S. dollar, having touched the 99 level Friday. (chao.deng@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0027 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  makes it more likely the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  will cut interest rates twice more this year, says ASB. It says Brexit's impact on financial markets is likely to give the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  pause for thought over its next rate hike. "Instead of potentially moving as early as July, the Fed may wait until much later in the year," ASB says. That would likely keep the NZD elevated against the USD, increasing pressure on the RBNZ to cut rates in August. ASB economists then see a second cut coming in November, taking the official cash rate to 1.75%. "Prior to the Brexit vote, we had become less confident of the second cut. However, the global outlook is now marred by uncertainty and we expect to see declines in global commodity prices, further adding to the global deflation impulse," ASB says. (kate.geenty@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0025 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW opened lower Monday but is clawing back up to its last closing level of 1,179.9, even as traders keep a wary eye out for possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. Risk aversion remains the theme, though the weekend break may have soothed investors' nerves slightly. Markets are still coming to grips with the result of the U.K. referendum, and while the implications aren't all clear yet, investors are going to shy away from taking risk. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the won, which are highly-sensitive to external economic impacts, are likely to remain depressed. High-yield \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  too are under pressure. There may be a spanner in the works for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets if the BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, which skyrocketed Friday as the pound went into a nosedive. Though the won usually follows the yen, in this case yen-weakening might be construed as a risk-on sign and might reverse the course for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  temporarily. USD/KRW is now 1,178.5 from its Friday closing of 1,179.9. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0017 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are likely to rise again, as the pound struggles to find its feet after the weekend. The freefall of the pound, after the earth-shattering U.K. referendum result, continues to cue the markets. Unless it manages to recover, the rout of risky, high-yield Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to be over. The Malaysia ringgit suffered one of the biggest losses Friday in reaction to the "Brexit", but the internationally-exposed Singapore dollar also fell sharply, though its depreciation was staunched by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Apart from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2 , other Asian central banks, including the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 , were also said to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tamp down excessive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility. There is a high chance that they will have to step in again today. Risk aversion is still rife, as seen in stocks, gold, and government bonds. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0009 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Oil prices drop lower in early Asia trade Monday as pessimism on the U.K. and \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 's economic growth as a result of the "Brexit" vote continues to weigh heavily on risk assets. "Buckle up for Monday, volatility is not ready to fade yet," says ANZ Research. Oil prices slid as much as over 6% on Friday after U.K. voted to surrender its \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership. "It is impossible to do justice to what has occurred over the past few days in the small space available here. For many market participants it will be one of those "always remember where you were and what you were doing" kind of moments," the bank says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 70 cents at $46.94 a barrel, Brent is down 61 cents to $47.80 a barrel. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0000 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is largely unchanged (now at 102.10) just above the 102.00 line, having risen earlier in the session on news that Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  was meeting senior \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  and finance ministry officials this morning. Abe reportedly instructed finance minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2  to team up with the BOJ to watch the financial markets closely. The prime minister reportedly says uncertainties still remain in the market, following the wild gyrations Friday after the U.K. decision to vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  becomes realistic if the dollar falls below Y100 again," says a Japanese bank dealer. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2338 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Australia's conservative government has pulled ahead of the Labor opposition a week from July 2 elections after Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malcolm Turnbull\f28 \uc2 \f2  urged voters to opt for stability in the wake of the startling U.K. Brexit referendum outcome. Newspoll on Monday showed the conservatives leading Labor for the first time in the two-month campaign, with 51% support to 49%, breaking weeks of deadlock. Other older surveys still have both sides on 50% support each, pointing to a result that could deliver a hung parliament. The U.K.'s surprise choice to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  will dominate the last week of the election race. On Sunday, Turnbull urged voters to "leave nothing in doubt" and back his economic plan in the wake of the Brexit vote. "At a time of uncertainty, the last thing we need is a Parliament in disarray," he said at the official campaign rally. "This is no time to pull the doona over our heads, or as Labor and the Greens would have it, to pretend that the good times will just keep rolling no matter how much you tax, how much you borrow or how much you spend." (rob.taylor@wsj.com; @WSJRobTaylor)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2335 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Britain's decision to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  could choke British tourism to Australia, but it is not a complete disaster, says Chris Caton, chief economist at BT Financial Group. "My personal view is that both sides overstated their case, so what has happened is only a major economic calamity rather than a complete disaster," says Caton. He expects direct economic effects on Australia should be relatively small, albeit clearly negative. "Only about 1.5% of our goods exports go to the U.K., and about 8% of our services exports, so tourism may see a larger impact than any other sector," says Caton. He also says commodity prices could fall further due to shrinking demand both in the U.K. and elsewhere. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; Twitter: @verasydney)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 26 Jun 2016 20:41 ET \par EUR/GBP Should Continue It's Advance Toward -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2334 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Brexit aside, the big event in the week ahead in Australia is Saturday's national election. At this stage, polls suggest it's too close to call, AMP Capital's Shane Oliver says. Still, it's a "big ask" to see the center-left Labor Party become the first opposition in 85 years to regain government after a single term in opposition: it needs to win 19 seats to achieve that. The big issue may be what happens in the Senate, Oliver says, with a good chance that the Greens and minority parties control the balance of power--again acting as a huge constraint on the government--which would mean another de facto minority government. That signals "poor prospects for getting government spending under control over the next three years and for implementing serious productivity enhancing economic reforms." He notes markets have rallied after 8 of the past 12 elections, partly out of relief that an election is out of the way. The Australian share market has fallen 3.1% in the lead up to the poll. (rachel.pannett@wsj.com Twitter: @RachelPannett)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 26, 2016 20:41 ET (00:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160627ec6r0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
