\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Central banks flex muscles to soften Brexit blow\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 17:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 24 (Reuters) - A number of central banks around the world geared up to take action or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their markets on Friday in the wake of Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  said it could provide more than 250 billion pounds plus "substantial" access to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease any squeeze in markets, while the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they were ready to offer liquidity through existing swap lines. The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also signalled readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem excessive yen strength.\par \par \uc2 Below is a list of central banks that have already taken action in the wake of Britain's \uc2 EU\uc2  referendum.\par \par \uc2 SWITZERLAND - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  provided rare confirmation that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the Swiss franc in the wake of the vote. The safe-haven franc hit its highest level against the euro since August 2015 and had its biggest one-day jump since the SNB removed its franc peg to the euro on Jan. 15, 2015.\par \par \uc2 NORWAY - \uc2 Norway's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped 23.2 billion Norwegian crowns ($2.73 billion) into its commercial banking system. While providing funds through "F-loans" auctions is a frequently used policy tool, it came with an unusually early deadline and was accompanied by a statement that said the bank was continuously assessing the liquidity situation. SERBIA - Dealers say the \uc2 Serbian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold euros on the local market to bolster the dinar after it tumbled to its weakest level on record against the euro.\par \par \uc2 SOUTH KOREA - South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were believed to be selling dollars to curb the won's fall amid Brexit fears, multiple traders told Reuters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 INDIA - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  likely sold dollars through state-owned banks to prevent the rupee sliding further, according to traders, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to its weakest since March.\par \par \uc2 CHINA - Traders said state-owned banks were offering dollar liquidity in the market - a tactic the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 often employs when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - as China's yuan slumped to more than 5-year lows. But that was seen more as an attempt to avert a cash squeeze at the end of the half year next week, as has happened previously, rather than as a response to the Brexit vote. (Compiled by Karin Strohecker)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-27T15:55:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160624ec6o01of1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
