\par\par \b \uc2 Volatility Has \uc2 G-7\uc2  Addressing  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Policy -- Brexit Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 17:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:39 ET - The \uc2 G-7\uc2  has essentially given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other members the green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Under previous agreements, the world's leading industrialized nations said \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only legitimate if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are "disorderly." Friday, after the Brexit caused wild swings in the pound, the dollar, the yen and a host of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 G-7\uc2  said in a statement: "We recognize that excessive volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates can have adverse implications for economic and financial stability." Tokyo's leadership has already signalled it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame yen strengthening. Although \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  and Fed officials said they're prepared to act and work in concert with other global policymakers, there's no sign yet of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Washington. (ian.talley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:39 ET - Strengthening US dollar has been a challenge for US meat exporters and Brexit's not likely to help, with the WSJ Dollar Index putting the greenback 1.71% higher against a basket of international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far Friday. "Our partners obviously have less spending power when that happens," says Mike Cockrell, CFO of Sanderson Farms (SAFM), one of the top US chicken firms. US meatpackers compete against poultry producers in the UK for international business, and Cockrell said it's a tossup on how that dynamic could be impacted by the UK vote to leave \uc2 EU\uc2 . (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 11:36 ET - Investors sold off Canadian bank stocks after the UK voted to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2 . The S&P/TSX Composite Bank Index was down 2.1% in morning trading in Toronto. Of the major banks, shares of RBC (RY.T) and Scotiabank (BNS.T) registered the biggest declines at 2.1% each. "While the direct implications on [Canadian] bank \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to be limited, the Brexit vote is clearly damaging to sentiment and adds incremental risk to an already weak global macro and interest rate outlook," Macquarie says. "In terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we continue to highlight challenging conditions in Wholesale/Capital markets \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  where our global colleagues are noting elevated risks/volatility as likely damaging to revenues this [quarter]." (rita.trichur@wsj.com; @ritatrichur)\par \par \uc2 11:35 ET - Senior Investment Manager \uc2 Ben Ritchie\uc2  at \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2  is pointing out a few hopeful things for UK after the Brexit vote. The UK-based fund manager expects the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to rapidly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the country's economy if it slows, and the declining pound should already soften any economic blow. Companies with shares on the FTSE will also be buffered by "hefty exposure to revenues" from abroad. But fear is also still in the air. "The negative implications of years of political uncertainty are bound to weigh heavily on the UK economy in the near term," Ritchie says. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:32 ET - One of the biggest surprises, beyond that Brexit is actually happening? People didn't understand why bookmakers were getting it wrong, said David Kelly, chief market strategist for JPMorgan's funds unit. "The reason the bookmakers got it wrong is because they don't forecast actual odds," he said in an interview. "They tell you where money is being placed, and bookmakers in the UK are heavily in favor of the remain campaign. That's because the money was being put in that way." Kelly added that the investment community shouldn't trust the market that is "heavily influenced by interested money." He said the fixed income market is similar because it is "distorted by buying." (emily.glazer@wsj.com; @EmilyGlazerWSJ)\par \par \uc2 11:28 ET - Gold prices surged on safe haven demand following Britain's decision to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and some analysts and traders are betting on an added benefit for the precious metal--continued low interest rates. The Fed has held off on raising interest rates this year as global economic uncertainty has unnerved markets, which has helped drive gold higher. While speculation of a rate increase this summer has previously weighed on prices, the chances are looking less likely now, clearing the way for gold to move higher, traders say. However, not everyone is convinced that a rate increase is off the table. \uc2 Julian Jessop\uc2 , head of commodities research at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said if US economic data continues to improve, the Fed may proceed with a rate hike this year. (stephanie.yang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:28 ET - Oil prices were near the high end of their two-month range of $44-$52 when Brexit happened, so even with a 4% plunge the \uc2 Nymex\uc2  Aug contract is at $48, smack in the middle of that range. Some analysts say Brexit won't leave a permanent mark, but Citi Futures says "we suggest operating from the assumption that today's moves are only an initial reaction and not the full price adjustment." He says Brent "will have difficultly regaining the $50 level now." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:27 ET - The \uc2 IMF\uc2  weighs the possibility of Brexit fallout requiring the emergency lender to bail out countries in the wake of the UK's decision to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2 . "We will continue to monitor developments closely and stand ready to support our members as needed," says IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 . There are a host of unknowns about how the Brexit will affect the UK's trade relationships, its role as a global financial hub and the economic policies of a new \uc2 British government\uc2 . Economists say negotiating the exit, and resolving unknowns, may take years. Such uncertainty will likely harm investor and consumer confidence. (ian.talley@wsj.com; @iantalley)\par \par \uc2 11:25 ET - Thanks to surging investor demand for gold this year, inflows into gold exchange-traded funds have taken off, with the two largest gold funds, GLD and IAU, seeing $14.8B of inflows year to date, according to Convergex. The physical holdings of GLD and IAU combined amounts to about 1,037 metric tons, exceeding the gold reserves of all but seven sovereign nations, including that held by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Saudi Arabia, Convergex says. With Britain voting to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , expect even more inflows, Citi says. "Though the current composition of gold ETF investors is more balanced today between long-term holders and short-term return-chasers versus 2011/12, we expect more of the retail and institutional 'hot money' to garner exposure, leading to further ETF inflows," Citi says. (stephanie.yang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:22 ET - MetLife CEO \uc2 Steven Kandarian\uc2  says about Brexit: "The decision to exit creates uncertainty for the global economy. From a strictly economic perspective, it's difficult to see who benefits from this outcome." MET is one of the most global of US insurers, with a big overseas business acquired from \uc2 AIG\uc2  in 2010. MET's regional headquarters for Europe is in Dublin, and its UK \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are run out of a branch office. \uc2 MetLife\uc2  said in a statement that it "is committed to the UK as it is a long-term strategic growth market." As the environment becomes clearer, \uc2 MetLife\uc2  will continue to evolve its business model." (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:20 ET - UK oil majors \uc2 BP\uc2  (BP.LN) and \uc2 Shell\uc2  (RDSB.LN) are among the only energy companies to trade higher after the Brexit vote, recovering from earlier losses. That was "probably an unfair reaction. They're global companies," says \uc2 Dan Pickering\uc2  of Tudor Pickering Holt. The bank earlier Friday said those British companies will actually get help from the vote as a falling pound and rising dollar lower some costs and raise their dividend yields. The stronger dollar and post-Brexit economic fears have WTI trading down 4.2% and natural gas down 1.5%. That is leading to broad losses for producer share prices with the XOP ETF down 1.4%. But Pickering says investors must be disciplined in thinking about what their companies are worth. "You have to make sure you're not freaking out with the rest of the market." (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:20 ET- Enjoy the US rig count rebound while it lasts, because it might come to an abrupt halt before long as a suddenly volatile oil market may yield caution from drillers. The number of active US oil rigs has risen for three straight weeks to 337 as markets await the latest \uc2 Baker Hughes\uc2  report due at 1pm ET. But amid a British vote for \uc2 EU\uc2  exit that's causing the dollar to soar and oil prices to plunge, R.T. Dukes at Wood Mackenzie tweets out: "This volatility is not a positive for the US rig count in the next few months. Economic uncertainty is not on oil exec wish lists." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2016 11:39 ET (15:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160624ec6o003hk\par }\page {\par\fs20{