\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Surges as 'Brexit' Vote Sparks Search for Safety\b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 07:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen surged Friday and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were battered as voters in the U.K. upset market expectations and backed their nation leaving the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen, considered a haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , strengthened to as high as \u165\'00\'A5 99.00 against the U.S. dollar\u8212\'20\'14its strongest level since November 2013, and a 7.2% appreciation from its Thursday closing level.\par \par \uc2 While the British pound was the main focus of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market action, having fallen to its weakest level versus the U.S. dollar since September 1985, the yen's surge is the latest headache for policy makers trying to nurse the Japanese economy back to health.\par \par \uc2 Some traders said the rocketing yen could potentially trigger the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to direct the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. The yen later pulled back and was recently at 102.35 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Takashi Hiratsuka, a trading group leader at the asset-management division of \uc2 Resona Bank\uc2 , said the ministry might not engage in direct yen selling as some in the market expect.\par \par \uc2 He said the \uc2 Japanese Finance Ministry\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without consent from U.S. authorities, who have so far officially said the yen has been stable.\par \par \uc2 "I believe the authorities will come up with a scenario to guide the yen weaker indirectly by using steps, including more easing," Mr. Hiratsuka said.\par \par \uc2 The pullback in the yen after its dramatic strengthening was likely due to market participants betting that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could step in to soothe the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's excessive volatility, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The yen shot past the 100.00 psychological barrier as preset trading orders were triggered, rallied to 99.00 versus the U.S. dollar, then rebounded to 101.50.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of global financial-market upheaval over Britain leaving the \uc2 EU\uc2  has upset \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets around the globe.\par \par \uc2 Most of the moves may have been due to a reversal of earlier consensus bets that the U.K. would vote to stay.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan slid as much as 0.8% against the U.S. dollar Friday to notch a five-year low of 6.6285. The depreciation was of a greater extent than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily yuan benchmark, which had implied a 0.2% weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The onshore-traded yuan has now stabilized with the \uc2 People's Bank of China\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, a Shanghai-based senior trader at a domestic bank said, though the volume of its yuan-buying hasn't been very large. The yuan has crossed the 6.6000 psychological barrier twice this month, due to wild swings in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Among other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, the Malaysian ringgit, the Singapore dollar and the South Korean won were most affected by the unfolding U.K. vote results.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar and the won slumped as much as 2.4% against the U.S. unit, versus their Thursday closing levels.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's ringgit fell 2.8%, Indonesia's rupiah lost 1.3%, while the Philippine peso weakened 0.9% versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar is highly vulnerable to external economic risks, more so than its neighbors, due to the city-state's reliance on global trade.\par \par \uc2 Victor Yong, interest-rates strategist for \uc2 United Overseas Bank\uc2  in Singapore, said that if a Brexit were to occur, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  might cut its base interest rate from the current record low of 0.50% potentially to zero, and this could happen within days, or at most weeks.\par \par \uc2 Given the urgency of the situation, the BOE will likely act quickly, though its first priority would be to ensure that interbank funding liquidity doesn't dry up.\par \par \uc2 Banks might refuse to lend to each other for fear of a deterioration of creditworthiness in the wake of a Brexit. The BOE has already laid out arrangements with other global central banks to flush the market with liquidity to prevent a freezing of the financial system, said Mr. Yong.\par \par \uc2 An interest-rate cut likely wouldn't happen on the same day, he said, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bias would certainly shift toward easing policy. Mr. Yong said a zero interest-rate policy might prove too radical given the recent debate about its effectiveness, but a quarter percentage-point cut combined with an asset-purchase program might be announced, to buffer the Brexit's negative shock to the U.K. economy.\par \par \uc2 Kosaku Narioka and Shen Hong contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2016 01:15 ET (05:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160624ec6o000jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
