\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia Ringgit Extends Losses on UK Vote to Leave -- Brexit Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 07:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysia's ringgit has lost 2.7% against the U.S. dollar, as the market reacted to the unexpected U.K. vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . The ringgit was earlier Friday weaker by only 0.7% when signs first appeared that the referendum result might be an upset. As the outcome became clearer with more votes trickling in, traders rushed to the U.S. dollar, Japanese yen, and other safe haven assets. Government bond yields fell dramatically, while gold prices rocketed higher. The ringgit is likely to keep sliding, the chart suggests. The USD/MYR will adopt a bullish technical bias if it ends Friday above 4.1260 and inside the Bollinger uptrend channel. But there is hope that the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , joining other Asian central banks that are said to have already stepped in to shore up excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. China, India, and Singapore's central banks have been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as authorities grapple with the 'Brexit' fallout.\par \par \uc2  (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0539 GMT The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could step up its monthly QE purchases to EUR100 billion per month, from EUR80 billion currently, over the coming 1-3 months, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in a research note Friday morning. In reaction to the U.K.'s vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 the ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  "could front-load purchases already today -- even without any official announcement. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  could also purchase longer-dated bonds or buy more periphery bonds without announcing changes," the bank says. "We do not expect rate cuts as a tool to limit contagion and \f28 \uc2 \f2 the ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  also seems less focused on a weaker euro." (todd.buell@wsj.com, @ToddBuell) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0537 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by global central banks and G20 governments may be necessary to stem the selloff arising from a British vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says Angus Nicholson, a market analyst at IG Markets. However, when there is so much division in Europe at the moment, "it is difficult to see how they can come together and present a unified case that will reassure markets," he says. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0536 GMT Commenting on the U.K. vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its implications for Switzerland, analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swissquote\f28 \uc2 \f2  write: "Clearly the [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] will be watching developments with wariness. Should the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  begin pricing the break-up of the EU-28 and possible EMU, we expected a significant rotation into CHF, forcing the SNB to act," they write. The first line of defense would likely be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the euro from weakening too much against the franc, they add. EUR/CHF trades at 1.0705, down 1.9%. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0535 GMT Citi says it expects the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cut interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point by July at the latest and restart its bond-buying program to support the economy, after Britons voted in favor of exiting the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 . It expects the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to consider further easing and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to delay its next rate increase until December or later. (jason.douglas@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0533 GMT Britain's vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  will have political and economic implications in a host of other \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  member states, ING's James Knightley says. It could boost anti-EU parties elsewhere in Europe ahead of elections in Spain, Netherlands, France, Germany and Italy over the next 18 months, he writes in a research note. There could also be economic contagion in countries with close trade ties to the U.K. "The Netherlands, Ireland and Belgium look most at risk," he adds. (maarten.vantartwijk@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0531 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The British pound slides more than 10% against the dollar to its lowest in more than 30 years after the U.K. votes in a referendum to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 . It drops to as low as $1.3228, according to CQG data, and last trades at $1.3411, with analysts seeing the potential for further substantial falls. "We would see further downward pressure on sterling as other asset markets open, where another 5% depreciation from its current level of 1.34 is very well possible in our view," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a note. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0531 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Sumitomo Mitsui FG (8316.TO) said Friday that it had already secured sufficient liquidity in U.S. dollars, British pound and euro. The British pound dropped sharply as the latest forecasts on Friday showed Britain has voted to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There may be some unexpected moves in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and credit markets but we don't think there will be a big impact on our business due to the preparation," Japan's second-largest lender said. "The process of leaving the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  will take two years so we don't expect an immediate impact but will continue monitoring the situation," the bank said. SMFG was off 9% at Y2,960. Rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \f28 \uc2 \f2 drops 7% at Y151. (atsuko.fukase@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TRL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0530 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The vote for the U.K. to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  will likely seal a victory for Australia's conservative government in the July 2 general elections, according to brokerage IG. "For Australia this is likely to lock in the Turnbull Liberal government's election as the economy and economic security will probably rocket to the forefront of voters' concerns," says IG market analyst Angus Nicholson. Australia's Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malcolm Turnbull\f28 \uc2 \f2  earlier appealed to voters to back his government to help ensure stability. (rob.taylor@wsj.com; @WSJRobTaylor) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0528 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Speculation in the markets will increasingly focus on the weakest links in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  post "Brexit," says Angus Nicholson, an analyst at IG Markets. "The PIGS (Portugal, Italy, Greece, Spain) are likely to fall under the intense focus of speculators again," he says. Spain has elections on Sunday and the Brexit vote will likely have a major influence on its outcome, Mr. Nicholson says. There is also increased speculation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be forced to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's strength, particularly given that it at one point broke 100--a red line for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , he says. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0528 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee tumbles as much as 1.4% against the haven U.S. dollar, reaching its lowest level in nearly four months, as the market reacted to the unexpected outcome that the U.K. has voted to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 . USD/INR spiked to 68.22 compared to its last closing of 67.25, but has since subsided to 68.12. The surprise outcome of the U.K. referendum triggered a surge of haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the U.S. dollar and the Japanese yen, which rose as much as 7.2% versus the greenback. Stock markets around the world fell, with the benchmark Sensex tanking 2.8%, exacerbating the bearish outlook for the rupee. The India rupee has tenatively recouped some of its losses, due to suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which has been selling U.S. dollars to tamp down the rupee's excessive weakening. The falling rupee has implications for India's inflation levels, which will affect the RBI's monetary policy stance in the medium term. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DJP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0527 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The president of Germany's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ifo Institute\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Clemens Fuest\f28 \uc2 \f2 , called the British vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  "a defeat of reason." He said that politicians must now do whatever they can to limit the economic damage. This involves ensuring that Britain "remains integrated into the single market as much as possible." He said it was important "to conclude negotiations as quickly as possible so that the phase of uncertainty over the future economic relations stays as short as possible." (todd.buell@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @ToddBuell) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 24, 2016 01:41 ET (05:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160624ec6o000n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
