\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: EU Referendum, Yellen Testimony in Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dominic Chopping   \par \uc2 1,267 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2016\par 04:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par  \par Other Macro \par 0900 Germany ZEW Economic \par      Sentiment \par 1300 \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  'S Draghi Speaks in \par      Brussels \par 1400 Fed's Yellen Testifies to \par      Senate Panel \par  \par Other Equities \par Adobe Systems 2Q \par Carnival 2Q \par FedEx 4Q \par  \par GLOBAL NEWS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Neel Kashkari\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday "all policy options would be on the table" at the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if Britons vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a referendum on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The upcoming vote, and the potential turbulence it could create, were factors in Fed officials' decision not to raise interest rates at their June meeting, Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  told reporters at a press conference last week.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously I think we think it would be better for the world economy...if 'remain' is what the vote is," Mr. Kashkari said.\par \par \uc2 He said he sees a "moderate, direct effect" on the U.S. economy in the near-term from a so-called Brexit vote, and possible financial stability risks. "If we're wrong and it ends up being a much larger effect, then I think all policy options would be on the table in response to that," he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister said Tuesday the government wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "easily," in a sharp shift in rhetoric suggesting that global criticism may have made Tokyo less eager to undertake steps to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "In any case, we have no intention to easily resort to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," finance minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials recognized in April that more fundamental problems than cheaper oil were weakening inflation, but they couldn't agree on how they might counter mounting risks to their rosy price scenario.\par \par \uc2 At the April 27-28 meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  revised lower its inflation projections for the coming years through a quarterly update and pushed back its forecast date for achieving its 2% price target once again to early 2018.\par \par \uc2 According to meeting minutes released Tuesday, the BOJ's nine board members agreed that the latest cut made to the inflation forecast for the year ending March 2017 was "mainly attributable to downward revisions in projections for gross-domestic-product growth and wage developments."\par \par \uc2 Iran's intelligence ministry\uc2  on Monday said it had thwarted what would have been one of the largest-ever terrorist attacks on Tehran and had arrested people connected to the plot.\par \par \uc2 The ministry said that terrorists had planned numerous bombings across the country to coincide with religious occasions, and that an unspecified number of arrests had been made. Bombs and large quantities of explosives were seized, it said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from the ongoing focus on the U.K.'s \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  referendum, investors will look ahead to Federal Reserve Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  's semi-annual testimony to \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Congress\f28 \uc2 \f2  starting Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Yellen is likely to convey the same message as she did last week after the FOMC meeting. Thus, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects her to indicate that a rate hike is still likely in the near future, if the uncertainty related to the U.S. labor market is resolved favorably and the U.K. votes to remain in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We continue to expect the next rate hike in September and one more in December. At this juncture, however, risks are obviously tilted towards less tightening. In case of Brexit, Fed rate hikes will likely be postponed until end 2016 or even into 2017." \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par 0150 GMT 9.3908-58 \par Previous 9.3645-95 \par %Chg +0.28 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par 0150 GMT 9.3280-380 \par Previous 9.3262-362 \par %Chg +0.02 \par  \par USD/NOK \par 0150 GMT 8.2803-63 \par Previous 8.2781-841 \par %Chg +0.03 \par  \par USD/SEK \par 0150 GMT 8.2263-353 \par Previous 8.2413-503 \par %Chg -0.18 \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Scandinavian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gained on Monday, with EUR/NOK down 0.55% at 9.3753 and EUR/SEK down 0.6% at 9.3319, recovering after weakening over the past week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DNB said that risk-off sentiment had weighed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and it expected appetite for them to pick up. NOK will be helped by "steadily higher oil prices", while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "still is undervalued despite weakening fundamentals."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Sweden, DNB believes inflation will see a temporary lift, making the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Riksbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  more concerned about financial stability and less concerned about SEK strength. It sees EUR/SEK falling to 9.25, 9.00 and then 8.70 in one, three and 12 months respectively. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 1421.07 +3.03% \par OMXS30 1340.29 +3.23% \par OBX     541.98 +2.37% \par OMXC20  992.99 +2.51% \par OMXH25 3225.87 +3.24% \par  \par Brent crude: $50.34 \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic markets closed sharply higher Monday, buoyed by a weekend poll that showed increasing support for the Remain campaign in the U.K.'s upcoming \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  referendum.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Risk-on sentiment is hitting markets, but caution is needed," warned \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saxo Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "The worst scenario if this pricing continues is that should a reversal of U.K. opinion poll sentiment (or a Brexit itself) occur in the next few days that massive volatility would be unleashed."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent crude oil posted solid gains on the day to trade over the $50 a barrel mark, after prices flirted with $47 last week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices slipped slightly in early Asian trade after Monday's surge, but analysts said the market mood remains upbeat as more investors think the British voters will choose for their country to stay in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A weaker U.S. dollar and easing concerns over U.K.'s \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  referendum saw investors pile back into commodities. Oil prices lead the sector higher, supported by expectations of another strong decline in U.S. oil inventories," said ANZ Research. The Brexit vote is on June 23 and the U.S. oil data will be released Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0051 GMT, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  was down 23 cents at $49.14 a barrel, while Brent was down 31 cents at $50.34 a barrel. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Denmark's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vestas Wind Systems\f28 \uc2 \f2  will hold a capital markets day in London on Tuesday, in the wake of a giant merger between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siemens\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gamesa\f28 \uc2 \f2  that will replace Vestas as the world's biggest wind turbine maker, a sign of sharper global competition. Some discussion about market share is expected. Although the U.S. 2,000-megawatt Wind XI project is not finally approved, Friday's news that the project had chosen Vestas turbines is a strong signal to competitors, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sydbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note. The bank expected no change to Vestas' 2016 outlook on Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Sweden's Spotify, a music streaming service, is valued at $8.5 billion, making it the highest valued of Europe's 47 unicorns, a word used to describe a start-up tech company worth $1 billion or more, according to a report by tech investment bank GP Bullhound. Sweden is home to seven unicorns, which places the country second after the U.K., but before Germany and France. In March, Spotify raised $1 billion in convertible debt from investors, under conditions that push the company to hold a public offering within a year. Spotify recently hired Wall Street veteran Paul Vogel as head of investor relations, the company confirmed on Monday, which is seen as an apparent move towards an IPO.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Dominic Chopping at dominic.chopping@wsj.com; Twitter: @domchopping @WSJNordics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 20, 2016 22:52 ET (02:52 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160621ec6l0004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
