\par\par \b \uc2 Why Is The Yen Stubbornly High Even As Brexit Risk Fades -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2016\par 08:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The risk of Britain leaving the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is now fading after the tragic murder of lawmaker Jo Cox. The British pound jumped almost 3.5% against the U.S. dollar in the last three trading days, while the euro rose 0.85%.\par \par \uc2 But the Japanese yen remains stubbornly high, hardly budging after it crossed the psychologically important 105 per dollar mark last week. The yen retreated 0.4% in Asia hours today to 104.37.\par \par \uc2 The recent rise in the euro is because Brexit risk is fading, but the yen remains elevated because the market is now pricing in a slower pace of Fed rate hikes, and more importantly, of uncertainly over the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's resolve to ease further, commented \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, how the yen moves after the UK referendum on Thursday is worth watching. If the yen is slow to deflate even after the referendum, "the BOJ may be pressured to take action," said analyst Naka Matsuzawa.\par \par \uc2 Will \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market? Eisuke Sakakibara, an ex-official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  who is nicknamed "Mr. Yen" for his accurate prediction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told Bloomberg yesterday "the yen may break 100 at any time". Not until then will Japan's Finance Minister get the U.S.'s nod to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He told Bloomberg:\par \par \uc2 "There will be no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before 100, but the U.S. also doesn't want too weak a dollar," he said. "So if the yen is heading toward 90, it's possible Japan would get U.S. backing to do something."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yen finds it hard to imagine the yen moves back to 120 or 125, reported Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei 225 rose 1.4% buoyed by a weaker yen today. Overnight, the \uc2 iShares MSCI Japan ETF\uc2  (EWJ) rose 2.3%, the \uc2 WisdomTree Japan Hedged Equity Fund\uc2  (DXJ) was up 2.1%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 21, 2016 02:20 ET (06:20 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160621ec6l000ci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
