\par\par \b \uc2 Small Investors May Want to Sit This One Out -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2016\par 13:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:30 ET - Mom and pop investors would be wise to just watch the churn that may well occur all week if today's risk-on action is any indication. Much of what will go on is the hot money crowd moving into and out of asset classes based in the wind direction of the moment regarding Brexit. It's not anything individuals would be wise to partake in until the dust settles next week. While waiting, there's Yellen's Humphrey Hawkins appearances on the Hill tomorrow and Wednesday. S&P 500 futures remain up 27 points as oil futures rise nearly 2% and gold falls 1%. The euro gains amid the pound's bounce, rising to $1.1330 but well off session highs near $1.14. And sovereign debt pulls back; the 10-year yield is at 1.66%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 1128 GMT Scandinavian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gain on Monday, with EUR/NOK down 0.55% at 9.3753 and EUR/SEK down 0.6% at 9.3319, recovering after weakening over the past week. DNB says risk-off sentiment weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it expects appetite for them to pick up. NOK will be helped by "steadily higher oil prices", while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "still is undervalued despite weakening fundamentals". In Sweden, DNB believes inflation will see a temporary lift, making the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  more concerned about financial stability and less concerned about SEK strength. It sees EUR/SEK falling to 9.25, 9.00 and then 8.70 in one, three and twelve months respectively. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1059 GMT Even after tightening 2-3 bps in the secondary market, Danish \f28 \uc2 \f2 BRFkredit\f28 \uc2 \f2 's EUR750 million seven-year covered bond, launched last week, still seems cheap, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. The bond featured a new issue premium of 4-5 bps, which is partly due to the fact that \f28 \uc2 \f2 BRFkredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  is a rather recent entrant. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BRFkredit\f28 \uc2 \f2 's outstanding five-year euro covered bond also looks cheap, as it trades 5 bps above comparable issues by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Considering the two banks' credit quality, analyst Uffe Kalmar Hansen sees "no reason" for \f28 \uc2 \f2 BRFkredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  to be trading wider than \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1045 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] International Monetary Fund First Deputy Managing Director \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Lipton\f28 \uc2 \f2  has a clear message to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  upon his latest visit to Tokyo: Monetary policy alone can't create stable 2% inflation it wants. "If there were only one arrow of Abenomics--monetary policy--that would not likely pull the economy out of disinflation decisively, nor would it reactivate growth in the short run, nor would it allow Japan to achieve faster growth in the long-run," Lipton said in an interview. His strong remark underscores how three years of aggressive easing by BOJ Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda, and its failure to generate stable inflation, has convinced international policymakers of the limits of monetary policy. Kuroda's argument that deflation is a monetary phenomenon that can be fixed by a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is now faced with growing skepticism both at home and abroad. (Takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1042 GMT Credit investors shouldn't get too scared about a possible U.K. exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as the direct negative implications of Brexit are unlikely to last very long, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Philip Gisdakis. What's more, this period of weakness could create buying opportunities, he adds. Non-financial corporate bonds, even British company issues ineligible for \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases, have been broadly stable in recent days, suggesting a degree of resilience against U.K. referendum jitters. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1031 GMT Outside of sterling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs, EUR/JPY is likely to be the cross that reacts most to Thursday's U.K. referendum on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership, whichever way the vote goes, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts say. Commenting on CFTC data on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positioning, they say: "the most striking position is a short in EUR/JPY, the non-GBP cross that will react most on either outcome from the referendum." Data show yen longs building and euro shorts rebuilding, while dollar longs have been reduced, they note. However, positioning in these three \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is "not extreme" by recent standards. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1022 GMT Political risk events, starting with the U.K. referendum on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership on Thursday, could keep any new euro covered bond shelved until the third quarter, which begins in two weeks, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 ABN Amro\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Senior fixed income strategist Joost Beaumont expects issuers and investors to remain in wait-and-see mode for the time being. "Constructive" outcomes in the U.K. referendum and the Spanish elections may spur some primary market activity, but issuers are more likely to wait until next month. In the secondary market, most covered bonds are resilient, but U.K. names underperformed last week, likely reflecting Brexit concerns, Mr. Beaumont adds. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1020 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  has begun to push Japan to take more radical steps, such as strong-arming firms into raising wages, and the move seems to reflect a view that Tokyo just has to keep experimenting with new things to find a fix to its deflationary problems. The fund's First Deputy Managing Director, \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Lipton\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said in an interview that there is no model country for Japan to follow because it has been the only nation in the modern era that has gone through decades of deflation or near-zero inflation. "You have to be the path breaker," Lipton said of Japan. "I suspect that other countries may learn from your experience, but I think you have to be the path breaker on this." (Takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1009 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's SET ends up 0.1% at 1421.99 flirting with gains along with other Asian markets after a poll showed that Britain may be less likely to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  than previously thought. Oil and energy companies trend higher, as oil prices also gain. Thai Oil (TOP.TH), for example, ends up 0.4%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 PTT Exploration & Production\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PTTEP.TH) ends up 2.8% after publishing its first quarter results analysis. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glow Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GLOW.TH) is the exception, down 1.8%, while TMB Bank (TMB.TH) falls 1.8%. (jake.watts@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1006 GMT Issues from supranational and sovereign agencies, such as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Financial Stability Facility\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Investment Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , have become substantially cheaper compared with \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds following recent market moves, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2 . This is especially the case in maturities beyond the 10-year mark, analyst Uffe Kalmar Hansen says. The widening SSA-bund gap shows investors have yet another reason to focus on the U.K. referendum on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership on June 23, as a 'Remain' outcome will "very likely" lead to SSA outperformance versus bunds. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1003 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  is giving Japan the cold shoulder over the issue of whether action to stem the yen's rise is warranted, underscoring lack of global support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Tokyo. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a set of understandings with its partner countries in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the G-20, with respect to exchange rate policy," IMF Deputy Managing Director \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Lipton\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who is in Tokyo for annual policy dialogues, says in an interview Monday. "Those understandings are for Japan to sort out with those partners." The remarks by \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 's No. 2 official come in contrast to what he said in the summer of 2012, when he noted that, if market forces "lead to disruptive flows, short-term smoothing is something that we would understand." The difference suggests that the rest of the world views the yen as still being at levels with which Japan's exporters can live with. The USD/JPY is currently at around Y104.50, compared with around Y80.00 in June 2012. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0959 GMT Slovakia's EUR388 million government bond sale at its monthly auction Monday heralds the beginning of a summer bond sale pause as the Central European sovereign's next bond auction is scheduled for Sept. 19. Ardal, the Slovak debt agency, didn't conduct bond auctions in the July and August months of 2015 and 2014 either. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 20, 2016 07:30 ET (11:30 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160620ec6k0018r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
