\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan finmin says deeply concerned about yen rise, vows urgent response\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2016\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Aso warns vs rapid, speculative yen gain amid Brexit fears\par \par \uc2 * Closely watching June 23 British vote on whether to leave \uc2 EU\par \par \uc2 * MOF, BOJ see \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility high, no debate on contingency plan (Adds detail on meeting between MOF, BOJ, FSA)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 17 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  on Friday fired off a warning shot against a recent rise in the yen, saying he was deeply concerned about "one-sided, rapid and speculative" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and would respond urgently if necessary - a hint at possible yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest jawboning - official comment intended to influence markets - comes as the yen surged across the board after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (BOJ) left monetary policy unchanged on Thursday, despite market fears of global turmoil if Britain votes to exit the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in the June 23 referendum.\par \par \uc2 Senior officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), the BOJ and the Financial Services Agency (FSA) later held a regular meeting to discuss financial markets. They did not debate contingency plans to counter a market rout in the event of Brexit.\par \par \uc2 Masatsugu Asakawa, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, told reporters after the meeting that the officials agreed that volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was increasing and they should liaise closely.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  the MOF has jurisdiction over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under instructions from the ministry.\par \par \uc2 "Stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is extremely important. We'll closely coordinate with other countries on this issue," Aso said earlier, adding that he was watching the market with a sense of urgency to prevent speculative moves from persisting.\par \par \uc2 "We will respond more than ever when necessary. I believe such response is in line with G7 and G20 agreements."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed out from the market since it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November 2011.\par \par \uc2 Aso declined to comment when asked if the MOF and FSA have any contingency plans to avoid financial market turmoil in case of Brexit. "It is desirable for Britain to stay in a strong \uc2 EU\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 Asakawa would not be drawn on Brexit contingency measures either. "It is up to Britain whether a Brexit will happen. We did not necessarily discuss concrete responses on the basis of hypothetical situations." (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Chris Gallagher and Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-17T04:28:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160617ec6h0048t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
