\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Rise on Reduced Brexit Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 1,273 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2016\par 05:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         17733.10   92.93  0.53% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2         4844.91    9.98  0.21% \par S&P 500       2077.99    6.49  0.31% \par FTSE 100      5950.48  -16.32 -0.27% \par Nikkei Stock 15648.80  214.66  1.39% \par Hang Seng    20212.66  174.24  0.87% \par Kospi         1960.65    8.66  0.44% \par SGX Nifty     8206.00   52.00  0.64% \par June contract \par  \par USD/JPY 104.70-71   +0.42% \par Range   104.82   104.25 \par EUR/USD 1.1256-59   +0.29% \par Range   1.1274   1.1224 \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBOT\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wheat July $4.724 per bushel \par Comex Gold    $1,298.40  +0.8% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude (NY) $45.99 -$2.02 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks climbed Thursday, snapping a five-session streak of declines as telecommunication and utilities shares gained.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow industrials had fallen as much as 169 points in morning trading after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dashed hopes for additional monetary easing and amid concerns about the impact of a coming U.K. vote on whether to remain in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Official campaigning ahead of the referendum was halted Thursday after an attacker killed a lawmaker from the U.K.'s main opposition Labour Party.\par \par \uc2 By midday, stocks had recovered most of their losses. Trading was below this year's average and some analysts said investors were taking advantage of the multiday decline to exit bets against the market, helping stocks rebound from the day's lows.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 92.93 points, or 0.5%, to 17733.10. The S&P 500 gained 6.49 points, or 0.3%, to 2077.99 and the Nasdaq Composite Index advanced 9.98 points, or 0.2%, to 4844.91.\par \par \uc2 "The market has been quite resilient," said Jeremy Zirin, head of equity strategy at UBS Wealth Management Americas.\par \par \uc2 Telecommunication stocks in the S&P 500 rose 0.8% and utilities shares climbed 0.7% as investors bought companies that tend to hold up better in times of economic uncertainty. \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2  gained 62 cents, or 1.2%, to $53.46.\par \par \uc2 Energy shares were the only S&P 500 sector to decline, losing 0.2% as U.S. oil prices dropped 3.7% to $46.21 a barrel. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese stocks bounce back Friday, the day after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  decided to hold fire on easing, triggering a sharp stocks selloff. Concerns smolder, however, that the U.K. could vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , triggering global economic and trade disruptions. Stocks across sectors are rising, but particularly precision and industrial machineries and commodities. Electronics and ceramics maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kyocera\f28 \uc2 \f2  (6971.TO) is up 3.0% at 5,089 yen. Trading firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  (8053.TO) is up 2.9% at 1,018.5 yen. Construction machinery maker Komatsu (6301.TO) is up 2.7% at 1,830.5 yen. The Nikkei is up 1.6% at 15681.70.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-term JGB yields were higher Friday morning in Tokyo, following a recovery in Tokyo stocks. The benchmark 10-year JGB yield advanced to minus 0.190%, higher than the record low of minus 0.210% set on Thursday, while the 20-year note yielded 0.115%, up from Thursday's all-time low of 0.090%. Lead JGB futures are currently down 0.17 at 152.56.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese shares edged up Friday, driven by improving sentiment as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market stabilizes, said analysts. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index gains 0.6% at 2896.46, led by gains in telecom, finance and brokerages. "The sentiment has stabilized a bit as yuan ceased its depreciating trend," said Zhang Xin, an analyst at Guotai Junan Securities. The Shenzhen Composite Index rallies 1.3% at 1909.57 while the ChiNext Price Index gains 1.7% at 2136.84.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Kospi was up 0.9%, while the S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  200 edged up 0.3%. A global recovery in stocks started overnight with U.S. equities; They snapped a five-session streak of declines. Investors are still worried about the impact of the coming U.K. vote on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership. But for now, markets look resilient. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set Friday's yuan mid-point at 6.5795 versus Thursday's 6.5739, representing a 0.1% weakening of the yuan against the U.S. dollar. The U.S. dollar index crept up overnight, influencing the USD/CNY parity rate today. The greenback has subsided since, with slimmer odds of a "Brexit" occurring, which ought to result in a lower USD/CNY fixing on Monday. The yuan has been whipsawing of late, in the wake of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 MSCI\f28 \uc2 \f2  decision to keep Chinese shares out of its Emerging Markets index for now. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's volatility may continue, though the \f28 \uc2 \f2 MSCI\f28 \uc2 \f2  effect has faded, due to wild gyrations of the Japanese yen. The yen acts as a beacon for many Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and on Thursday rallied to a 22-month peak when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  kept policy unchanged. The yen is now weakening, on fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0136 GMT, USD/CNY was at 6.5848 from its official close of 6.5840 on Thursday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold has retraced much of its gains after rallying to a 22-month high the previous day, which was mainly fuelled by safe haven demand over the 'Brexit' vote and benign U.S. interest rates. The safe haven appeal of the precious metal waned, however, after the assassination of a U.K. member of parliament led to a suspension of the campaign for the referendum on Britain's decision. Spot gold was recently trading at $1,279.48 per ounce, slightly higher than the previous close at $1,277/oz, but well below the previous day's high of $1,315.61/oz. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil prices took a positive turn in early Asia trade Friday, pushed by bargain-hunting and speculative trades, but analysts warn that suspense around U.K.'s future in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  is still spooking investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude for July delivery was up $0.29 or 0.6% at $46.50 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 , after posting its largest one-day loss since April overnight, resulting in a 5.2% decline for the week. Brent, the global benchmark was last up $0.41 cents or 0.9% at $47.60 a barrel. It has dropped nearly 6% week-to-date. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par Japan MOF, BOJ Reach Understanding Yen 'Very Volatile' \par Japan Finance Minister Ratchets Up Warnings on Yen \par Japanese Government Maintains Its Assessment of the Overall Economy \par Singapore May Non-Oil Exports in Surprise Surge \par HSBC to Pay $1.58 Billion to Settle Shareholder Suit \par PSAV Postpones IPO, Citing Unfavorable Market Conditions \par EU Agrees on Measures Regulating Conflict Minerals \par FedEx Agrees to $240 Million Settlement With Drivers in 20 States \par Chile Leaves Key Interest Rate Unchanged at 3.5% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lumber Liquidators\f28 \uc2 \f2  Won't Resume Sales of Inventory of Chinese-Made Laminate \par Union Workers Threaten Strike at Atlantic City Casinos \par U.S. Government Says It Met Requirements for MetLife Oversight \par Inflblox to Cut About 110 Jobs, or 12% of Workforce \par Smith & Wesson Boosts Guidance After Strong Results \par Revlon To Buy Elizabeth Arden \par Sumner Redstone's National Amusements Removes Five Viacom Directors \par U.S. State Department Officials Call for Strikes Against Syria's Assad \par Brazil President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michel Temer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Says Graft Claims Are 'Lies' \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 16, 2016 23:01 ET (03:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160617ec6h00056\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
