\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar's Slide Upends Asia's Best-Laid Plans\b0\par\par\uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2016\par 07:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 All it took was a single U.S. jobs report to ruin summer for Asian central bankers.\par \par \uc2 Policy makers in the region's major economies have spent much of the year hoping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would weaken against the dollar, to stoke their countries' exports. In recent weeks those hopes seemed on track, as expectations grew that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would soon raise interest rates. China's yuan and Japan's yen both slid against the dollar in May, the yuan by the most since August and the yen by the most in a year and a half.\par \par \uc2 But the hopes came crashing down Friday, when figures showing weaker-than-expected employment growth in the U.S. last month led traders to mark down the chances of a Fed rate increase this summer. Investors dumped their dollar holdings\u8212\'20\'14and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen, the yuan and the Korean won surged.\par \par \uc2 While Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped back a touch on Monday, the damage has been done. The yen weakened 0.6%, but at \u165\'00\'A5 107.158 against the U.S. dollar it ended far stronger than the \u165\'00\'A5 111.43 of just days earlier.\par \par \uc2 The yen's resurgence has revived the possibility of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Yoshihide Suga\uc2  was the latest to suggest that authorities could yet step in to drive the yen down.\par \par \uc2 "We will watch movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely intensely and take firm actions as necessary to ensure that speculative movement won't continue," Mr. Suga said at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 Still, for Tokyo policy makers, weakening the yen is complicated by political pressure from the U.S. not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly. In recent months both leading U.S. presidential candidates, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , have accused \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  of past \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  but talk does the rounds in the markets that the U.S. opposes it, that will undoubtedly weaken the effects of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  considerably," said former Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Naoyuki Shinohara in an interview with The Wall Street Journal. Mr. Shinohara handled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy during the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Eisuke Sakakibara, one of Mr. Shinohara's predecessors, said in a separate interview that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "would give U.S. presidential candidates no choice but to denounce Japan" in their efforts to win over voters.\par \par \uc2 "Japan must not let that happen," he said.\par \par \uc2 Even in the absence of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could deploy additional stimulus measures this summer, analysts said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year set negative interest rates on some deposits for the first time.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. situation is going to be more challenging for the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  than for other central banks, said Binay Chandgothia, a portfolio manager at \uc2 Principal Global Investors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Japanese earnings have been cut to one of the worst in the region and another appreciation in the yen towards 100 will be negative for the entire corporate sector," he said.\par \par \uc2 In China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan's rate 0.45% stronger on Monday than on Friday, its biggest one-day jump in five weeks. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  fixes a daily midpoint for the yuan and allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade within 2% of that, higher or lower.\par \par \uc2 Monday's stronger fix, though, only left markets wondering why it wasn't stronger. Since August last year, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has said it would allow market forces to play a greater role in determining the exchange rate. Using data on how other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have traded, investment banks have produced models that forecast the yuan's path with increasing degree of accuracy.\par \par \uc2 Zhou Hao, senior emerging markets economist for Asia at Commerzbank, said the yuan should have been fixed stronger, given what the market rate suggested\u8212\'20\'14one indicator that Chinese policy makers are attempting to engineer weakness in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "China is still trying to manage its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the exchange rate," he said. "China doesn't want to lose control and follow the market."\par \par \uc2 "We thought they were pegged to a basket [of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] but it isn't showing up in the fixing," Principal's Mr. Chandgothia said. "We're not sure if they have had a change of heart, or if this is something temporary."\par \par \uc2 A stronger yuan reduces the need for China to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and reduces demand among Chinese citizens to convert their yuan into dollars or other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan will likely trade in a broadly stable range for the next few weeks as \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expectations are repriced. "Right now the renminbi isn't in focus, it's the U.S. that's in the spotlight," Mr. Zhou said, using another name for the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com and Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 06, 2016 01:45 ET (05:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160606ec66000bo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
