\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar plummets against yen on G7 meeting, Japanese data\b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2016\par 21:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar falls nearly 1 pct vs yen\par \par \uc2 * G7 meeting, Japan trade data boost yen\par \par \uc2 * Dollar erases most early gains vs euro\par \par \uc2 * Traders await U.S. data (Updates prices, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Sam Forgione\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, May 23 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar tumbled nearly 1 percent against the yen on Monday on Japanese trade data and Washington's resistance to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo, but was little changed against the euro as investors awaited fresh U.S. economic data.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last down 0.86 percent at 109.19 yen, near a session low, after data showing Japan logged a trade surplus for the third consecutive month. The dollar was set for its biggest daily percentage decline against the yen in more than three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Besides the trade data, a \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  finance ministers' meeting concluded on Saturday with the United States warning \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, a rift that is perceived as preventing Tokyo from acting.\par \par \uc2 "You had no firm resolution coming from G7 with respect to the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes people a little bit more comfortable in being long yen, and you had a marketplace that was frankly over-short yen," said Richard Scalone, co-head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at TJM Brokerage in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The dollar erased most of its earlier gains against the euro , which last traded roughly flat at $1.1217 after hitting a session low of $1.1187.\par \par \uc2 Hawkish comments from San Francisco Federal Reserve President John Williams and St. Louis Fed President \uc2 James Bullard\uc2  on Monday initially drove the dollar's gains against the euro by reinforcing expectations for a potential U.S. interest rate increase in June or July.\par \par \uc2 The dollar gave back those gains as investors held out for more U.S. data, said Shahab Jalinoos, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "There's only so much the dollar can rally in the absence of proof that the data will compel the Fed to act in June or July," he said.\par \par \uc2 Jalinoos said he expected the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to wait until September at the earliest to raise rates again, and that the euro would likely remain within a range of $1.10-$1.16 until the next release of U.S. monthly employment data on June 3.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data on new home sales, durable goods orders, consumer sentiment and first-quarter GDP are set to be released this week. More Fed officials also are scheduled to speak.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against a basket of six major rivals, was last down 0.12 percent at 95.223. (Reporting by Sam Forgione; Additional reporting by Patrick Graham in London; Editing by Paul Simao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-23T20:39:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160523ec5n01wir\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
