\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Slumping Japan exports, factory orders add to headaches for PM Abe, BOJ\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2016\par 09:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * April exports, imports fall more sharply than March\par     * Yen gains, slowdown in China, other emerging markets weigh\par     * May factory activity shrinks most in over 3 years - survey\par     * Pressure grows on gov't, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for more stimulus\par     * US warns Tokyo not to weaken yen through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     By Tetsushi Kajimoto and Leika Kihara\par     TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - Japan's exports fell sharply in\par April and manufacturing activity suffered the fastest\par contraction since Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  swept to power in\par late 2012, providing further evidence that the premier's\par Abenomics stimulus policy is struggling for traction.\par     The bleak readings on the health of the world's\par third-largest economy follow Japan's failure last week to win\par support from its global counterparts to weaken the strong yen,\par which Tokyo fears could do further damage to the sputtering\par economy.\par     They also add pressure on both Abe and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to\par do more to rev up flagging growth, even as economists and global\par investors worry that central banks may be reaching their limits\par with radical experiments that have yet to kick-start growth.\par     Data on Monday showed Japan's exports fell 10.1 percent in\par April from a year earlier, the fastest decline in three months\par as a stronger yen and weakness in China and other emerging\par markets take their toll on the country's shipments.\par     Imports shrank more than 23 percent, reflecting not only\par lower commodity prices but stubbornly weak domestic demand that\par has defied a massive asset-buying programme by the BOJ which is\par now into its fourth year. \par     The decline was likely exaggerated by a drop in U.S.-bound\par car exports due to supply-chain disruptions caused by \par earthquakes in Japan last month, but a firmer yen and lacklustre\par global demand are clouding the outlook for 2016. Some analysts\par fear the economy could contract this quarter after dodging a\par return to recession early in the year.\par     "Drops in U.S.-bound car exports were noise," said Takeshi\par Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute.\par     "Asia and the global economy remain weak. On top of that,\par yen gains squeeze profits at exporters, causing wages and\par capital spending to weaken, which would hamper 'Abenomics' aim\par of creating virtuous growth," Minami said.\par     \par     STRONG YEN PUTS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN A BIND\par     Japanese officials recently threatened to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to halt "disorderly, one-sided" moves\par as the yen bolted to 18-month highs, but the United States\par issued a fresh warning to Tokyo on Saturday against weakening\par its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , overshadowing a Group of 7 finance leaders'\par gathering to discuss how to revive growth.   \par     Bank of Japan Deputy Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hiroshi Nakaso\f28 \uc2 \f2 , while\par declining to comment on how a strong yen could affect its policy\par decisions, said on Monday it was desirable for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates to\par move stably, reflecting economic fundamentals.\par     "The desirability of exchange-rate stability is widely\par shared by the Japanese industry," he said at a seminar.\par     The BOJ is widely expected to expand policy again by July,\par after shocking global markets by moving to negative interest\par rates earlier this year. Such a move, along with a possible\par interest rate hike by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , could take some\par fire out of the yen. \par     Abe is widely expected to unveil an additional budget this\par summer, and many analysts expect he will postpone a sales tax\par increase slated for early next year, though officials have\par publicly said they will press ahead with the plan barring a\par massive shock to the economy.\par     A private business survey on Monday suggested more pain\par ahead for Japanese manufacturers. The Markit/Nikkei preliminary\par survey for May showed factory activity shrank for the third\par straight month while total new orders declined at the sharpest\par pace in 41 months. \par     Exports to China - Japan's largest trading partner - fell\par 7.6 percent in April, while the U.S.-bound shipments fell 11.8\par percent year-on-year. Car exports to the United States fell 4.4\par percent, down for the first time since November 2014.\par     Exports to Asia, which accounts for more than half of\par Japan's shipments, fell 11.1 percent in the year to April, but\par EU-bound shipments rose 9.9 percent.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  recently forecast a\par bigger-than-expected 35 percent tumble in net profit for the\par current year due to the sharp appreciation of the yen, ending\par three straight years of record profits driven in part by a weak\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But it still expected global sales to inch up this\par year. \par     The yen was trading around 110 to the dollar on\par Monday, pulling back from a high above 105 yen earlier this\par month, its strongest since late 2014.\par \par  (Additional reporting by Stanley White; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-23T08:14:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160523ec5n00mgf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
