\par\par \b \uc2 U.S., Japan Clash Over Yen Policy at G-7 Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Mitsuru Obe   \par \uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2016\par 07:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SENDAI, Japan -- Differences between the U.S. and Japan over the yen surfaced again Saturday, underscoring the difficulty the world's leading economies face as they try to coordinate efforts to stoke global growth.\par \par \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs ended a two-day meeting without an agreement on a more balanced policy mix, including additional, possibly coordinated fiscal stimulus, and aligning divergent monetary policies.\par \par \uc2 Policy makers have stressed in recent months that, given risks to the global economy, fiscal stimulus and structural reforms should be used to supplement the extraordinary monetary easing being conducted by central banks in the U.S., Europe and Japan. They have also reiterated that countries should avoid resorting to competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations to generate growth at the expense of other nations.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo and Washington clashed over whether Japan should be allowed to arrest the yen's recent rise. A sharply weaker yen, catalyzed by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's easing policies, had been a key element of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's growth program, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rebounded moderately since January.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said Saturday that he expressed concern about what he termed excessive movements in the yen to his counterpart, U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew, on the sidelines of the meeting. Mr. Aso said he told Mr. Lew that "one-sided, speculative trades" have been seen in the market, something that Tokyo considers undesirable.\par \par \uc2 "The movement seen over the past several weeks can't be described as 'orderly,'" Mr. Aso said at a news conference, reflecting his position that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be warranted.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will accept changes in the value of the yen as long as they are gradual, he added.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have disagreed with Japan's assessment of the market, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have been orderly.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lew, speaking at a news conference, stressed the importance of commitments among \uc2 G-7\uc2  and \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  nations not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "The conversations we've had here were consistent with the value of having frank and direct discussions on difficult subjects," he said. "That's a good thing for us to continue to do."\par \par \uc2 A senior \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  official said it would take the kind of surging strength seen in the yen around the time of a major earthquake and tsunami in northeastern \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2011 for Washington to endorse \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "You have to distinguish between the kind of crisis that was present in those circumstances from the kinds of fluctuations in the market that just happened," the official said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. joined Japan and the other \uc2 G-7\uc2  nations in March 2011 to undertake joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the yen lower.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aso said the yen's exchange rate versus the U.S. dollar had become politicized in the U.S. because of the presidential campaign there and debate over the ratification of the Trans-Pacific Partnership trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 "It is important to continue dialogue and avoid getting emotional over the issue," Mr. Aso said.\par \par \uc2 The comments follow a series of verbal jabs between the two countries over the yen. Mr. Aso recently threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to blunt the yen's rise, while the U.S. has repeatedly warned against it.\par \par \uc2 The finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs found common ground in their concern about the state of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "What we know is that we have inadequate demand overall globally," the U.S. official said. "In order to grow global demand, it's going to require investment and spending."\par \par \uc2 That sentiment was echoed by Japan. "The only way to grow the economy when private consumption and investment are stalling is through government expenditures," Mr. Aso said.\par \par \uc2 Within \uc2 G-7\uc2 , many countries support the need for significant infrastructure investment as a way to create short-term demand and increase productivity in the longer term.\par \par \uc2 But no concrete agreement was reached on increased government spending, reflecting divergent views among countries such as Germany.\par \par \uc2 J\u252\'00\'FCrg Weissgerber, a spokesman for \uc2 Germany's finance ministry\uc2 , stressed the need for a balanced policy approach, noting that the importance of structural reforms was recently highlighted by G-20 meetings in Shanghai and Washington.\par \par \uc2 "In terms of fiscal policy, improving the structure or quality of public spending is much more important for growth than just raising the amount of spending," Mr. Weissgerber said in an email. "We have too much debt world-wide -- both public and private -- and thus need to bring down debt levels and deficits. This will help to improve the resilience of economies much more than any short-term fiscal stimulus financed by borrowing."\par \par \uc2 In a compromise, \uc2 G-7\uc2  has left individual countries to devise their own policy programs to contribute to stronger global growth.\par \par \uc2 In line with its recent practice, the group issued no joint written statement. Its discussion will feed into a final communiqu\u233\'00\'E9 of the \uc2 G-7\uc2  leaders when they hold a summit meeting scheduled to take place in Ise-Shima, Japan, next Thursday and Friday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com and Mitsuru Obe at mitsuru.obe@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 23-05-16 0535GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160523ec5n0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
