\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Gov Kuroda: \uc2 G-7\uc2  Agrees to Steer Monetary Policy In Line With  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Mandates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2016\par 08:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SENDAI, Japan--Bank of Japan Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda said Saturday that central banks from the world's seven wealthiest nations agreed that they should steer monetary policy in ways that are "consistent" with their policy mandates.\par \par \uc2 "Speaking of monetary policy, I believe we've confirmed that monetary policy should be consistent with central banks' mandates and will continue to support economic activities and price stability," Mr. Kuroda said at a joint news conference with Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda spoke following a meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  leading industrialized nations. Mr. Kuroda didn't elaborate further on what he called "central banks' mandates," but coming at a time of intense debate between the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , his remarks could raise speculation that \uc2 G-7\uc2  members put pressure on each other during the gathering to avoid using monetary policy to influence exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 21, 2016 02:31 ET (06:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160521ec5l0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
