\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2  researchers say Japan monetary easing has benefits for Asian economies\b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2016\par 07:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, May 21 (Reuters) - Japan's extraordinarily easy monetary policy, a source of friction with the United States for pushing the yen's value down, has had some positive effects on emerging Asian economies, including increased growth and equity prices, \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  researchers have found.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  findings were released in a paper on Friday, as \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors began meeting in Japan, where divergent views on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to be a hot topic.\par \par \uc2 The paper claims to be the first to model the spillover effects of Japan's qualitative and quantitative easing program (QQE) on Southeast Asian countries.\par \par \uc2 QQE was launched in 2013 and targets both government bond purchases as well as other assets, such as exchange-traded funds and real estate investment trusts.\par \par \uc2 The program has helped push down the yen from 82 to the dollar in 2012 to 119 to the dollar by August 2015. Japanese equity prices surged and inflation also strengthened during this period.\par \par \uc2 Despite Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising against the yen, they mirrored some of these effects, study authors Giovanni Ganelli, and Nour Tawk said.\par \par \uc2 "Our results show that implementation of QQE in Japan, when estimated by a positive shock to Japanese equity prices, consistently caused an increase in equity prices across emerging Asian countries, as well as an appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," they wrote.\par \par \uc2 "Most (Southeast Asian) countries experienced an increase in output and a temporary increase in inflation. Capital inflows surged in many countries."\par \par \uc2 They argue that while most existing research uses interest rate changes as proxies for quantitative easing, this transmission channel is broken in Japan because rates have been at the zero bound for an extended period.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they use stock market surges and increases in the monetary base as proxies for QQE.\par \par \uc2 CONFIDENCE\par \par \uc2 The researchers said that Japan's QQE caused a significant increase in equity prices in emerging Asia, with increases in the 2 to 5 percent range for China, Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 And while nearly all countries surveyed experienced an appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the yen as a result of QQE, it had a "generally positive" impact on growth in emerging Asian countries, with statistically significant increases in Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Thailand.\par \par \uc2 The authors suggested that a shift away from domestic goods to Japanese goods did not occur across the region.\par \par \uc2 They also said that another reason for the increased growth in these countries may have been increased confidence prompted by rising equity prices, which overcame the effects of their higher exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 This was particularly true of China, which has strong trade linkages with Japan, the researchers said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan rose against the yen, but China saw a mild positive effect on GDP, though not significant, the authors said. But China likely benefited from lower input costs from Japanese components used in Chinese manufactured goods, as well as higher equity prices.\par \par \uc2 "In other words, despite the depreciation of the yen and the appreciation of the RMB (yuan), the overall spillover impact of QQE on China's GDP was not negative, due to increased confidence as illustrated by the increase in equity prices, and a reduction in import costs" wrote Tawk and Ganelli, who is the deputy head of the IMF's Japan office.\par \par \uc2 The paper does not represent the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's staff policy view on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it may provide some ammunition for Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  as he seeks to persuade his G7 counterparts to back \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The paper can be read here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/wp/2016/wp1699.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/wp/2016/wp1699.pdf}}}\uc2  (Reporting by David Lawder; Editing by Robert Birsel) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-21T06:05:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160521ec5l006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
