\par\par \b \uc2 Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Could Hit U.S.- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Relations, Abe Adviser Warns\b0\par\par\uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2016\par 07:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO\u8212\'20\'14Japan could face a strong U.S. backlash if it tries to artificially weaken the yen, an adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said, citing objections from U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and White House officials to the possibility of direct-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The remark by Yale University Professor Emeritus Koichi Hamada follows open clashes between \uc2 Japan's finance ministry\uc2  and the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  over yen policy, underscoring tension between the world's largest and third-largest economies.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has repeatedly threatened over recent weeks to step into the markets to stop the yen's rise, which is weighing heavily on Japanese exporters, but Washington has made it clear it won't tolerate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Under present circumstances, if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the yen's advance, it could have "considerable aftereffects" on its relations with Washington, Mr. Hamada said in a recent interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "To \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without giving diplomatic relations due consideration would be unwise," Mr. Hamada said. Avoiding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a "safe bet" for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if it couldn't arrange a deal that the U.S. would keep quiet, he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hamada is an architect of Mr. Abe's pro-growth strategies launched in 2013 and knows many U.S. officials. Japan has stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since late 2011, when the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  issued a harsh reprimand against solo \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted in that year and 2010 by Tokyo. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen policy is dictated by the finance ministry,\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hamada senses a changing attitude in Washington toward Abenomics. At the start of Mr. Abe's efforts to spur growth in Japan, U.S. authorities turned a blind eye to a weak yen fueled by Abenomics, probably because they "wanted to provide help for a Japan that was trying to get back on its feet," he said. "But they may not have that feeling anymore."\par \par \uc2 Behind the U.S.'s change of attitude may lie "a sense of uncertainty in both the U.S. and Japan over the prospects of their economies," Mr. Hamada said. "I recently met with a few officials from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and they gave me the impression the Fed has considered the yen's weakness\u8230\'20\'26as a burden on the U.S. economy. They are opposed (to yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) as they want to exit" from their loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 In addition, officials from the Fed and the \uc2 U.S. Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  appear concerned that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would offer Congress members who are against a trans-Pacific trade deal, known as the TPP, an opportunity to void it, Mr. Hamada said. "However volatile (exchange rates) may become, if you conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you'll be accused of waging a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," he said, an accusation that could strengthen opposition to the deal.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities are "probably torn in a difficult situation right now" between the need to discourage speculators from buying the yen and the need to keep on good terms with the U.S., he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hamada's account suggests that the chance of Tokyo winning over Washington is slim. The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  has both privately and publicly warned \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to launch \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and recently put the country on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy monitoring list. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew has rejected Japan's view that the yen's appreciation this year has been one-sided and speculator-driven.\par \par \uc2 All this means that successfully sapping the yen's strength through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a challenge for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Open complaints from the U.S. tend to embolden yen-buying speculators by strengthening the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a sustainable manner.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Hamada added that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is by no means a permanent taboo.\par \par \uc2 "If the yen were to appreciate in an extreme manner, and threatened to make waves in the Japanese economy, the authorities, under normal circumstances, should administer slightly stronger medicine through monetary policy," he said. "If that was unlikely to yield quick results, authorities shouldn't rule out (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) as taboo, however fearful they may be about a U.S. backlash."\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 18, 2016 01:25 ET (05:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160518ec5i000a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
