\par\par \b \uc2 US To Push Germany at G-7 for Greek Debt Relief -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2016\par 19:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 13:03 ET - US is still trying to broker Greek bailout deal that includes debt relief despite growing signs Europe will have to move ahead without a restructuring and the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . "All of the parties should come together and find a way forward that results in Greece receiving the financial support and debt relief it needs to make its reform program a successful one," a senior \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  official says. Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  will press German Finance Minister \uc2 Wolfgang Schauble\uc2  at the upcoming \uc2 G-7\uc2  meeting this week to relent and give Greece debt relief.\par \par \uc2  But given the \uc2 IMF\uc2  doesn't appear ready to make 1+1=3 for a bailout program that doesn't add up without debt relief, some analysts see the \uc2 IMF\uc2  may be out of a deal, and along with it, any chance of restructuring.\par \par \uc2 "The 24 May Eurogroup will conclude Greece's first review and disburse funds; It's becoming more likely this will happen without the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , delaying if not killing debt relief for Greece," says Mujtaba Rahman, head of \uc2 Eurasia Group\uc2 's Europe department. (ian.talley@wsj.com; @iantalley)\par \par \uc2 13:00 ET - Although US officials won't say it outright in public, Washington is clearly signaling to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  any yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now would violate the Tokyo's exchange rate commitments to the \uc2 G-7\uc2 . "It's notable that exchange markets in recent months have been orderly," a senior \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  official says ahead of the \uc2 G-7\uc2  meeting later this week. That's a technical reference to \uc2 G-7\uc2  and G-20 statements that give countries room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event of "disorderly" exchange rate movements. Since the US sees the yen's appreciation as "orderly," it's telling Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would contravene their commitment and could spark a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, including a depreciation by China. "Those are very important commitments for all members of the G-20 to continue to adhere to, and it's particularly important countries in the \uc2 G-7\uc2  as leaders of the global economy," the US official says. (ian.talley@wsj.com; @iantalley)\par \par \uc2 12:49 ET - The bankruptcy of Hillview, Ky.--the first city to file for Chapter 9 protection after Detroit's historic case--has ended with a judge granting the town's request to drop the case after city leaders reached a repayment deal on its largest debt. The Louisville suburb plans to raise taxes and borrow $5M to pay off a decade-old property dispute with a truck-driver training school. The legal award won by the school, which grew by $3,759.54/day in interest, prompted Hillview leaders to put the 9K-resident municipality into bankruptcy proceedings in August. (katy.stech@wsj.com; @katystech)\par \par \uc2 12:46 ET - Texas' Republican Attorney General, Ken Paxton, announced his state was joining Alabama in asking a state judge to end what Paxton calls a "ridiculous" investigation launched by the US Virgin Islands attorney general probing \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  climate-change documents over the past few decades. The move raises the stakes in an increasingly political legal fight that has implications not only for \uc2 Exxon\uc2 , the world's largest privately owned oil company, but also for the entire US oil and natural gas industry and also environmentalists seeking to cast the sector under the same unforgiving public limelight as tobacco companies faced in the 1990s. (amy.harder@wsj.com; @AmyAHarder)\par \par \uc2 12:45 ET - \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  has 3 reasons to be wary of the latest surge in oil. First, production outages from Nigeria to Canada have been supporting prices of late--but the output cuts are temporary. This as current prices have oil back at levels which could induce more US production. Capital Economic adds cheaper oil removes another excuse for the Fed not to raise interest rates again soon--and higher rates mean a stronger dollar, which is bearish for oil. He sees Brent at $45 a barrel by year end. It is trading at $49.10 on Monday. (georgi.kantchev@wsj.com; @georgikantchev)\par \par \uc2 12:20 ET - Raymond James slashes its 2016 production outlook by 829K barrels/day due to a combination of unexpected outages in some regions and faster-than-expected production declines in others. Oil analysts are closely watching Nigeria, Canada, Libya and Venezuela due to reports of violence, political unrest or natural disasters. But Raymond James also calls for output to fall more than expected in China, Colombia, Mexico and Angola due to aging wells and spending cutbacks by oil producers. "Oil supply from outside the US is falling meaningfully faster than we thought possible 6 months ago." (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 12:16 ET - Prompted by \uc2 LendingClub\uc2 's (LC) jarring management change last week, Fitch warns online lenders "will have an uphill battle in winning back investor confidence." The firm has so far only rated 2 securitization deals backed by Prosper Marketplace loans, and Fitch does "not expect recent events to have an impact on the ratings of these deals." But it thinks investor scrutiny could harm online lenders' ability to keep selling new loans via securitizations while regulatory scrutiny could increase costs, harming their core advantage over banks. Fitch's statement, while it carried no credit-rating action, could spook investors still considering backing securitization deals from peer-to-peer online lenders. (telis.demos@wsj.com; @telisdemos)\par \par \uc2 12:13 ET - The problems for North American land drillers of oil and gas aren't going away amid diminished rig counts and prolonged low spot prices, says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  as it highlights the risk of covenant violations. "In the medium-term there is a heightened expectation \uc2 Parker Drilling\uc2  (B3 negative) and \uc2 Pioneer Energy Services\uc2  (Caa3 negative) will breach at least one existing financial covenant by mid-2017," it says. The ratings firm notes that due to spending cuts by oil production companies the worldwide rig count is down by nearly 60%, only to be outdone by an almost 80% slide in the North American rig count. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:06 ET - Glass Lewis joined fellow proxy adviser Institutional Shareholder Services in supporting Harvest Capital Strategies' nominees to Green Dot's (GDOT) board, saying it shares "many of Harvest's concerns with respect to potential entrenchment, a lack of pro-shareholder mindset, and limitations on shareholders to ultimately exercise their rights as owners of the company." Harvest since January has been publicly seeking leadership and board changes at the Pasadena, Calif.-based issuer of prepaid \uc2 MasterCard\uc2  (MA) and \uc2 Visa\uc2  (V) cards, citing "continued mismanagement and value destruction" at the company where the hedge fund now holds a 9.3% stake. Shares of GDOT up 2.5% to $23.40 in recent trading. (anne.steele@wsj.com; @AnneMarieSteele)\par \par \uc2 11:57 ET - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  thinks \uc2 Haemonetics\uc2  (HAE) could be on the slow road to recovery as it upgrades the blood processor to neutral after more than two years on its sell list. It's been an especially rocky few weeks for the company after a 4Q miss that sent shares plummeting 14%, followed by a disappointing 2017 forecast and announcement of a new CEO pushing shares to five-year lows. Goldman says management has habitually misjudged end markets resulting in inflated Street expectations, but now it's the first time since 2013 that it has not viewed HAE's guidance as "overly optimistic." Goldman ups price target $4 to $29. HAE up 6% at $27.72, off 33% in the past year. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:57 ET - Hewlett-Packard Enterprises (HPE) CEO \uc2 Meg Whitman\uc2  will be deposed in a lawsuit challenging pre-split Hewlett's $5B takeover last year of \uc2 Aruba Networks\uc2 , and her personal dislike of banker Frank Quattrone is fair game, a Delaware judge rules. During deal talks, she refused to negotiate with his \uc2 Qatalyst Partners\uc2 , Aruba's financial adviser, because of "bad blood" dating to her days as EBAY CEO and pre-split Hewlett's ill-fated Autonomy transaction, which Quattrone shepherded, according to court filings. So Aruba hired Evercore, which some former shareholders now say tainted the sales process. Company lawyers resist calls for a full-day deposition of Whitman, saying she wasn't intimately involved in the deal. But Vice Chancellor J. Travis Laster called that argument "demonstrably wrong" and rejected the company's request. (liz.hoffman@wsj.com; @lizrhoffman)\par \par \uc2 11:41 ET - Peru's economy expanded in line with expectations for 1Q as mining production continued to drive growth. The government said GDP expanded 4.4% in 1Q, with the mining and hydrocarbons sector up 16%. Economists, including those at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had forecast growth of about 4.5% in 1Q. Other sectors that grew in the quarter were retail at 2.8%, fishing at 1.8%, construction at 2.2% and retail at 2.8%. Manufacturing declined 3%. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 16, 2016 13:03 ET (17:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160516ec5g002px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
