\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Shares Start Higher but Await China Open -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2016\par 02:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares in Asia were mostly higher early Monday, ahead of the open in China, where investors are expected to react to fresh data out of China suggesting that recent stimulus may be leveling off. The Nikkei Stock Average was up 0.9%, Australia's S&P \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 was up 0.1%, but South Korea's Kospi was down 0.2%. Shares in Japan were up as the yen weakened slightly. The U.S. dollar had climbed to its highest level in more than a month Friday in New York. "Chinese data over the weekend managed to miss market expectations for every single release: credit growth, industrial production, retail sales and fixed-asset investment," wrote Angus Nicholson, market analyst at brokerage IG. Chinese banks sharply reduced their lending in April to 556 billion yuan ($85 billion) from 1.37 trillion yuan the previous month. IG says that Asian markets are set for a difficult start to the week. The Shanghai Composite Index has already been seeing a bout of weakness.\par \par \uc2  It lost nearly 3% last week, also the fourth straight week it was down. (chao.deng@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0032 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Singapore dollar may still be skewed toward weakness against the greenback, the daily chart suggests, but the pace of its depreciation is likely to remain limited for now. USD/SGD is still pointed upwards along the Bollinger uptrend channel that was activated early May, but it's bogged within the Ichimoku Cloud consolidation zone. A clean break of the ceiling of the Cloud at 1.3872 is needed to clear the path for USD/SGD to rally with more vigor. The greenback is broadly firm in Asia after better-than-expected U.S. retail sales data released Friday. A fresh catalyst for U.S. dollar strength may come from Tuesday's U.S. consumer price inflation data. The inflation outlook is one of the key factors that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  considers in deciding when to raise interest rates again. USD/SGD is now 1.3736 from its Friday close of 1.3719. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0028 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's road to economic recovery is at risk of being derailed by "another flare-up of its long-standing political crisis this year," according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2 . As the junta-appointed government's new draft constitution has been widely criticized, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a note that the country's economy could be hurt if protests and violent conflicts erupt. "The bottom line is that, even if unrest is avoided later this year, there is no clear end in sight to Thailand's long-running political crisis," it says. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0018 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The South Korea won is likely to weaken further against the U.S. dollar this week, the daily chart suggests. USD/KRW has just taken on a more bullish technical stance, now that it has crossed the 200-day moving average resistance line at 1,175. The pair is now aiming for the 1,194 ceiling of the daily Ichimoku Cloud consolidation zone, which if breached might unleash another wave of technical-driven buying. The dollar is advancing after U.S. retail sales data released Friday soothed fears that the U.S. economy isn't recovering as expected. Retail sales rose 1.3% in April, above expectations and the biggest jump since more than a year ago. Looking ahead, the next catalyst for USD/KRW may be the release of the U.S. consumer price inflation data on Tuesday, which could again skew the market's view on when U.S. rates might be raised. USD/KRW is now 1,178.1 from its Friday close of 1,171.4. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0019 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to restrict to Auckland tighter loan-to-value ratios on home loans, say ASB economists. ASB says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a "rather sanguine view" on the rise in house prices outside of Auckland. "The RBNZ is confident that a supply response to increased regional demand would not be as hamstrung as we have seen in Auckland," ASB says. In the short term, a delay in introducing further housing measures may temper the RBNZ's willingness to cut the official cash rate much further, but ASB still expects rate cuts in June and August. The RBNZ will release its survey of inflation expectations Tuesday, which ASB says will be an important decider for the June Monetary Policy Statement. "We expect that inflation expectations will remain steady, at uncomfortably low levels," it adds. (kate.geenty@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0011 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are marching higher again, continuing from momentum seen at the end of last week. The dollar is advancing after U.S. retail sales data released Friday put to rest fears that the U.S. economy isn't recovering as expected. Retail sales rose 1.3% in April, above expectations and the biggest jump since more than a year ago. The upbeat number soothed fears of a retail sector slowdown, which had been stoked by large U.S. retailer stocks plunging last week. The dollar is likely to keep heading higher now that technical signals on the USD/Asia charts have turned more bullish. Even if a U.S. interest rate-rise isn't expected in the immediate future, it still remains the biggest lead that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders have to go on for now. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0005 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] While \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has highlighted recently that job market trends have become more uncertain, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  says any evidence of lost momentum in hiring will be a passing phase, and the unemployment rate, now at a two-and-a-half year low of 5.7%, will not drift back above 6.0%. Strength in the monthly reading on hiring intentions in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NAB\f28 \uc2 \f2  survey, rising capacity utilisation and SEEK on-line job vacancies, "suggest this is likely only a temporary moderation," and that unemployment is unlikely to lift above 6% again, it adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2350 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Australian dollar continues to edge lower because of the prospect of additional RBA easing and a stronger U.S. dollar, says Elias Haddad, strategist at CBA. CBA is looking for an additional 50 basis points of rate cuts by the RBA in 2016. A cut in August and then November. Softer Chinese industrial and retail sales data released over the weekend is also weighing on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Haddad said the reports have reinforced CBA's view that China's 2Q GDP is expected to moderate to 6.6% from 6.7% in 1Q. The pair now trades at US$0.7259. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2349 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY will likely break below 105 toward the second half of this year, with the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy keeping a lid on the pair's upside momentum, says SMBC Nikko \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Makoto Noji in a morning note. The pair (now at 108.61) was weighed down after U.S. Treasury Secretary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacob Lew\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Friday warned of the risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  battles hurting the global economy. The USD/JPY will "likely remain volatile," amid a rift between the U.S. and Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, Noji says. While Tokyo said it is "prepared to undertake \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem" the JPY strength, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  in its semiannual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report called recent JPY movements "orderly" and also urged Japan to adhere to G-20 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  commitments regarding exchange rate policies. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2312 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY will likely struggle to go upside during Asia trade, placing the pair (now at 108.50) to move in a 108.00-109.00 range, says Akira Moroga, manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  products group at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aozora Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "We don't see yen selling given a perception gap," between the U.S. and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Moroga. While Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities have ramped up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rhetoric in recent weeks, U.S. is increasingly worried \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could spark a global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war. "We need to stay cautious about a downside risk," of the dollar against the yen, he says. The EUR/USD is tipped in a 1.1270-1.1330 range and is now at 1.1314. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 15, 2016 20:36 ET (00:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160516ec5g0000d\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\